window.shHubContent.receive("questions",{"questions":[{"id":"in-def","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which definition of intellectual disability matches the one in Dr. Mitchell's lecture?","choices":["Deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning that begin during the developmental period","An IQ score under 70 at any age, with or without trouble adapting","Any loss of cognitive function, whatever the age it begins","Deficits in adaptive functioning alone, with intellect that tests in the normal range"],"answer":0,"ex":"Intellectual disability is a series of deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning that begin during the developmental period. The DSM-5 splits it into those three parts, and all three must be present.","src":"rec","why":["All three pieces are in it: intellect, adaptation, and onset in childhood.","That was the older IQ-only approach; today's diagnosis goes well beyond a test score.","Loss after childhood, as from a brain injury in adulthood, is special needs but not ID.","Adaptive deficits alone describe some autistic patients; ID requires an intellectual deficit too."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"The definition of intellectual disability: intellect, adaptive behavior, onset in childhood","basis":"hint","ids":["in-def","in-tbi"]}},{"id":"in-tbi","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"A man developed normally, then lost short-term memory and the ability to adapt to new settings after a gunshot wound to the head at 18. How is he classified?","choices":["Special needs, but not intellectual disability, because it did not begin in childhood","Intellectual disability, because he now has both intellectual and adaptive deficits that limit daily life","Intellectual disability, because the injury happened before age 21","Neither: adaptive problems after injury are not a special need"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Mitchell's own patient, shot in his senior year of high school, lost intellectual and adaptive function but is not technically ID: the deficits did not begin during the developmental period.","src":"rec","why":["Onset in the developmental period is one of the three required parts; his injury came after it.","He has both deficits, but the third part, onset during development, is missing.","The line is the developmental period (childhood), not the age when Medicaid dental coverage ends.","He is clearly special needs; he just doesn't meet the ID definition."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"The definition of intellectual disability: intellect, adaptive behavior, onset in childhood","basis":"hint","ids":["in-def","in-tbi"]}},{"id":"in-iq","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Under the older approach, which IQ score placed a person in the category of intellectual disability?","choices":["Below 70","Below 100","Below 85","Below 50"],"answer":0,"ex":"Older testing focused heavily on IQ tests: a score lower than 70 qualified. Today's assessment is a comprehensive clinical evaluation that adds adaptive functioning and cultural and linguistic factors.","src":"rec","why":["Older testing used a score lower than 70.","100 is the average IQ, not the cutoff.","85 is one standard deviation below average, not the cutoff he gave.","Under 50 falls in the moderate-to-severe range, not the overall cutoff."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"How ID is assessed today vs the old IQ cutoffs","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-modern","in-iq","in-lang"]}},{"id":"in-modern","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"How does today's assessment for intellectual disability differ from older testing?","choices":["It adds a long clinical evaluation, adaptive functioning, and cultural and linguistic factors","It relies only on a shorter, computer-based IQ test that a single specialist gives in one sitting","It drops intellectual testing and measures adaptive functioning alone","It is done only after age 21"],"answer":0,"ex":"Modern assessment goes beyond the IQ test to clinical assessment over long periods by several specialists (often half a day or more at the Civitan-Sparks Center), adds adaptive functioning, and weighs cultural and linguistic factors.","src":"rec","why":["It is comprehensive: specialists, adaptive behavior, language and culture, not just a score.","Testing moved away from a single test; Civitan-Sparks evaluations take half a day or more.","Intellect is still part of the diagnosis; adaptive functioning is added, not substituted.","Onset is in the developmental period, and children are evaluated."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"How ID is assessed today vs the old IQ cutoffs","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-modern","in-iq","in-lang"]}},{"id":"in-lang","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does modern ID testing weigh linguistic factors?","choices":["A patient who can't speak may understand far more once given a communication tool","Because patients who speak two languages always score lower on IQ tests","Because speech problems by themselves are what defines intellectual disability in the DSM-5","Because only verbal patients can be tested for ID"],"answer":0,"ex":"Historically, the inability to express oneself verbally was read as an IQ problem. With new communication tools, some patients turn out to communicate well: they had a linguistic problem, not only an intellectual one.","src":"rec","why":["Lack of speech was once mistaken for low intellect.","He didn't say that; the point is not mistaking a language problem for an intellectual one.","Speech problems are common in ID but are not its definition.","Nonverbal patients are evaluated too; that is the point of weighing linguistics."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"How ID is assessed today vs the old IQ cutoffs","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-modern","in-iq","in-lang"]}},{"id":"in-imbecile","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"In early-1900s medical texts, an \"imbecile\" was a person with a mental age of:","choices":["2 to 7 years","7 to 12 years","Under 2 years","12 to 16 years"],"answer":0,"ex":"The broad category was feeble-minded, subdivided by mental age into moron (7-12), imbecile (2-7) and idiot (under 2). They were medical terms that the culture turned into insults.","src":"rec","why":["Imbecile was the middle category.","That was the \"moron\" category.","That was the \"idiot\" category.","That range was above all three categories."]},{"id":"in-rosa","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"What did Rosa's Law (2010) do?","choices":["Removed the term \"mental retardation\" from U.S. federal documents","Required public schools to provide special education","Added disability to the ADA Code of Ethics","Required dental schools to teach management of special needs patients"],"answer":0,"ex":"By 2010, Rosa's Law eliminated \"mental retardation\" from federal documents; the DSM-5 made the same change in 2013, using intellectual disability.","src":"rec","why":["It replaced it with intellectual disability in federal documents.","That came from national laws by 1978.","That was the ADA's own revision in 2018.","That was CODA's 2019 change to Standard 2."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Person-first language: ID is a diagnosis, not a description","basis":"hint","ids":["in-person","in-cycle","in-rosa"]}},{"id":"in-dsm","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"In what year did the DSM-5 replace \"mental retardation\" with \"intellectual disability\"?","choices":["2013","2010","2018","2019"],"answer":0,"ex":"Rosa's Law (2010) covered federal documents; the DSM-5 (2013) made the same change in medicine.","src":"rec","why":["The DSM came out with the change in 2013.","2010 was Rosa's Law, for federal documents.","2018 was the ADA Code of Ethics revision on disability.","2019 was CODA's change to Standard 2."]},{"id":"in-cycle","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"According to the lecture, why have the terms for intellectual disability kept changing?","choices":["Each medical term is taken up by the culture as an insult, then retired for a new one","New genetic discoveries keep redefining the condition, so each era needs its own name","Insurance companies require new billing terms","Each new IQ test brought its own vocabulary"],"answer":0,"ex":"Medical terms (feeble-minded, imbecile, mental retardation) were neutral when introduced; culture used them derogatorily, they took on stigma, and they had to be replaced.","src":"rec","why":["The cycle: medical term, derogatory use, stigma, replacement.","Genetics advanced, but that isn't why the words changed.","He didn't mention billing.","The terms changed because of stigma, not test versions."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Person-first language: ID is a diagnosis, not a description","basis":"hint","ids":["in-person","in-cycle","in-rosa"]}},{"id":"in-person","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which phrasing does Dr. Mitchell recommend?","choices":["\"A patient who has Down syndrome\"","\"A Down syndrome patient\"","\"A Down's\"","\"A retarded patient\""],"answer":0,"ex":"Intellectual disability is a diagnostic condition and not a description. Put the person ahead of the disorder; patients treated with respect tend to return it.","src":"rec","why":["Person first: the disability is a diagnosis, not a description of who they are.","This puts the disorder before the person, the phrasing he asked you to avoid.","Defining someone by the condition alone is what families object to.","A retired term that became an insult."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Person-first language: ID is a diagnosis, not a description","basis":"hint","ids":["in-person","in-cycle","in-rosa"]}},{"id":"in-willow","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Whose investigative report on Willowbrook helped change how the nation treated people with intellectual disability?","choices":["Geraldo Rivera","Rosa Marcellino","Dr. Langdon Down","Ami Klin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Geraldo Rivera's undercover report from Willowbrook (New York) documented deplorable conditions and was pivotal in the shift away from institutions.","src":"rec","why":["He snuck cameras into Willowbrook in the 1970s.","Rosa's Law is named for her, not for Willowbrook.","He described Down syndrome in 1865.","He studies eye tracking in autism."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Deinstitutionalization: where people with ID live now","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-deinst","in-most","in-inst","in-group","in-willow"]}},{"id":"in-deinst","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"What does deinstitutionalization mean?","choices":["Providing care for people with ID in the community where they live","Closing special education classrooms and mainstreaming every child","Moving everyone with ID into small state hospitals","Ending Medicaid coverage at 21"],"answer":0,"ex":"By the 1970s the large institutions had become places of mistreatment; deinstitutionalization moved care into the community, and it remains the predominant model.","src":"rec","why":["The 1970s push that is still the main model today.","Public schools were required to provide special education.","That is still institutional care.","That is a coverage rule, not deinstitutionalization."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Deinstitutionalization: where people with ID live now","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-deinst","in-most","in-inst","in-group","in-willow"]}},{"id":"in-1978","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"By 1978, national laws required what?","choices":["Public schools to provide special education programs","Dentists to treat any patient with a disability who asks for care","Dental schools to teach special patient care","Medicaid to cover adult dental care"],"answer":0,"ex":"By 1978 laws required public schools to provide special education, which is why many children with ID get much of their therapy at school (and lost it during COVID closures).","src":"rec","why":["Children with ID were brought into the school system.","No law requires every dentist to treat every patient.","CODA's standard came much later.","Alabama Medicaid's dental benefit still ends at 21."]},{"id":"in-most","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Where do most people with intellectual disability live?","choices":["At home with family","In group homes","In institutions","In nursing homes"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Mitchell stressed that most special needs clients live at home with their families; a segment, especially as they get older, lives in group homes.","src":"rec","why":["Most live at home; many attend a day program.","Many adults do, but not most people with ID.","Only about 1-2% live in institutions.","He did not describe nursing homes as the usual setting."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Deinstitutionalization: where people with ID live now","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-deinst","in-most","in-inst","in-group","in-willow"]}},{"id":"in-inst","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of people with intellectual disability live in institutions today?","choices":["1-2%","10-15%","About half","About 30%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Only about 1-2% live in institutions, usually the most profoundly affected or those at risk of harming themselves or others (Glenwood in Birmingham).","src":"rec","why":["A small group, mostly the most profoundly affected.","Far too high; institutions are now rare.","Most live at home.","Far too high for today."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Deinstitutionalization: where people with ID live now","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-deinst","in-most","in-inst","in-group","in-willow"]}},{"id":"in-group","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which describes a group home?","choices":["About 6-8 clients at similar levels living in a house with caregivers","A large state hospital with round-the-clock therapy","A daytime program that people attend during school hours, then go home each evening","A private apartment with no staff"],"answer":0,"ex":"Group homes have a small number of clients (about 6-8) grouped around similar levels of disability, living with caregivers who help with daily care and getting to doctors. The system is underfunded and staff burn out.","src":"rec","why":["Small, community-based, with caregivers who also bring them to appointments.","That was the institution model.","That is a day program.","Group homes have caregivers."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Deinstitutionalization: where people with ID live now","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-deinst","in-most","in-inst","in-group","in-willow"]}},{"id":"in-day","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"United Ability, the Exceptional Foundation and the Arc of Central Alabama are examples of:","choices":["Day programs","Institutions","Group home operators","Medicaid dental plans"],"answer":0,"ex":"Day programs bring adults during roughly school hours for socialization and therapy. United Ability used to be called United Cerebral Palsy.","src":"rec","why":["Places adults spend the day for socialization and therapy, then go home.","Glenwood was the institutional example.","Arc PHP and the Learning Tree were the group home examples.","They are community programs, not insurers."]},{"id":"in-al","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"By the broad federal definition, about how many adults in Alabama have some form of disability?","choices":["1 in 3","1 in 5","1 in 6","1 in 10"],"answer":0,"ex":"Nationally 1 in 5 adults (over 53 million) has some disability; Alabama leads the nation at 1 in 3. Mobility disabilities, largely in the elderly, are the most common.","src":"rec","why":["Alabama leads the nation.","That is the national figure.","That is about Minnesota's rate, the low end.","Too low for any state in the lecture."]},{"id":"in-sev","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which state has the highest percentage of people with severe disability in the lecture's data?","choices":["West Virginia","Alabama","Arkansas","Mississippi"],"answer":0,"ex":"Severe disability: 12.6% nationally; Alabama 16.7% (about 800,000 people); Arkansas 17.1%, Kentucky 17%; West Virginia leads the country at 19.4%.","src":"rec","why":["19.4% of its population.","Alabama is 16.7%, third in the region, not first.","17.1%, high, but below West Virginia.","16.2%, just below Alabama."]},{"id":"in-sevsex","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"How do men and women compare for severe disability?","choices":["About the same (12.5% vs 12.7%)","Women are about 4% higher","Men are about twice as likely","Women are about twice as likely"],"answer":0,"ex":"For general disability, females are slightly higher; for severe disability, males and females are statistically the same. Whites and African Americans are also the same for severe disability; Native Americans are highest and Hispanics low.","src":"rec","why":["The difference is within the margin of error.","That gap is for general disability, not severe.","He found them nearly identical.","He found them nearly identical."]},{"id":"in-rise","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which disabilities have declined, even as developmental disabilities overall have risen?","choices":["Cerebral palsy, hearing loss and seizure disorders","Autism and ADHD","Down syndrome and fragile X, from earlier prenatal testing","All developmental disabilities"],"answer":0,"ex":"Developmental disabilities in children rose 9.5% (2009-2017) and 38% since 1997, partly from less stigma and better diagnosis; autism and ADHD rose most. CP, hearing loss and seizure disorders declined.","src":"rec","why":["These more definable disabilities have measurably decreased.","These have risen sharply, largely from diagnosis.","He did not list these as declining.","Overall they rose 38% since 1997."]},{"id":"in-sg","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"The Surgeon General's 2005 report connected the lack of dental care for people with disabilities directly to:","choices":["Training: schools were not teaching enough special needs care","Medicaid's dental benefit ending at the 21st birthday for adults with ID","A shortage of dental chairs that recline","Patients' refusal to seek care"],"answer":0,"ex":"2000: people with disabilities have an access-to-care problem. 2002: a large disparity and lack of providers in oral health. 2005: the problem tied directly to training in dental and medical programs.","src":"rec","why":["Graduates felt unequipped and turned families away.","That's an access problem he discussed, but not the 2005 report's link.","He didn't attribute it to equipment.","The barrier was on the provider side."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Access to care: the Surgeon General's 2005 report, cost, Medicaid at 21","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-sg","in-cost","in-medicaid","in-or"]}},{"id":"in-cost","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"In the studies Dr. Mitchell cited, about what share of people with disabilities could not get dental care because of cost?","choices":["About 29%","About 5%","About 50%","About 75%"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 29% of people with disabilities could not access dental care because it cost too much, and many with Medicaid couldn't find a dentist who accepted it.","src":"rec","why":["Almost 30%.","Much too low.","Higher than the figure he gave.","Much higher than the figure he gave."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Access to care: the Surgeon General's 2005 report, cost, Medicaid at 21","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-sg","in-cost","in-medicaid","in-or"]}},{"id":"in-medicaid","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"In Alabama, what happens to Medicaid dental coverage for a person with severe ID at age 21?","choices":["The dental benefit ends, though medical coverage often continues","It continues for life","It switches to cover only care done in the operating room, not the office","It doubles to cover adult prosthetics"],"answer":0,"ex":"Alabama Medicaid covers most people with ID well until the 21st birthday, including OR care at Children's. After 21 the medical coverage often continues, but the dental benefit ends.","src":"rec","why":["After 21, most have no dental insurance and pay out of pocket.","Medical coverage may; the dental benefit does not.","There is no dental benefit after 21.","The dental benefit ends."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Access to care: the Surgeon General's 2005 report, cost, Medicaid at 21","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-sg","in-cost","in-medicaid","in-or"]}},{"id":"in-or","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"For a child with ID in Alabama who needs complex dental work in the operating room, which insurance does Dr. Mitchell say works best?","choices":["Medicaid (or Blue Cross Blue Shield / ALL Kids)","Most private dental plans, such as Delta Dental or MetLife","No insurance; families pay the hospital directly","Medicare"],"answer":0,"ex":"Medicaid is one of the best insurances a child with ID can have, because it covers OR care; BCBS and ALL Kids also allow it. With most dental plans there's almost no mechanism to reach the OR.","src":"rec","why":["These give access to the OR at Children's.","With these, getting into the OR is almost impossible.","He described insurance as the key to OR access.","He discussed Medicaid, not Medicare."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"Access to care: the Surgeon General's 2005 report, cost, Medicaid at 21","basis":"lecture","ids":["in-sg","in-cost","in-medicaid","in-or"]}},{"id":"in-etio","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"In about what share of cases of intellectual disability can no cause be found?","choices":["30-40%","Under 5%","About 2%","About 90%"],"answer":0,"ex":"30-40% of cases have no discernible etiology. The more profound the disability, the more likely a cause can be identified.","src":"rec","why":["The figure in print; he suspects it's higher.","Far more than that remain unexplained.","2% is the share attributed to fetal alcohol syndrome.","Most cases are not unexplained."]},{"id":"in-fas","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of intellectual disability has been attributed to fetal alcohol syndrome?","choices":["2%","20%","50%","Less than 0.1%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prenatal physical causes include maternal malnutrition, infections and fetal alcohol syndrome, which accounts for about 2% of the ID population.","src":"rec","why":["A prenatal physical cause.","Too high.","Too high; up to 55% may be genetic.","Too low."]},{"id":"in-sexchrom","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Why do single-gene errors on the sex chromosomes cause disability more often than errors on other chromosomes?","choices":["There is often no second healthy copy to mask the faulty one","Sex chromosomes carry more genes than all of the other chromosomes combined","They are the only chromosomes copied in meiosis","Sex chromosome errors always come from the father"],"answer":0,"ex":"An error on one copy of a non-sex chromosome may be masked by the healthy copy; on the sex chromosomes that duplication often isn't there, so the problem shows (fragile X is the example).","src":"rec","why":["On other chromosomes, the partner chromosome's working copy can hide the error.","That isn't the reason he gave.","All chromosomes are copied.","He didn't say that."]},{"id":"in-ace","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"How do adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) change a child's brain?","choices":["Excess cortisol and adrenaline over- and under-stimulate areas, raising fear responses","They add an extra chromosome","They speed growth of the hippocampus, which improves memory and calms the stress response","They lower all hormone levels, calming the child"],"answer":0,"ex":"Toxic stress releases too much cortisol and adrenaline; cells die in the hippocampus, circuits change, and the stress state stays raised, so the child more easily interprets things as dangerous. The effect is dose-related.","src":"rec","why":["Toxic stress leaves the brain on edge; danger is read everywhere.","ACEs are environmental, not chromosomal.","Cells die in the hippocampus; memory suffers.","Stress hormones are overproduced."]},{"id":"in-ace15","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"People with many adverse childhood experiences are about how many times more likely to attempt suicide?","choices":["15 times","2 times","50 times","They are no more likely"],"answer":0,"ex":"Children with multiple ACEs can become about 15 times more likely to attempt suicide, and more likely to smoke, drink, miss work and have job and money problems.","src":"rec","why":["One of the most troubling numbers on the slide.","Too low for the figure he gave.","Too high.","The risk rises with the number of ACEs."]},{"id":"in-coda","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"What did CODA change in Standard 2 in 2019?","choices":["Graduates must be competent in assessing and managing special needs patients","Graduates must be competent in assessing special needs patients","Every dentist must treat every patient with a disability who asks for care, whatever its complexity","Schools must own a papoose board"],"answer":0,"ex":"Before 2019, graduates had to be competent in assessing the treatment needs of patients with special needs; in 2019 CODA added \"and managing.\" Management can include getting the patient to someone more specialized.","src":"rec","why":["\"And managing\" was added.","That was the standard before 2019.","Neither CODA nor the ADA requires that.","No such standard."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"The 2018 ADA Code of Ethics: refer, don't just decline; CODA 2019","basis":"hint","ids":["in-ada","in-coda"]}},{"id":"in-ada","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Under the ADA Code of Ethics revision of 2018, what must a dentist do if a disabled patient's care is beyond their skill?","choices":["Help connect the family with a dentist who can provide the care","Treat the patient anyway, since refusing is now unethical","Simply decline and let the family keep searching","Report the family to Medicaid"],"answer":0,"ex":"In 2018 the ADA added disability to the reasons it is unethical to refuse a patient. It doesn't require every dentist to treat everyone, but you must get the family connected to a place that can help.","src":"rec","why":["Refer: don't leave the family hanging.","Never attempt care you can't do safely.","That is exactly what the revision aims to stop.","Not part of the code."],"likely":{"lec":"intro","t":"The 2018 ADA Code of Ethics: refer, don't just decline; CODA 2019","basis":"hint","ids":["in-ada","in-coda"]}},{"id":"in-learn","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which approach helps people with ID learn in the dental office?","choices":["Short, concrete phrases repeated often, in a quiet setting with a routine","Detailed explanations with several examples, given once so the patient isn't bored","Abstract comparisons, such as \"open like a lion\"","A busy, lively room to keep them distracted"],"answer":0,"ex":"People with ID often discriminate auditory stimuli more slowly and have weak short-term memory, so use short, clear, concrete language, repeat it, avoid overstimulation and establish a routine.","src":"rec","why":["These address slow auditory processing, weak memory and overstimulation.","Long explanations overwhelm them, and memory is weak.","Abstract cues may not be understood.","Overstimulation interferes with learning."]},{"id":"in-tf-1","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Intellectual disability is a diagnostic condition, not a description.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Mitchell's point about person-first language: the person comes before the disorder.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["He stressed exactly this: it's a diagnosis, so say \"a patient who has Down syndrome.\"","The statement is true; describing someone as their diagnosis is what person-first language avoids."]},{"id":"in-tf-2","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most people with intellectual disability live in institutions.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Most live at home with family; only about 1-2% live in institutions.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Only about 1-2% live in institutions, the most profoundly affected.","Correct: most live at home, and many adults live in group homes."]},{"id":"in-tf-3","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Alabama leads the nation in the share of adults with some form of disability: about 1 in 3.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Nationally it's 1 in 5; Alabama leads at 1 in 3.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Alabama is about 1 in 3 by the broad federal definition, the highest in the nation.","It is true; the national figure is 1 in 5."]},{"id":"in-tf-4","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: For general (not severe) disability, females have a slightly higher prevalence than males.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"General disability is slightly higher in females; severe disability is the same in both sexes.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["General disability: females a little higher. Severe disability: the same.","It's true for general disability; the sexes even out only for severe disability."]},{"id":"in-tf-5","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Under the 2018 ADA Code of Ethics revision, every dentist must treat every patient with a disability who asks.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The code doesn't require treating everyone; it forbids simply turning families away without helping them find care.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: you must refer if you can't treat, but you aren't required to treat everyone.","Correct: the duty is to connect the family with a dentist who can help."]},{"id":"in-tf-6","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The more profound the disability, the more likely a specific cause can be identified.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Mitchell said exactly this; 30-40% overall have no discernible cause.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: profound disability is more often traceable to a cause.","It's true; mild disability is more often unexplained."]},{"id":"in-tf-7","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Alabama Medicaid's dental benefit for people with severe ID continues for life.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The dental benefit ends at the 21st birthday, even when medical coverage continues.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["It ends at 21; most adults then have no dental insurance.","Correct: it stops at 21."]},{"id":"in-tf-8","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The effects of adverse childhood experiences are dose-related: the more ACEs, the bigger the measurable impact.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Scientists can detect a dose-response: more ACEs, more impact.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: risk climbs with the number of ACEs, though many with several ACEs still do fine.","It's true; the slides showed the dose-response."]},{"id":"in-2s-1","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The DSM-5 kept the term mental retardation until 2018.\n2. Rosa's Law removed the term mental retardation from U.S. federal documents in 2010.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Rosa's Law (2010) changed federal documents; the DSM-5 made the same change in 2013.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the DSM-5 switched to intellectual disability in 2013, not 2018.","Statement 2 is true, not false: it eliminated the term from federal documents in 2010.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the DSM-5 switched to intellectual disability in 2013, not 2018. Statement 2 is true, not false: it eliminated the term from federal documents in 2010.","Statement 1 is false: the DSM-5 switched to intellectual disability in 2013, not 2018. Statement 2 is true: it eliminated the term from federal documents in 2010."]},{"id":"in-2s-2","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Group homes typically house about 6-8 clients at similar levels with a caregiver.\n2. Only about 10-15% of people with ID live in institutions today.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Group homes are small; institutions now house only about 1-2%.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the figure is about 1-2%, not 10-15%.","Statement 1 is true, not false: that's the size and makeup he described.","Statement 1 is true: that's the size and makeup he described. Statement 2 is false: the figure is about 1-2%, not 10-15%.","Statement 1 is true, not false: that's the size and makeup he described. Statement 2 is false, not true: the figure is about 1-2%, not 10-15%."]},{"id":"in-2s-3","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Before 2019, CODA required graduates to be competent in assessing the treatment needs of special needs patients.\n2. In 2019 CODA added the word \"managing\" to that standard.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true: \"assessing\" before 2019, \"assessing and managing\" since.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: the pre-2019 standard said assessing. Statement 2 is true: 2019 added \"and managing.\"","Statement 1 is true, not false: the pre-2019 standard said assessing. Statement 2 is true, not false: 2019 added \"and managing.\"","Statement 2 is true, not false: 2019 added \"and managing.\"","Statement 1 is true, not false: the pre-2019 standard said assessing."]},{"id":"in-2s-4","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. For severe disability, Hispanics have the highest percentage of any group.\n2. Mobility disabilities are the most common type of general disability.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Native Americans are highest for severe disability and Hispanics low; mobility is the most common general disability.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Native Americans are highest; Hispanics are low.","Statement 2 is true, not false: mobility, much of it in the elderly, is most common.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Native Americans are highest; Hispanics are low. Statement 2 is true, not false: mobility, much of it in the elderly, is most common.","Statement 1 is false: Native Americans are highest; Hispanics are low. Statement 2 is true: mobility, much of it in the elderly, is most common."]},{"id":"in-2s-5","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. An IQ test alone is enough to diagnose intellectual disability today.\n2. Fetal alcohol syndrome accounts for about 20% of intellectual disability.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Modern diagnosis is a comprehensive evaluation; FAS is about 2%.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: today's assessment adds adaptive functioning and a long clinical evaluation. Statement 2 is false, not true: it's about 2%, not 20%.","Statement 1 is false: today's assessment adds adaptive functioning and a long clinical evaluation. Statement 2 is false: it's about 2%, not 20%.","Statement 1 is false, not true: today's assessment adds adaptive functioning and a long clinical evaluation.","Statement 2 is false, not true: it's about 2%, not 20%."]},{"id":"in-num-1","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are part of the DSM-5 diagnosis of intellectual disability?\n1. Deficits in intellectual functioning\n2. Deficits in adaptive functioning\n3. Onset during the developmental period\n4. An IQ score below 50","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"The three parts are intellectual deficits, adaptive deficits and onset in childhood; an IQ cutoff (70, not 50) belonged to older testing.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: IQ alone isn't the test today, and the old cutoff was 70.","It includes 4, which is false: IQ alone isn't the test today, and the old cutoff was 70. It leaves out 3, which is true: the deficits must begin in childhood.","It includes 4, which is false: IQ alone isn't the test today, and the old cutoff was 70. It leaves out 2, which is true: adaptive deficits were added to the diagnosis.","It leaves out 1, which is true: intellectual deficits are the first part."]},{"id":"in-num-2","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which describe Alabama in the lecture's data?\n1. About 1 in 3 adults has some disability\n2. About 16.7% have a severe disability\n3. It has the highest rate of severe disability in the country\n4. Its Medicaid dental benefit ends at 21","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Alabama leads the nation for general disability (1 in 3) and has 16.7% severe disability, but West Virginia leads for severe disability (19.4%). Medicaid dental ends at 21.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: the dental benefit stops at the 21st birthday.","It includes 3, which is false: West Virginia leads at 19.4%; Alabama is behind Arkansas and Kentucky too. It leaves out 4, which is true: the dental benefit stops at the 21st birthday.","It includes 3, which is false: West Virginia leads at 19.4%; Alabama is behind Arkansas and Kentucky too.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: West Virginia leads at 19.4%; Alabama is behind Arkansas and Kentucky too."]},{"id":"in-num-3","lec":"intro","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are general characteristics Dr. Mitchell sees often in his patients with ID?\n1. Some form of speech problem\n2. A single physical feature shared by all of them\n3. Hypersensitivity to sounds or tastes, with a sensitive gag reflex\n4. Emotional swings, with a child's coping skills","choices":["1, 2 and 4","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":2,"ex":"Speech problems, hypersensitivity and emotional swings are common; there is no physical feature shared by everyone with special needs.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: no feature defines everyone; syndromes have their own. It leaves out 3, which is true: hypersensitivity, including the gag reflex, is common.","It leaves out 4, which is true: they often struggle with coping skills like children.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: no feature defines everyone; syndromes have their own.","It includes 2, which is false: no feature defines everyone; syndromes have their own. It leaves out 1, which is true: most of his patients with ID have some speech issue."]},{"id":"in-match","lec":"intro","type":"match","stem":"Match each year with what happened.","pairs":[["1978","Laws require public schools to offer special education"],["2010","Rosa's Law"],["2013","DSM-5 adopts intellectual disability"],["2018","ADA Code of Ethics adds disability"],["2019","CODA adds \"and managing\""]],"ex":"1978 special education; 2010 Rosa's Law; 2013 DSM-5; 2018 ADA Code; 2019 CODA.","src":"rec"},{"id":"in-mm-1","lec":"intro","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which must be present for a DSM-5 diagnosis of intellectual disability?","choices":["Deficits in intellectual functioning","Deficits in adaptive functioning","Onset during the developmental period","An IQ score under 70","An identified genetic cause"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"DSM-5: deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning, beginning in the developmental period.","src":"rec","why":["One of the DSM-5's three parts, and all three must be present.","The ability to adapt to a setting and new sensory input: the part that matters most in autism.","Why a normally developing young adult with a brain injury is special needs but doesn't technically have ID.","The older, test-based cutoff; today's assessment is a comprehensive clinical one, not one test.","No cause is found in 30-40% of cases, and ID can be biological, psychosocial or both."]},{"id":"in-mm-2","lec":"intro","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are physical, prenatal causes of intellectual disability in the lecture?","choices":["Maternal malnutrition","Acute or chronic maternal infection","Fetal alcohol syndrome","A hypoxic event at birth","Lack of stimulation early in life","Fragile X"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Prenatal: maternal malnutrition, infection, fetal alcohol syndrome. Neonatal: prematurity, hypoxia, birth injury. Postnatal: injury, tumors, infections, toxins.","src":"rec","why":["Listed under physical, prenatal causes.","Listed under physical, prenatal causes.","Prenatal: about 2% of ID.","Neonatal, with prematurity and birth injury.","Environmental and psychosocial, not physical.","A single-gene error: a genetic cause."]},{"id":"in-mm-3","lec":"intro","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which match what Dr. Mitchell said about dental coverage for people with intellectual disability in Alabama?","choices":["Medicaid covers most of them until their 21st birthday, including OR care at Children's","After 21, people with severe ID usually keep Medicaid medical coverage but lose the dental benefit","The Affordable Care Act may keep someone on a parent's private insurance to about 26","Medicaid's dental benefit continues for life when the disability is severe","Most private dental plans make operating-room care easy to get"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Medicaid until 21 (dental ends then), maybe a parent's plan to about 26, then mostly out of pocket.","src":"rec","why":["And it covers them well.","The dental benefit ends at 21.","After that, most pay out of pocket, often from a Social Security check.","It ends at 21, whatever the severity.","Most make it nearly impossible; Blue Cross Blue Shield and ALL Kids were the exceptions he named."]},{"id":"bg-mal","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"What does \"maladaptive behavior\" mean in this course?","choices":["The patient cannot adapt to the treatment conditions in a dental office","Any behavior caused by a medication side effect","Behavior seen in every patient with IDD","A planned behavior the patient uses on purpose to get attention from the caregiver"],"answer":0,"ex":"Maladaptive behavior: the patient can't adapt to treatment conditions in a dental office. It applies to a subset of the IDD population and can be unpredictable.","src":"rec","why":["He called it the academic term for not behaving well in the chair.","Not the definition he gave.","It applies to only a subset; many adapt very well.","That sounds like a tantrum, not maladaptive behavior."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Behavior support: least invasive first; care limited only by the patient's benefit","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-least","bg-limits","bg-mal","bg-benefit","bg-tolerate"]}},{"id":"bg-limits","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"According to Dr. Mitchell, quality care for patients with IDD should be limited only by:","choices":["Their ability to benefit from and to tolerate the treatment","Their insurance coverage and the length of appointment the office can give","The caregiver's preference for esthetics","Their IQ score"],"answer":0,"ex":"Patients with IDD deserve quality care limited only by their ability to benefit from the treatment and their ability to tolerate it.","src":"rec","why":["Those are the two limits.","Not the limits he named.","Caregivers are included, but these aren't the limits.","Intellect alone doesn't limit care."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Behavior support: least invasive first; care limited only by the patient's benefit","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-least","bg-limits","bg-mal","bg-benefit","bg-tolerate"]}},{"id":"bg-benefit","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A profoundly disabled patient has stained but healthy anterior enamel. Esthetic crowns would mean a long, emotionally taxing process. Which limit does this case illustrate?","choices":["Ability to benefit from the treatment","Ability to tolerate the treatment","Contingent escape","Implied consent"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Mitchell used this example (and a CP patient with a severe gag reflex and a denture) for the ability to benefit.","src":"rec","why":["Does the patient actually gain from the crowns?","Tolerance is the second limit; this example was about benefit.","That's a behavior term, not a limit on care.","Consent isn't the issue here."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Behavior support: least invasive first; care limited only by the patient's benefit","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-least","bg-limits","bg-mal","bg-benefit","bg-tolerate"]}},{"id":"bg-tolerate","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A tooth could be saved with a cast crown, but the patient's maladaptive behavior makes restoring it dangerous, so it is extracted. Which limit is this?","choices":["Ability to tolerate the treatment","Ability to benefit from the treatment","Beneficence","Non-contingent escape"],"answer":0,"ex":"Tooth-colored restorations a patient can't keep clean, or a crown that is too dangerous to place: both illustrate the ability to tolerate treatment.","src":"rec","why":["The patient couldn't safely tolerate the restorative care.","The patient would benefit; tolerating it is the problem.","Beneficence guides decisions for incompetent patients; this example was about tolerance.","A behavior technique, not a limit on care."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Behavior support: least invasive first; care limited only by the patient's benefit","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-least","bg-limits","bg-mal","bg-benefit","bg-tolerate"]}},{"id":"bg-least","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which behavior support is the most commonly used and least invasive?","choices":["Communication","Passive stabilization","Active stabilization","Sedation"],"answer":0,"ex":"The three groups are communication, environment and protective stabilization. Communication tools are the most commonly used and least invasive.","src":"rec","why":["The first and least invasive tool.","More invasive, used only when needed.","Holding a patient is more invasive than talking.","Beyond this course, and far more invasive."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Behavior support: least invasive first; care limited only by the patient's benefit","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-least","bg-limits","bg-mal","bg-benefit","bg-tolerate"]}},{"id":"bg-social","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"What is a patient's \"social age\"?","choices":["The level of skill the patient has acquired","The patient's chronological age","The age at which the disability began","The age of the patient's caregiver"],"answer":0,"ex":"Speak at the social age: the level of skill the patient has acquired. A good history question: at what level does the patient understand (normal, none, or like a ___-year-old)?","src":"rec","why":["Speak at that level.","Social age is about acquired skills, not years.","That's about onset, part of the ID definition.","Not related."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Start talking at a normal level, then back down to the social age; tone carries","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-start","bg-social","bg-tone"]}},{"id":"bg-start","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Dr. Mitchell asked for special attention to how to start a conversation with a patient with ID. What did he say?","choices":["Start at a normal level, then back down to the social level you find","Start at a toddler level and work up only if they seem to follow","Speak only to the caregiver","Avoid speaking; use only gestures"],"answer":0,"ex":"Always go into a conversation talking at a normal level, then back down. Many patients have more cognitive ability than we credit, and speaking down shuts them down.","src":"rec","why":["If you must err, talk too high; talking down is offensive.","Starting low can offend a patient who understands far more.","Include caregivers, but talk to the patient.","Visual cues add to speech; they don't replace it."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Start talking at a normal level, then back down to the social age; tone carries","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-start","bg-social","bg-tone"]}},{"id":"bg-tone","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A family says their son understands none of the words you say. What does he still understand?","choices":["Tone and emotion","Abstract metaphors","Written instructions","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"ex":"Even with profound cognitive deficits, people understand tone and emotion. Anxiety or irritation ramps things up; calm and compassion bring a response in kind.","src":"rec","why":["A more primitive part of the brain reads the feeling behind the words.","Those are the hardest to understand.","Not what he said.","He picks up the emotional tone."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Start talking at a normal level, then back down to the social age; tone carries","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-start","bg-social","bg-tone"]}},{"id":"bg-goal","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"\"I'm going to lay the chair down like a bed. I need you to lay back against the chair. It will take until the count of 10 to stop.\" What technique is this?","choices":["Establishing clear and limited goals","Hand guarding","Contingent escape","Tell-show-do with a mouth prop"],"answer":0,"ex":"Clear and limited goals: tell the patient what you are going to do, what they need to do, and how long it will take.","src":"rec","why":["What you'll do, what they need to do, and how long.","That's a light touch on the hands.","That's stopping because of behavior.","No prop is involved."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Non-contingent vs contingent escape: the break comes at the goal","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-nce","bg-ce","bg-interval","bg-goal"]}},{"id":"bg-nce","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"In non-contingent escape, what does the dentist do when the patient starts resisting partway through a set goal?","choices":["Keeps going until the goal is reached, then gives the break","Stops at once and gives a break so the patient learns to trust the team","Ends the appointment","Lengthens the goal as a consequence"],"answer":0,"ex":"Non-contingent escape: set a specific small goal (five seconds, three teeth) and don't stop until it's done, even if the patient resists. Stopping for behavior is contingent escape.","src":"rec","why":["The break doesn't depend on the behavior.","That is contingent escape: the patient learns resisting ends treatment.","Ending it rewards the behavior too.","Intervals are kept consistent, not used as punishment."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Non-contingent vs contingent escape: the break comes at the goal","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-nce","bg-ce","bg-interval","bg-goal"]}},{"id":"bg-ce","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is stopping treatment whenever the patient acts out a problem?","choices":["It is contingent escape: the patient learns that acting out ends treatment","It violates informed consent","It is a form of protective stabilization","It shortens the appointment too much to finish the planned work or bill for it"],"answer":0,"ex":"If the break comes because of bad behavior, the patient learns to use that behavior to escape. Non-contingent escape keeps the break tied to the goal.","src":"rec","why":["The escape becomes contingent on bad behavior.","Stopping doesn't violate consent.","Stopping is the opposite of stabilizing.","Billing isn't the point."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Non-contingent vs contingent escape: the break comes at the goal","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-nce","bg-ce","bg-interval","bg-goal"]}},{"id":"bg-interval","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"How should intervals be handled in non-contingent escape?","choices":["Kept consistent, lengthened only as the patient relaxes","Varied randomly so the patient can't predict breaks","Made as long as possible from the start, so treatment is finished in fewer rounds","Shortened every time the patient resists"],"answer":0,"ex":"Be consistent with intervals so the patient adapts to the routine; as they relax and accept treatment, you may increase them.","src":"rec","why":["Consistency lets the patient settle into a rhythm.","Predictability is the point.","Start small and short.","That rewards resistance."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Non-contingent vs contingent escape: the break comes at the goal","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-nce","bg-ce","bg-interval","bg-goal"]}},{"id":"bg-water","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient fights as soon as the continuous air-water spray starts. What change does Dr. Mitchell recommend?","choices":["Spray in short bursts, in and out of the mouth","Use more water at once to finish the cleaning faster","Place a papoose board first","Tip the head back further"],"answer":0,"ex":"Many patients' gag reflexes are too sensitive for a continuous spray; they fight because they can't breathe. Work in short bursts.","src":"rec","why":["Many patients can't manage continuous water and can't breathe.","More water makes the gag and panic worse.","The fighting is about breathing; fix the water first.","Laying back further increases the gag risk."]},{"id":"bg-light","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"During a simple oral cancer screening, a patient kept turning away from the light. What fixed it?","choices":["Turning off the operatory lights and using only the unit light in the mouth","A papoose board","Playing loud music","Adding two more staff members to hold the patient's head toward the light"],"answer":0,"ex":"Adapt the operatory to the patient: once the room lights were off and only the unit light was used, the light-sensitive patient opened right up.","src":"rec","why":["The patient was light sensitive.","Restraint wasn't needed once the cause was found.","Music helps some patients, but this one was light sensitive.","More people would make it worse."]},{"id":"bg-hyper","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"For a patient with hyperdistractibility, what environmental change helps?","choices":["Fewer people in the operatory, ideally an isolated room","A busy open bay so they have more things to look at and stay entertained","A different operatory each visit for variety","Bright overhead lights"],"answer":0,"ex":"Patients who distract very easily do better with fewer people present, or in an isolated operatory.","src":"rec","why":["They are distracted very easily.","More stimulation makes it worse.","Routine and the same room help more.","That's unrelated to distractibility."]},{"id":"bg-cms","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Under the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services definition, which of these counts as a physical restraint?","choices":["A drug used to restrict behavior that isn't standard treatment for the patient's condition","A drug given at its usual dose for the patient's condition, such as a seizure medicine","A conversation that calms the patient","A pair of sunglasses"],"answer":0,"ex":"CMS: (A) any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material or equipment that reduces the ability to move freely; (B) a drug used as a restriction that is not a standard treatment or dose for the patient's condition.","src":"rec","why":["Part B of the definition covers medications used as restraints.","Standard treatment is excluded.","Communication isn't restraint.","Doesn't reduce movement."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"A staff member's hold is restraint too: active stabilization needs informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-active","bg-legal","bg-cms","bg-aapd"]}},{"id":"bg-aapd","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which detail is part of the AAPD definition of protective stabilization?","choices":["Limitation of movement by a person or by equipment, for a finite period of time","Only devices count; a person holding the patient still is not stabilization at all","It is used continuously for patients with dangerous behavior","Its purpose is to punish dangerous behavior"],"answer":0,"ex":"AAPD: the physical limitation of a patient's movement by a person or restrictive equipment, materials or devices for a finite period of time, to safely provide examination, diagnosis and/or treatment.","src":"rec","why":["A person holding the patient counts, and it's time-limited.","He stressed that a person holding the patient is protective stabilization.","It's for a finite period.","Its purpose is safe examination and treatment."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"A staff member's hold is restraint too: active stabilization needs informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-active","bg-legal","bg-cms","bg-aapd"]}},{"id":"bg-active","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is an example of active protective stabilization?","choices":["A staff member cradling the patient's head","A Molt mouth prop held open by the assistant","A Rainbow Wrap","A weighted blanket"],"answer":0,"ex":"Active stabilization is a human holding the patient still (Module 6's \"physical restraint\"); passive is a device (\"mechanical restraint\").","src":"rec","why":["A person holding the patient still.","A device: passive.","A device: passive.","It comforts rather than restrains, and it's a device."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"A staff member's hold is restraint too: active stabilization needs informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-active","bg-legal","bg-cms","bg-aapd"]}},{"id":"bg-module6","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"When Module 6 says \"mechanical restraint,\" what term does Dr. Mitchell use instead?","choices":["Passive protective stabilization","Active protective stabilization","Non-contingent escape","Therapeutic hold"],"answer":0,"ex":"Read Module 6's \"physical restraint\" as active protective stabilization, and \"mechanical restraint\" as passive.","src":"rec","why":["A device holding the patient still.","That replaces Module 6's \"physical restraint.\"","A communication technique.","A kind of active stabilization."]},{"id":"bg-carseat","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Why did Dr. Mitchell compare protective stabilization to an infant car seat?","choices":["Both restrain for the child's safety, and not using one can be negligent","Both can be used as a punishment when a child refuses to cooperate","Both are used only with sedation","Both need no consent"],"answer":0,"ex":"We don't hesitate to put an infant in a car seat, though it restrains and they may fuss, because we care about safety. Use that frame for stabilization.","src":"rec","why":["Stabilization is predominantly about safety.","Stabilization must never be punishment.","No sedation involved.","Stabilization always needs informed consent."]},{"id":"bg-never","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is an acceptable reason to use protective stabilization?","choices":["To keep a very resistive patient and the staff safe during needed treatment","To punish a patient who won't cooperate","To move a busy clinic faster when the schedule is running behind","Instead of trying communication first"],"answer":0,"ex":"Never use stabilization as punishment, as a substitute for guiding behavior, or for the practitioner's convenience. Its purpose is safety and efficient care.","src":"rec","why":["Safety during treatment is the purpose.","Never as punishment.","Never for the practitioner's convenience.","Never as a substitute for guiding behavior."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Protective stabilization done well: when, how, head holds and mouth props","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-never","bg-ideal","bg-papoose","bg-headhands","bg-molt","bg-fingers"]}},{"id":"bg-ideal","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Only the patient's head needs to be kept still. What does good protective stabilization call for?","choices":["Hold only the head: the least restrictive alternative","A full papoose board, to be safe in case the arms move too","Hold arms and legs as well","Sedation instead"],"answer":0,"ex":"Good protective stabilization has the lowest potential to cause injury, is the least restrictive alternative, and is used only with informed consent.","src":"rec","why":["Restrict only what must be restricted.","More restrictive than needed.","Not the least restrictive alternative.","Beyond this course, and far more restrictive."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Protective stabilization done well: when, how, head holds and mouth props","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-never","bg-ideal","bg-papoose","bg-headhands","bg-molt","bg-fingers"]}},{"id":"bg-negl","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"What does Module 6 say about treating a very resistive patient without restraints?","choices":["It may even be considered negligence toward patient and operator","It's always preferred, because using restraints adds legal risk for the office","It's required by law","It is only allowed with sedation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Module 6: where indicated, for physically resistive patients, restraints should be used without reservation; treating a very resistant patient without them may be negligence. Dr. Mitchell had a staff member hit when he wasn't quick enough.","src":"rec","why":["Where indicated, restraints should be used without reservation.","Not using them where indicated has its own risks.","No law requires it.","Not what the module said."]},{"id":"bg-headhands","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A staff member cradles a resistive patient's head with both hands. Where should the hands rest?","choices":["On the temporal and occipital bones, with the force spread across the hands","On the patient's cheeks, pressing firmly so the jaw can't move","Around the neck","On the forehead with the fingertips"],"answer":0,"ex":"Kneel or sit at the head of the chair; fingers spread, lower fingers cupped under the head, resting on the temporal and occipital bones, pressure distributed. Relax as the patient relaxes, but stay ready.","src":"rec","why":["Stay on bone and spread pressure to avoid bruising.","Soft tissue bruises; stay on bone.","Never on the neck.","Concentrated fingertip pressure can bruise."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Protective stabilization done well: when, how, head holds and mouth props","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-never","bg-ideal","bg-papoose","bg-headhands","bg-molt","bg-fingers"]}},{"id":"bg-module6head","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Why doesn't Dr. Mitchell recommend Module 6's forearm head hold?","choices":["It's uncomfortable for his shoulders and doesn't stop the patient lifting the head","It leaves finger marks and bruises on the patient's face","It is illegal in Alabama","It requires a papoose board"],"answer":0,"ex":"Module 6 recommends using the forearms (fewer finger marks), but Dr. Mitchell finds it hurts his shoulders and doesn't control the head lifting up.","src":"rec","why":["He finds it uncomfortable and unstable.","Minimizing finger marks is the forearm method's advantage.","Not what he said.","It doesn't."]},{"id":"bg-guard","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"What is hand guarding?","choices":["Gently laying hands over the patient's hands to remind them and prevent sudden movement","Strapping the wrists to a board","Wearing two pairs of gloves","Holding the patient's hands firmly behind the head for the whole procedure"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hand guarding reminds the patient where their hands should be and protects against sudden extreme movements; it can become an active restraint if needed.","src":"rec","why":["More a passive touch than an active restraint.","That's a papoose board.","Not a behavior technique.","Not described."]},{"id":"bg-legs","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient keeps digging their heels in and pushing up the chair. What is Dr. Mitchell's personal technique?","choices":["Cup under the ankles with open hands and lift the legs off the chair","Sit on the patient's knees","Squeeze the ankles tightly","Let the patient push, then pull them back down the chair each time they slide"],"answer":0,"ex":"Lift the legs by the ankles with open hands; if the patient arches, lift higher. The legs may move, but the head stays still, which is what matters in dentistry.","src":"rec","why":["Without leverage, the head stays still even if the legs move.","Unsafe and not described.","Open hands, not squeezing.","Lifting the legs prevents the leverage instead."]},{"id":"bg-home","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"What did Dr. Mitchell say about HOME and HOMAR?","choices":["Don't use them: HOMAR is not used at all, and HOME only rarely with extensive training","They're the preferred first step for a tantrum before any other stabilization is tried","HOMAR is safer than HOME","They are types of mouth props"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hand-over-mouth exercise (HOME) and hand-over-mouth with airway restriction (HOMAR) are presented in Module 6, but neither is a technique to use.","src":"rec","why":["They are in the reading but not current practice.","Not recommended.","HOMAR (airway restricted) isn't used at all anymore.","They are hand-over-mouth techniques."]},{"id":"bg-fingers","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Working without a mouth prop, where should you keep your fingers?","choices":["In the buccal vestibule","Across the occlusal surfaces to hold the mouth open","Under the tongue","Behind the last molar"],"answer":0,"ex":"Keep your fingers in the buccal fold, not across the dentition. Dr. Mitchell learned this one the hard way.","src":"rec","why":["If the patient bites down, your fingers are safe.","Fingers between the teeth get bitten.","Not what he recommended.","Still between the jaws."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Protective stabilization done well: when, how, head holds and mouth props","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-never","bg-ideal","bg-papoose","bg-headhands","bg-molt","bg-fingers"]}},{"id":"bg-molt","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Where should the blades of a Molt mouth prop sit?","choices":["On the posterior teeth","On the anterior teeth","On the lower lip","Against the palate"],"answer":0,"ex":"Keep the Molt blades on posterior teeth. Slipped onto anterior teeth they can luxate or avulse them, and lips caught under a blade can be lacerated. Report any trauma to the caregiver.","src":"rec","why":["Forward placement risks the anterior teeth and lips.","Anterior teeth can be luxated or even avulsed.","The lip can be caught and lacerated.","They sit on teeth."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Protective stabilization done well: when, how, head holds and mouth props","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-never","bg-ideal","bg-papoose","bg-headhands","bg-molt","bg-fingers"]}},{"id":"bg-silicone","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does Sparks use Molt mouth props with bare blades instead of silicone covers?","choices":["The covers can come off in the mouth and become a choking hazard","The covers make the prop too wide to slide between the teeth of a small mouth","Silicone can't be sterilized","Bare blades grip the teeth better"],"answer":0,"ex":"The silicone covers have been shown to come off in the patient's mouth and become a choking hazard.","src":"rec","why":["Safety, not comfort.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason given."]},{"id":"bg-mckesson","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Where is the McKesson bite block used?","choices":["In the second-floor and other adult clinics, not in special needs or pediatric dentistry","Mainly at Sparks and in pediatric dentistry, for patients who can't keep their mouth open","Only at home","Only under general anesthesia"],"answer":0,"ex":"The McKesson bite block is a rubber wedge used in the adult clinics; it isn't used in special needs or pediatric dentistry, and one patient simply crushed it.","src":"rec","why":["A rubber wedge; patients can crush it.","Sparks uses the Molt prop.","The Open-Wide prop is the home device.","Not what he said."]},{"id":"bg-openwide","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which mouth prop did Dr. Mitchell describe for families to use at home?","choices":["The Open-Wide mouth prop","The Molt mouth prop","The McKesson bite block","A papoose board"],"answer":0,"ex":"The Open-Wide mouth prop (Specialized Care Company) has a wood core surrounded by dense foam and is available on Amazon, four for about $20.","src":"rec","why":["Wood in dense foam, about $20 for four.","Used in the clinic.","Used in adult clinics.","Not a mouth prop."]},{"id":"bg-papoose","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is \"papoose board\" called the \"Kleenex\" term?","choices":["Papoose is a brand name now used for any board-type restraint","Because the boards are disposable and thrown away after one patient","Because they are made of paper","Because Kleenex makes them"],"answer":0,"ex":"Papoose Board is a specific brand (an inflexible padded board, wrist straps and canvas wraps with Velcro) whose name became the generic term.","src":"rec","why":["Like Kleenex for tissues.","They are reusable.","Padded board and canvas.","A brand-name joke, not a manufacturer."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Protective stabilization done well: when, how, head holds and mouth props","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-never","bg-ideal","bg-papoose","bg-headhands","bg-molt","bg-fingers"]}},{"id":"bg-rainbow","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"How does the Rainbow Wrap that Sparks uses differ from a Papoose Board?","choices":["It has a hinge so the patient can sit up","It has no wrist straps","It is a weighted blanket that comforts rather than restrains","It holds the mouth open"],"answer":0,"ex":"The Rainbow Wrap (Specialized Care Company), which Sparks calls \"the blanket,\" bends in the middle; since COVID a sheet goes between it and the patient.","src":"rec","why":["Stiff except at the hinge.","It has wrist straps too.","A different product.","That's a mouth prop."]},{"id":"bg-x","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"When wrapping a patient in the papoose, where do the wraps cross?","choices":["In an X over the chest","Over the face","Around the neck","Only at the ankles"],"answer":0,"ex":"Secure the wrists at the wrist bone, the leg wrap over the knees, then pull the body wraps tight (ideally two people) and form an X over the chest.","src":"rec","why":["Each comes down across a shoulder.","Never over the face.","Never around the neck.","The critical spot is the chest."]},{"id":"bg-weighted","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the main problem with weighted blankets in dentistry?","choices":["They are hard to disinfect between patients","They don't calm autistic patients","They restrain too tightly","They are illegal for minors"],"answer":0,"ex":"Weighted blankets give comforting sensory input (popular in autism) but don't restrain; the challenge is disinfecting them.","src":"rec","why":["Most would have to be laundered.","Many find them comforting.","They don't truly stop movement.","Not what he said."]},{"id":"bg-parents","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"In Dr. Mitchell's experience (not from studies), how do families of patients with moderate to severe IDD tend to feel about passive stabilization?","choices":["Many are relieved to find a practice that can do it","Almost all find it unacceptable and leave the practice","They prefer it to communication techniques","They are required by law to refuse it"],"answer":0,"ex":"Large parent studies show falling acceptance, but in his experience families of patients with moderate to severe IDD are often exhausted and relieved. Every family is different.","src":"rec","why":["Instead of being asked to hold the patient themselves.","Studies of large parent populations show that trend, but less so in this group.","Communication always comes first.","No such law."]},{"id":"bg-legal","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A staff member holds a patient's head without anyone obtaining consent. What could the dentist face?","choices":["A criminal charge of assault and battery and a malpractice claim","Nothing, because a person holding a patient isn't a form of restraint","Only a warning from CODA","Loss of Medicaid coverage for the patient"],"answer":0,"ex":"Active or passive stabilization requires informed consent. Without it: criminal charges (assault and battery) or a personal tort (malpractice).","src":"rec","why":["Holding a patient is restraint and needs consent.","A person holding a patient is protective stabilization.","CODA accredits schools; it doesn't police consent.","Not a consequence he described."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"A staff member's hold is restraint too: active stabilization needs informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-active","bg-legal","bg-cms","bg-aapd"]}},{"id":"bg-implied","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"An adult walks into a dental office and sits down for an exam and cleaning. What kind of consent is this?","choices":["Implied consent","Express consent","Informed consent for stabilization","No consent"],"answer":0,"ex":"Implied consent: actions or conduct raise the presumption that consent has been given. Routine care may be implied; beyond that, seek express consent.","src":"rec","why":["Their conduct raises the presumption of consent.","Express consent is given in words or writing.","Stabilization needs express informed consent.","Seeking care implies consent to routine care."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Implied vs express consent; a blanket consent is not informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-implied","bg-blanket","bg-justify"]}},{"id":"bg-blanket","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is a blanket consent signed at registration not informed consent?","choices":["The patient hasn't been told their needs or options yet","It isn't witnessed","Only caregivers may sign","It expires after 24 hours and must be signed again at each visit"],"answer":0,"ex":"Informed consent needs capacity, information and voluntariness. A blanket form at registration is meaningless because the patient hasn't been informed.","src":"rec","why":["Consent must be informed.","Witnessing helps documentation, but the problem is it isn't informed.","Competent patients sign their own.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Implied vs express consent; a blanket consent is not informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-implied","bg-blanket","bg-justify"]}},{"id":"bg-justify","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which item on a stabilization consent form does Dr. Mitchell say is often overlooked?","choices":["Justification for the use","The date","The patient's name","The name of the papoose board's manufacturer"],"answer":0,"ex":"Suggested items: date, justification (often overlooked), description of the restraint, its effectiveness, duration, and periodic re-evaluation; record any withdrawal of consent.","src":"rec","why":["For example, unable to tolerate treatment safely or biting instruments.","Straightforward and rarely missed.","Not what he flagged.","A description of the restraint matters, not the brand."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Implied vs express consent; a blanket consent is not informed consent","basis":"hint","ids":["bg-implied","bg-blanket","bg-justify"]}},{"id":"bg-competent","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with mild ID is competent to make decisions and declines a needed extraction. What does Dr. Mitchell say?","choices":["A competent patient has the right to decline care, even a bad decision","Stabilize and extract, because it's in their interest","The caregiver's consent overrides a competent patient's refusal of needed care","Refer for sedation without telling them"],"answer":0,"ex":"Issue one: is the patient competent? If so, they have every right to decline, and you should include patients in decisions whenever possible.","src":"rec","why":["Unless something is truly urgent.","Beneficence guides care for patients who aren't competent.","Not for a competent patient.","That violates consent."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Competence: a competent patient may decline; beneficence for one who isn't","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-competent","bg-benef"]}},{"id":"bg-benef","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which principle guides decisions for a patient who is not competent?","choices":["Beneficence: maximize the patient's well-being","Autonomy: do whatever the patient says in the moment","Non-contingent escape","Justice: treat every patient identically"],"answer":0,"ex":"Beneficence directs actions that maximize well-being. Is a composite placed in a wet, bleeding field to avoid stabilization really better for the patient?","src":"rec","why":["Issue two.","Autonomy matters for competent patients.","A behavior technique, not an ethical principle.","Not the principle he named."],"likely":{"lec":"behavior","t":"Competence: a competent patient may decline; beneficence for one who isn't","basis":"lecture","ids":["bg-competent","bg-benef"]}},{"id":"bg-tf-1","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: A staff member holding a patient's head still counts as protective stabilization and needs informed consent.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"A person holding the patient is active protective stabilization, and all stabilization needs consent.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: the AAPD definition includes a person, and consent is required.","It is true; people sometimes assume holding isn't restraint, but it is."]},{"id":"bg-tf-2","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Maladaptive behavior is seen in nearly every patient with IDD.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It applies only to a subset; many adapt very well, though who will is unpredictable.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: only a subset struggle to adapt.","Correct: many patients with IDD adapt well."]},{"id":"bg-tf-3","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Stopping treatment each time a patient resists is called non-contingent escape.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Stopping because of the behavior is contingent escape; non-contingent escape finishes the set goal first.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["That's contingent escape, the thing to avoid.","Correct: non-contingent escape doesn't stop for the behavior."]},{"id":"bg-tf-4","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Behavior is shaped better by rewarding the positive than by correcting the negative.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Positive reinforcement: praise desired behavior heavily, correct briefly, then praise again.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: \"more flies with honey than with vinegar.\"","It's true; that's the basis of positive reinforcement."]},{"id":"bg-tf-5","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The Molt mouth prop's blades should be placed on the anterior teeth for the best control.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Keep them on the posterior teeth; anterior placement can luxate or avulse teeth.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: anterior placement is the bad placement.","Correct: posterior teeth."]},{"id":"bg-tf-6","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Implied consent can come from a person's actions, such as an adult arriving for a routine exam.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Implied consent is conduct that raises the presumption of consent.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: seeking routine care implies consent to it.","It's true; express consent is the written or spoken kind."]},{"id":"bg-tf-7","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: A weighted blanket restrains a patient the way a papoose board does.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"A weighted blanket doesn't truly stop movement; it calms with sensory input.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: it comforts, it doesn't restrain.","Correct: it isn't a restraint."]},{"id":"bg-tf-8","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: During a therapeutic hold, the hold can be eased as the patient relaxes, which acts as positive reinforcement.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Relax with the patient without letting go; easing off rewards the behavior you want.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: they learn that cooperating brings relief.","It's true; he described easing off as reinforcement."]},{"id":"bg-2s-1","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Module 6's \"physical restraint\" means what Dr. Mitchell calls active protective stabilization.\n2. Module 6's \"mechanical restraint\" means what he calls passive protective stabilization.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true: physical = active (a person); mechanical = passive (a device).","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: a person holding the patient. Statement 2 is true: a device holding the patient.","Statement 1 is true, not false: a person holding the patient. Statement 2 is true, not false: a device holding the patient.","Statement 2 is true, not false: a device holding the patient.","Statement 1 is true, not false: a person holding the patient."]},{"id":"bg-2s-2","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Protective stabilization may be used as a convenience to treat more patients quickly.\n2. Good protective stabilization is the least restrictive alternative.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Least restrictive, least injurious and consented; never for convenience.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: never for the practitioner's convenience.","Statement 2 is true, not false: restrict only what must be restricted.","Statement 1 is false, not true: never for the practitioner's convenience. Statement 2 is true, not false: restrict only what must be restricted.","Statement 1 is false: never for the practitioner's convenience. Statement 2 is true: restrict only what must be restricted."]},{"id":"bg-2s-3","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Informed consent requires mental capacity, enough information and voluntariness.\n2. If a caregiver hesitates, it's best to proceed and explain afterward.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"The three elements are capacity, information and voluntariness; with hesitation, it's best not to proceed.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: if there is hesitation, it's best not to proceed.","Statement 1 is true, not false: those are the three elements.","Statement 1 is true: those are the three elements. Statement 2 is false: if there is hesitation, it's best not to proceed.","Statement 1 is true, not false: those are the three elements. Statement 2 is false, not true: if there is hesitation, it's best not to proceed."]},{"id":"bg-2s-4","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. HOMAR is still commonly used in special needs dentistry.\n2. The McKesson bite block is the standard mouth prop at Sparks.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"HOMAR isn't used at all anymore, and Sparks uses the Molt prop.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: HOMAR is not used at all anymore. Statement 2 is false, not true: Sparks uses the Molt prop; the McKesson block is used in adult clinics.","Statement 1 is false: HOMAR is not used at all anymore. Statement 2 is false: Sparks uses the Molt prop; the McKesson block is used in adult clinics.","Statement 1 is false, not true: HOMAR is not used at all anymore.","Statement 2 is false, not true: Sparks uses the Molt prop; the McKesson block is used in adult clinics."]},{"id":"bg-2s-5","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Speaking at a patient's social age means using the level of skill the patient has acquired.\n2. Because short-term memory may be weak, home care instructions should be repeated at every visit.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true: speak at the social age, and reinforce home care often.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: social age is the acquired skill level. Statement 2 is true: weak short-term memory means frequent reinforcement.","Statement 1 is true, not false: social age is the acquired skill level. Statement 2 is true, not false: weak short-term memory means frequent reinforcement.","Statement 2 is true, not false: weak short-term memory means frequent reinforcement.","Statement 1 is true, not false: social age is the acquired skill level."]},{"id":"bg-num-1","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are examples of passive protective stabilization?\n1. A staff member's head hold\n2. A Rainbow Wrap\n3. A Molt mouth prop\n4. Hand guarding","choices":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only","2 and 4 only","3 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Passive stabilization uses devices: mouth props and papoose-type wraps. Head holds and hand guarding are done by people: active.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: a person holding: active.","Statements 2 and 3 are true. Statement 1 is false: a person holding: active. Statement 4 is false: a person's hands: active.","It includes 4, which is false: a person's hands: active. It leaves out 3, which is true: a device holding the mouth open.","It leaves out 2, which is true: a device holding the body."]},{"id":"bg-num-2","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which should be documented when protective stabilization is used?\n1. The justification for its use\n2. A description of the restraint\n3. The cost of the device\n4. How effective it was and how long it was used","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Suggested items: date, justification, description, effectiveness, duration and periodic re-evaluation; cost isn't one of them.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 3, which is false: cost isn't part of the consent documentation.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: cost isn't part of the consent documentation.","It includes 3, which is false: cost isn't part of the consent documentation. It leaves out 1, which is true: justification is the often-overlooked item.","It leaves out 1, which is true: justification is the often-overlooked item."]},{"id":"bg-num-3","lec":"behavior","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are reasons Dr. Mitchell gives for using protective stabilization?\n1. It keeps the patient and staff safe\n2. It teaches the patient a lesson about behavior\n3. It allows treatment of the patient\n4. It makes care more efficient","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"It allows treatment, provides safety and increases efficiency; it is never punishment.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: never as punishment.","It includes 2, which is false: never as punishment. It leaves out 3, which is true: it lets needed care happen.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: never as punishment.","It leaves out 3, which is true: it lets needed care happen."]},{"id":"bg-match","lec":"behavior","type":"match","stem":"Match each term to its meaning.","pairs":[["Maladaptive behavior","Can't adapt to the dental setting"],["Non-contingent escape","The break comes when the goal is met, not when the patient resists"],["Express consent","Given in words or writing"],["Implied consent","Shown by the person's conduct"],["Beneficence","Acting to maximize the patient's well-being"]],"ex":"These are the five terms the lecture asked you to define (with protective stabilization).","src":"rec"},{"id":"bg-mm-1","lec":"behavior","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are passive stabilization?","choices":["A Molt mouth prop","A papoose board or Rainbow Wrap","An Open-Wide mouth prop","A staff member cradling the head with spread fingers","Hand guarding","Crossing and holding the ankles"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Active = a person holds the patient still; passive = a device does. Both need informed consent.","src":"rec","why":["A device holds the mouth open: passive.","A device holds the body: passive.","A device: passive.","A person holds the patient: active stabilization.","A person's hands over the patient's: listed with the active techniques.","A therapeutic hold by a person: active."]},{"id":"bg-mm-2","lec":"behavior","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which describe good protective stabilization?","choices":["The lowest potential to cause injury","The least restrictive alternative","Used only with informed consent","Short, used only for the time needed","Used as a consequence when a patient won't cooperate","Used for every child to keep the schedule moving"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Least injury, least restrictive, consent, short. Never punishment, never a substitute for guiding behavior, never convenience.","src":"rec","why":["One of its characteristics.","If only the head needs holding, hold only the head.","Active and passive both need it.","Its use should be short and let staff finish the needed treatment.","Never as punishment.","Never as a convenience: clinics that did this caused real problems for the profession."]},{"id":"bg-mm-3","lec":"behavior","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are elements of informed consent?","choices":["Mental capacity to make reasonable decisions","Enough information to make an educated decision","Given voluntarily","A blanket consent signed at registration","The caregiver's signature in every case"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Capacity, information, voluntariness; then document it, specific and witnessed.","src":"rec","why":["Of whoever gives the consent.","Being informed of the needs and options.","If there is hesitation, it's best not to proceed.","Meaningless: the patient hasn't been told their needs or options.","A competent patient consents, and can refuse, for themselves."]},{"id":"ds-1866","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"When was Down syndrome first recognized as a chromosomal anomaly?","choices":["1956","1865","1929","2008"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Langdon Down described and named the syndrome in 1865; the extra chromosome 21 was identified in 1956.","src":"rec","why":["Almost 100 years after it was described.","1865 is when Dr. Langdon Down described and named it.","That year is the life-expectancy data point (9 years).","That's the estimate of 250,000 people in the U.S."]},{"id":"ds-nondis","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What is nondisjunction?","choices":["A chromosome pair fails to split during meiosis, so one gamete gets an extra copy","A chromosome breaks during meiosis and part of it is lost from the gamete","A gene's code repeats too many times","A mutation that makes too much uric acid"],"answer":0,"ex":"Normally the pairs split so each gamete has 23. With nondisjunction the 21st pair doesn't split: one gamete lacks 21 (not viable) and one has 24; fertilized, it makes a 47-chromosome cell.","src":"rec","why":["In Down syndrome, the 21st pair doesn't split.","That's a deletion, as in Prader-Willi.","That's the fragile X mechanism.","That's Lesch-Nyhan."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"The genetics: nondisjunction trisomy 21, translocation, mosaicism","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-nondis","ds-pct","ds-trans","ds-mosaic","ds-match2"]}},{"id":"ds-mom","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"In nondisjunction Down syndrome, where does the extra chromosome come from most often?","choices":["The mother's egg, over 90% of the time","The father's sperm, over 90% of the time","Equally from mother and father","From a cell division after birth"],"answer":0,"ex":"Over 90% of the time nondisjunction occurs in the maternal egg; less than 10% in paternal sperm.","src":"rec","why":["Less than 10% come from the father's sperm.","Paternal cases are under 10%.","Mostly maternal.","That would be mosaicism, and it happens early in the embryo."]},{"id":"ds-trans","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"A person with Down syndrome has 46 chromosomes, one of them unusually long. Which type is this?","choices":["Translocation","Trisomy 21","Mosaicism","Fragile X"],"answer":0,"ex":"Translocation (5%): part of an extra chromosome 21 attaches to another chromosome, usually the 14th, so the total stays at 46.","src":"rec","why":["Extra 21 material attached to another chromosome, usually 14.","Trisomy 21 has 47 chromosomes.","Mosaicism mixes cells with 46 and 47.","A different syndrome on the X chromosome."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"The genetics: nondisjunction trisomy 21, translocation, mosaicism","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-nondis","ds-pct","ds-trans","ds-mosaic","ds-match2"]}},{"id":"ds-mosaic","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What makes mosaicism different from the other types of Down syndrome?","choices":["Nondisjunction happens early in the embryo, so some cells have 46 and some 47","The extra chromosome 21 attaches to chromosome 14, so the count stays at 46","It always comes from the father","It is the most common type"],"answer":0,"ex":"In mosaicism the egg and sperm are normal; nondisjunction happens in an early cell division of the embryo, giving a mix of 46- and 47-chromosome cells. About 1% of cases.","src":"rec","why":["Like the tiles of a mosaic.","That's translocation.","Not what defines it.","It's the rarest, about 1%."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"The genetics: nondisjunction trisomy 21, translocation, mosaicism","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-nondis","ds-pct","ds-trans","ds-mosaic","ds-match2"]}},{"id":"ds-pct","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of Down syndrome cases are trisomy 21?","choices":["94%","50%","5%","1%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Trisomy 21 is 94% of cases, translocation 5%, mosaicism 1%.","src":"rec","why":["Translocation 5%, mosaicism 1%.","Far too low.","That's translocation.","That's mosaicism."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"The genetics: nondisjunction trisomy 21, translocation, mosaicism","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-nondis","ds-pct","ds-trans","ds-mosaic","ds-match2"]}},{"id":"ds-age","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"For a mother aged 40-44, about how many live births have Down syndrome?","choices":["1 in 130","1 in 2,300","1 in 280","1 in 65"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 1 in 700 overall: 1 in 2,300 at 20, 1 in 280 at 34-39, 1 in 130 at 40-44, 1 in 65 at 46.","src":"rec","why":["Risk climbs with maternal age.","That's at about age 20.","That's at 34-39.","That's at 46 and over."]},{"id":"ds-life","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What was the average life expectancy of a person with Down syndrome in 1929?","choices":["9 years","30 years","55 years","2 years"],"answer":0,"ex":"Life expectancy rose from 9 years (1929) to 30 (1980) to 55 (2000s), and higher now.","src":"rec","why":["Most died of pneumonia before antibiotics.","That was 1980.","That was the 2000s.","Too low."]},{"id":"ds-iq","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of people with Down syndrome have mild or moderate intellectual disability?","choices":["About 68%","About 15%","About 17%","About 42%"],"answer":0,"ex":"42% mild, 26% moderate (about 68%), 15% severe, 17% profound.","src":"rec","why":["42% mild plus 26% moderate.","That's severe.","That's profound.","That's mild alone."]},{"id":"ds-alz","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What do autopsies of people with Down syndrome show about the brain?","choices":["100% show changes associated with Alzheimer's, though clinical signs are less evident","No changes before age 70","Only about half show any change, and only after age 50","Changes of Parkinson's disease"],"answer":0,"ex":"All post-mortem findings show Alzheimer's-type brain changes, though clinical findings aren't as evident; about half show deterioration before 50.","src":"rec","why":["A physical change whose day-to-day meaning is unclear.","Changes are universal at autopsy.","About half show clinical deterioration before 50; autopsy changes are in all.","He said Alzheimer's."]},{"id":"ds-person","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about behavior in Down syndrome matches the lecture?","choices":["As intellectual disability increases, behavior tends to worsen","Their emotions are steady and slow to change, even when frightened","They are unaffected by the mood of the room","Profoundly affected patients are the most playful"],"answer":0,"ex":"Generally happy, playful and loving, but emotions shift quickly and they sense others' emotions. As ID increases, behavior decreases: mistrust, withdrawal from strangers, even violent protectiveness.","src":"rec","why":["Lower-functioning patients may withdraw or be violently protective.","Emotions can shift rapidly.","They are sensitive to the emotions around them.","The happy, playful picture fits the mild-to-moderate majority."]},{"id":"ds-midface","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the cardinal facial characteristic of Down syndrome?","choices":["Midface hypoplasia","Mandibular prognathism","Hypertelorism","A smooth philtrum"],"answer":0,"ex":"Midface hypoplasia is the cardinal characteristic: failure of complete development of the maxillary complex, giving a flat or sunken face.","src":"rec","why":["The maxillary complex fails to develop fully: a flat, sunken midface.","Class III here comes from the small maxilla, not a big mandible.","Wide-set eyes are an Apert feature.","That's fetal alcohol syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Midface hypoplasia, the cardinal feature: mouth breathing, water and Class III","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-midface","ds-mouthbr","ds-class3"]}},{"id":"ds-mouthbr","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"A boy with Down syndrome starts fighting the moment the air-water spray fills his mouth. What is the likely reason?","choices":["He can't breathe through his nose, so water in the mouth cuts off his airway","He is having an autistic meltdown triggered by the sound of the suction","He has atlantoaxial instability","The water is too cold"],"answer":0,"ex":"Midface hypoplasia, a flat nasal bridge and a deviated septum and conchae narrow the airway, so many breathe through the mouth. Flood the mouth and you cut off the airway.","src":"rec","why":["Midface hypoplasia and nasal malformations make many mouth breathers.","The anatomy explains it; he's fighting for air.","That concerns neck extension, not water.","Not the reason given."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Midface hypoplasia, the cardinal feature: mouth breathing, water and Class III","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-midface","ds-mouthbr","ds-class3"]}},{"id":"ds-epi","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What is an epicanthal fold?","choices":["A fold of upper eyelid skin covering the inner corner of the eye","A hole in the iris","Wide spacing between the eyes","Involuntary, rapid movement of the eyes from side to side"],"answer":0,"ex":"Epicanthal folds cover the inner corner of the eye and are present in about 78%. Strabismus (14-54%), nystagmus and refractive error are also common.","src":"rec","why":["Gives the slanting, almond-shaped eye; present in about 78%.","That's a coloboma.","That's hypertelorism.","That's nystagmus."]},{"id":"ds-heart50","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of people with Down syndrome have congenital heart disease?","choices":["About 50%","About 5%","About 90%","About 20%"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 50% have congenital heart disease, which is why the cardiac history is so important.","src":"rec","why":["Half have some cardiac issue.","Far too low.","Too high.","Too low."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Congenital heart disease (about half; AV canal) and antibiotic prophylaxis","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-heart50","ds-avcanal","ds-tof","ds-prophy","ds-match"]}},{"id":"ds-avcanal","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which heart defect is most common in Down syndrome?","choices":["Atrioventricular canal (AV septal, endocardial cushion) defect","Tetralogy of Fallot","Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect"],"answer":0,"ex":"The AV canal defect is the most common (45-60%), then VSD (32-35%). Most are repaired early and, once healed, usually need no antibiotics.","src":"rec","why":["45-60% of the cardiac cases.","Only 4-6%.","About 7%.","1-8%."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Congenital heart disease (about half; AV canal) and antibiotic prophylaxis","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-heart50","ds-avcanal","ds-tof","ds-prophy","ds-match"]}},{"id":"ds-tof","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which defect often requires antibiotic prophylaxis and a cardiac consult?","choices":["Tetralogy of Fallot","A repaired ventricular septal defect, years after surgery","A surgically closed patent ductus arteriosus","A repaired atrial septal defect"],"answer":0,"ex":"Tetralogy of Fallot (VSD, pulmonary stenosis, thick right ventricle, aorta over the VSD) causes cyanosis and needs open-heart surgery that often isn't complete: get a cardiac consult; it often requires prophylaxis.","src":"rec","why":["Often not completely repaired; cyanosis.","Usually no antibiotics once healed.","Usually not an issue after closure.","Usually no antibiotics once healed."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Congenital heart disease (about half; AV canal) and antibiotic prophylaxis","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-heart50","ds-avcanal","ds-tof","ds-prophy","ds-match"]}},{"id":"ds-tofparts","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is NOT one of the four parts of tetralogy of Fallot?","choices":["An atrial septal defect","A ventricular septal defect","Pulmonary stenosis","An aorta lying over the ventricular septal defect"],"answer":0,"ex":"Tetralogy of Fallot: VSD, pulmonary stenosis, a more muscular right ventricle, and an aorta over the VSD.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["The septal defect in tetralogy is ventricular.","One of the four.","One of the four.","One of the four (with a thicker right ventricle)."]},{"id":"ds-mvp","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What did the EKG study show about mitral valve prolapse in Down syndrome?","choices":["57% had it, though only 14% reported it in their history","It was no more common than in the control group of patients without Down syndrome","Only 4% had it","It always shows up in the medical history"],"answer":0,"ex":"By history, 14% reported mitral valve prolapse (vs 4% of controls); on EKG, 57% had it. Aortic regurgitation: 6% by history, 11% on EKG.","src":"rec","why":["Patients often don't know their own cardiac disease.","The rates were well above controls.","4% was the control rate in the history study.","The history badly underreported it."]},{"id":"ds-prophy","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which patient requires antibiotic prophylaxis under the guidelines in the lecture?","choices":["A child whose defect was completely repaired with a prosthetic patch three months ago","An adult whose VSD was repaired in infancy with no residual defect","A patient with mitral valve prolapse found on an EKG but no symptoms or repair","Any patient with Down syndrome"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prophylaxis is required for: unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease (including shunts and conduits); a defect completely repaired with prosthetic material or a device, for the first six months; and repaired disease with residual defects at or next to the prosthetic patch or device.","src":"rec","why":["The first six months after a prosthetic repair.","Healed repairs without residual defects don't need it.","Not on the list.","Only the highest-risk cardiac conditions."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Congenital heart disease (about half; AV canal) and antibiotic prophylaxis","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-heart50","ds-avcanal","ds-tof","ds-prophy","ds-match"]}},{"id":"ds-leuk","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"How much more common is leukemia in Down syndrome?","choices":["15-50 times","2-3 times","It is less common","About 100 times"],"answer":0,"ex":"Leukemia is 15-50 times more common. Down syndrome myeloid leukemia has a cure rate around 80%; ALL now responds almost as well as in others. Solid tumors are not increased, maybe decreased.","src":"rec","why":["With a high cure rate for Down syndrome myeloid leukemia.","Too low.","Solid tumors may be less common; leukemia is not.","Higher than the range he gave."]},{"id":"ds-pneum","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Before antibiotics, what caused most early deaths in Down syndrome?","choices":["Pneumonia","Leukemia","Atlantoaxial injury","Diabetes"],"answer":0,"ex":"A smaller thymus, fewer B and T cells and impaired function mean more respiratory infections, otitis media and pneumonia, which caused most early deaths before antibiotics.","src":"rec","why":["Impaired immunity, no antibiotics.","More common, but not the main cause of early deaths he named.","Not the main cause.","Not the main cause."]},{"id":"ds-thyroid","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about thyroid disease in Down syndrome is correct?","choices":["Autoimmune hypothyroidism affects males and females equally","Hyperthyroidism is the most common thyroid problem in this population","Thyroid symptoms are easy to recognize","TSH is usually low"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypothyroidism is 28-35 times more common in infants, TSH is raised in 25-60%, symptoms are hard to diagnose, and autoimmune hypothyroidism (7.5-34% of children 8 and older) has an equal sex distribution.","src":"rec","why":["In the general population it's mostly female.","Hypothyroidism is.","They're hard to diagnose against the usual signs of Down syndrome.","TSH is raised in 25-60%."]},{"id":"ds-celiac","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of people with Down syndrome have celiac disease?","choices":["Up to 20%","About 1%","About 60%","About 80%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Endocrine-related problems: thyroid disease, diabetes (3 times more likely) and celiac disease (up to 20%).","src":"rec","why":["An immune response triggered by gluten.","Far too low for this group.","60% is the GERD figure.","The upper end of the sleep apnea range."]},{"id":"ds-aai","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Why should you avoid hyperextending the neck of a patient with Down syndrome, especially during stabilization or intubation?","choices":["Atlantoaxial instability: lax ligaments let C1 and C2 move too far","Their obstructive sleep apnea worsens when the neck is extended back","It triggers a seizure","It raises the risk of NUG"],"answer":0,"ex":"Atlantoaxial instability (10-20%): congenitally lax ligaments between C1 and C2 allow an unsafe range of motion. It may lessen with age.","src":"rec","why":["Risk of spinal cord injury; affects 10-20%.","Not the reason given.","Not related.","Not related."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Atlantoaxial instability: don't hyperextend the neck","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-aai"]}},{"id":"ds-osa","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Obstructive sleep apnea affects about what share of people with Down syndrome?","choices":["50-80%","10-20%","About 5%","About 30%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Obstructive sleep apnea 50-80%, dysphagia 58%, GERD 60%: position carefully, don't flood the mouth, suction well, and be cautious with sedation.","src":"rec","why":["Midface and airway anatomy.","That's atlantoaxial instability.","Too low.","Too low."]},{"id":"ds-class3","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is Class III malocclusion common in Down syndrome?","choices":["The maxilla is underdeveloped (midface hypoplasia)","The mandible is too large (prognathism)","Tongue thrust pushes the lower teeth forward","Missing lower incisors"],"answer":0,"ex":"Class III in 30-70%, from midface hypoplasia, not a large mandible. Class II 3-32%, posterior crossbite 71%, open bite 5%.","src":"rec","why":["The mandible is usually normal size.","He said it's not prognathism.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason given."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Midface hypoplasia, the cardinal feature: mouth breathing, water and Class III","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-midface","ds-mouthbr","ds-class3"]}},{"id":"ds-xbite","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which occlusal finding is seen in about 71% of people with Down syndrome?","choices":["Posterior crossbite","Anterior open bite","Class II malocclusion","Severe crowding"],"answer":0,"ex":"Narrow palate, spacing, Class III 30-70%, Class II 3-32%, posterior crossbite 71%, open bite 5%.","src":"rec","why":["The most frequent occlusal finding listed.","Only about 5%.","3-32%.","They usually have spacing."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Oral findings: caries, NUG, crossbite, missing laterals","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-caries","ds-nug","ds-xbite","ds-lat"]}},{"id":"ds-lat","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Missing lateral incisors occur in about what share of people with Down syndrome?","choices":["35-43%","About 2%","About 71%","About 90%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Missing teeth are more common: laterals in 35-43% vs 2% generally. Also peg or conical teeth and microdontia.","src":"rec","why":["Compared with about 2% in general.","That's the general population.","That's posterior crossbite.","Too high."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Oral findings: caries, NUG, crossbite, missing laterals","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-caries","ds-nug","ds-xbite","ds-lat"]}},{"id":"ds-tauro","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What is taurodontism?","choices":["Apical displacement of the root bifurcation and pulp chamber floor","Short, small clinical crowns on teeth with normal pulp chambers","Peg-shaped anterior teeth","Delayed eruption of the canines"],"answer":0,"ex":"Taurodontism: the floor of the pulp chamber and the bifurcation sit far apically, giving large pulp chambers, seen with microdontia in Down syndrome.","src":"rec","why":["Large pulp chambers.","That's microdontia.","A separate finding.","A separate finding."]},{"id":"ds-perio","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most common dental condition in Down syndrome?","choices":["Periodontal disease","Rampant caries","Enamel erosion from GERD","Supernumerary teeth"],"answer":0,"ex":"Periodontal disease is the most common: higher than the general population and other disabilities, early onset in 60-95%, detectable at 6.","src":"rec","why":["Early onset, faster progression, higher than in other disabilities.","Caries is commonly believed lower.","GERD is common, but perio is the big one.","They have missing teeth, not extra."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Periodontal disease is the most common dental condition: early, fast, probe from age 6","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-perio","ds-perio6","ds-doxy","ds-extract"]}},{"id":"ds-perio6","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"From what age does Dr. Mitchell suggest periodontal probing in children with Down syndrome?","choices":["About 6 years","About 18 years","Only after all permanent teeth erupt","Never: probing isn't tolerated"],"answer":0,"ex":"Begin perio probing as early as 6; look for bone loss on all films. Some patients lose teeth to bone loss in their early teens.","src":"rec","why":["Bone loss has been seen that early.","Far too late; teeth can be lost in the early teens.","Too late.","He recommends probing early and checking every film."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Periodontal disease is the most common dental condition: early, fast, probe from age 6","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-perio","ds-perio6","ds-doxy","ds-extract"]}},{"id":"ds-nug","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"In the institutional study, how did NUG in Down syndrome compare with NUG in other IDD?","choices":["45% vs 4%, often without the usual fetid breath or pain","4% vs 45%: rarer in Down syndrome","About the same in both groups","About 45%, and always with severe pain and fetid breath"],"answer":0,"ex":"45% of patients with Down syndrome had NUG vs 4% of patients with other IDD, and they rarely report the fetid breath or pain seen in others.","src":"rec","why":["Very common, and easy to miss.","Reversed.","Far higher in Down syndrome.","They rarely report pain."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Oral findings: caries, NUG, crossbite, missing laterals","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-caries","ds-nug","ds-xbite","ds-lat"]}},{"id":"ds-nugrisk","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is NOT one of the oral conditions related to developing NUG in Down syndrome?","choices":["Widely spaced teeth","Traumatic occlusion","Peg-shaped anterior teeth","Primary teeth whose roots don't resorb"],"answer":0,"ex":"Factors: crowded dentition (rare in Down syndrome), traumatic occlusion, peg-shaped anterior teeth, primary teeth with roots that don't resorb, and a high frenum.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["Crowding, not spacing, is the risk factor.","A listed factor.","A listed factor.","A listed factor (with a high frenum)."]},{"id":"ds-caries","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"What does the evidence say about caries in Down syndrome?","choices":["It's commonly thought lower, but the evidence is mixed","It's proven to be far higher than in their typically developing siblings","Caries never occurs in Down syndrome","It's identical in every study"],"answer":0,"ex":"Several studies found more caries-free patients (72-78% vs 46-58% of siblings), but the International Journal of Paediatric Dentistry (2016) found no scientific evidence that caries experience is lower.","src":"rec","why":["A 2016 review found no evidence they have less.","Several studies found less.","Dr. Mitchell has definitely seen it.","The studies disagree."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Oral findings: caries, NUG, crossbite, missing laterals","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-caries","ds-nug","ds-xbite","ds-lat"]}},{"id":"ds-doxy","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"When can low-dose doxycycline (Periostat) be used for periodontal disease in a child with Down syndrome?","choices":["After about 8-10 years of age","From age 2","Never in Down syndrome","Only under general anesthesia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Antibiotic options: penicillin; doxycycline (Periostat) in low dose after 8-10; metronidazole; and local PerioChip or Atridox.","src":"rec","why":["No longer a staining risk to developing teeth.","Tetracyclines stain developing teeth.","He listed it as an option after 8-10.","Not related."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Periodontal disease is the most common dental condition: early, fast, probe from age 6","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-perio","ds-perio6","ds-doxy","ds-extract"]}},{"id":"ds-chx","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"A moderately disabled patient with Down syndrome won't allow good brushing and can't spit. How can chlorhexidine be applied?","choices":["Soak the toothbrush in chlorhexidine and brush it on all teeth","Have the patient rinse with it for 30 seconds and spit, twice a day","Use a custom tray overnight","Chlorhexidine can't be used without rinsing"],"answer":0,"ex":"Long-term chlorhexidine may help; staining is an issue and spitting may be limited, so brush it onto all teeth and gums.","src":"rec","why":["Spitting isn't needed.","They can't spit well.","Trays are rarely tolerated.","It can be brushed on."]},{"id":"ds-liquid","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"An adult with Down syndrome who can't swallow pills needs amoxicillin 500 mg three times a day for 5 days. Using 250 mg/5 mL, which prescription matches?","choices":["Dispense 150 mL; take 10 mL by mouth TID for 5 days","Dispense 75 mL; take 5 mL by mouth TID for 5 days","Dispense 15 mL; take 1 mL by mouth TID for 5 days","Dispense 150 mL; take 20 mL by mouth TID for 5 days"],"answer":0,"ex":"Find the highest readily available liquid and prescribe a volume close to the pill dose. 250 mg/5 mL: 10 mL = 500 mg; 10 mL TID for 5 days = 150 mL.","src":"rec","why":["10 mL of 250 mg/5 mL = 500 mg.","5 mL of 250/5 is only 250 mg.","Far too little.","20 mL would be 1,000 mg a dose."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Liquid antibiotic doses: 250 mg/5 mL, 10 mL = 500 mg","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-liquid","ds-liquid400"]}},{"id":"ds-liquid400","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Using amoxicillin 400 mg/5 mL for the same patient, what did Dr. Mitchell write?","choices":["Dispense 75 mL; take 1 teaspoon (5 mL) TID for 5 days","Dispense 150 mL; take 10 mL TID for 5 days","Dispense 15 tablets; take 1 TID","Dispense 75 mL; take 1 tablespoon TID"],"answer":0,"ex":"Amoxicillin 400/5, disp 75 mL, 1 tsp (5 mL = 400 mg) TID for 5 days: not exactly 1,500 mg a day, but antibiotics allow some flexibility, and less liquid is easier.","src":"rec","why":["A little less drug, but less liquid to swallow.","That's the 250 mg/5 mL version.","That's the tablet version.","A tablespoon is 15 mL, 1,200 mg a dose."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Liquid antibiotic doses: 250 mg/5 mL, 10 mL = 500 mg","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-liquid","ds-liquid400"]}},{"id":"ds-strong","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"As intellectual disability increases in a patient with Down syndrome, which restorative approach does Dr. Mitchell favor?","choices":["Strong over esthetic: stainless steel crowns, posterior amalgam, RMGI","Esthetic over strong: tooth-colored composites everywhere, even without isolation","Implants for every missing tooth","Removable partial dentures"],"answer":0,"ex":"High-functioning, cooperative patients are planned like anyone. As ID increases, favor strong over esthetic: full-coverage steel crowns, posterior amalgam, and resin-modified glass ionomer.","src":"rec","why":["Things you won't have to replace.","Composites need isolation and maintenance.","Healing and hygiene limit implants.","Rarely tolerated."]},{"id":"ds-extract","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which factor favors extraction rather than preserving teeth in a patient with Down syndrome and periodontal disease?","choices":["The patient can't tolerate complex scaling and root planing","The patient won't tolerate a prosthesis","The patient has lots of spacing between the remaining teeth","The patient has excellent home care"],"answer":0,"ex":"A complicated decision: intolerance of SRP or crowding favors extraction; intolerance of prostheses or spacing favors preservation. Decide with the caregivers.","src":"rec","why":["Favors more aggressive extraction.","That favors keeping the teeth.","Spacing favors preservation.","Good home care favors keeping teeth."],"likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Periodontal disease is the most common dental condition: early, fast, probe from age 6","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-perio","ds-perio6","ds-doxy","ds-extract"]}},{"id":"ds-tf-1","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In translocation Down syndrome, the person has 46 chromosomes.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The extra 21 material rides on another chromosome (usually 14), so the count stays 46.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: 46, with one chromosome unusually long.","It's true; trisomy 21 is the 47-chromosome type."]},{"id":"ds-tf-2","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Class III malocclusion in Down syndrome usually comes from an oversized mandible.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It comes from the underdeveloped maxilla (midface hypoplasia); the mandible is usually normal.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: the maxilla is small; it isn't prognathism.","Correct: midface hypoplasia, not a big mandible."]},{"id":"ds-tf-3","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Patients with Down syndrome and NUG often lack the fetid breath and pain others report.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The institutional study noted they rarely report those signs.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True, which makes it easy to miss.","It's true; that's why you have to look."]},{"id":"ds-tf-4","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most heart defects in Down syndrome are repaired early and, once healed, usually don't need antibiotic prophylaxis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"AV canal defects, VSDs and ASDs are typically corrected early and rarely need antibiotics once healed.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True; consult the cardiologist if the repair left problems.","It's true; tetralogy of Fallot is the exception that often needs it."]},{"id":"ds-tf-5","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Solid tumors are much more common in Down syndrome, just as leukemia is.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Leukemia is 15-50 times more common, but solid tumors are not, and may even be less common.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: solid tumors aren't increased.","Correct: only leukemia is increased."]},{"id":"ds-tf-6","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Delayed eruption of both primary and permanent teeth is common in Down syndrome.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Delayed eruption, impacted canines and premolars and over-retained primary teeth are all common.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true for both dentitions."]},{"id":"ds-tf-7","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Post-extraction antibiotics in Down syndrome are strongly supported by research evidence.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"They're often recommended because healing is delayed, but it isn't evidence-driven.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: common practice, but not evidence-driven.","Correct: he said it is not evidence-driven."]},{"id":"ds-tf-8","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Down syndrome may be more common in more highly educated communities, because those families tend to start families later.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Risk rises with maternal age, and more educated families often delay having children.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: the age effect explains it.","It's true by way of maternal age."]},{"id":"ds-2s-1","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Over 90% of nondisjunction Down syndrome comes from the father's sperm.\n2. Mosaicism occurs when nondisjunction happens in the father's sperm.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Maternal over 90%; mosaicism is nondisjunction in the early embryo, not in a gamete.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: over 90% come from the mother's egg; less than 10% are paternal. Statement 2 is false, not true: in mosaicism the error happens after conception, in the embryo.","Statement 1 is false: over 90% come from the mother's egg; less than 10% are paternal. Statement 2 is false: in mosaicism the error happens after conception, in the embryo.","Statement 1 is false, not true: over 90% come from the mother's egg; less than 10% are paternal.","Statement 2 is false, not true: in mosaicism the error happens after conception, in the embryo."]},{"id":"ds-2s-2","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Atlantoaxial instability affects about 10-20% of people with Down syndrome.\n2. Its risk tends to increase with age.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"10-20% are affected, and it may decrease and normalize with age.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: it may lessen with age as the ligaments stiffen.","Statement 1 is true, not false: that's the figure he gave.","Statement 1 is true: that's the figure he gave. Statement 2 is false: it may lessen with age as the ligaments stiffen.","Statement 1 is true, not false: that's the figure he gave. Statement 2 is false, not true: it may lessen with age as the ligaments stiffen."]},{"id":"ds-2s-3","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Periodontal disease in Down syndrome is fully explained by poor brushing.\n2. Early-onset periodontal disease is seen in 60-95% of people with Down syndrome.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Perio is higher than in other disabilities, so brushing alone doesn't explain it; early onset is 60-95%.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: it's higher than in other disabilities, whose brushing is just as hard.","Statement 2 is true, not false: 60-95% have early onset.","Statement 1 is false, not true: it's higher than in other disabilities, whose brushing is just as hard. Statement 2 is true, not false: 60-95% have early onset.","Statement 1 is false: it's higher than in other disabilities, whose brushing is just as hard. Statement 2 is true: 60-95% have early onset."]},{"id":"ds-2s-4","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Macroglossia and a fissured tongue are common in Down syndrome.\n2. Spacing between teeth is common in Down syndrome.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true: a large, fissured tongue and spaced teeth.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: protect and work around the tongue. Statement 2 is true: spacing is the usual picture, crowding rare.","Statement 1 is true, not false: protect and work around the tongue. Statement 2 is true, not false: spacing is the usual picture, crowding rare.","Statement 2 is true, not false: spacing is the usual picture, crowding rare.","Statement 1 is true, not false: protect and work around the tongue."]},{"id":"ds-2s-5","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Dr. Mitchell knows many patients with Down syndrome who wear removable partial dentures well.\n2. High-functioning, cooperative patients with Down syndrome should be planned like any other patient.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"He doesn't know one who tolerates a denture or RPD, but cooperative patients are planned normally.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: he said he doesn't know of one who could tolerate a denture or RPD.","Statement 2 is true, not false: plan them like anyone; assess each person.","Statement 1 is false, not true: he said he doesn't know of one who could tolerate a denture or RPD. Statement 2 is true, not false: plan them like anyone; assess each person.","Statement 1 is false: he said he doesn't know of one who could tolerate a denture or RPD. Statement 2 is true: plan them like anyone; assess each person."]},{"id":"ds-num-1","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about Down syndrome are true?\n1. Most people with Down syndrome have severe or profound ID\n2. Life expectancy reached about 55 by the 2000s\n3. Over 90% of nondisjunction comes from the mother\n4. About 1 in 700 live births","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"About 68% have mild or moderate ID; the rest of the statements are true.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: about 68% are mild or moderate. It leaves out 4, which is true: the overall average.","It includes 1, which is false: about 68% are mild or moderate.","It leaves out 4, which is true: the overall average.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: about 68% are mild or moderate."]},{"id":"ds-num-2","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are true of the airway and swallowing in Down syndrome?\n1. Obstructive sleep apnea affects 50-80%\n2. About 60% have GERD\n3. About 58% have dysphagia\n4. Sedation is safer than in the general population","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":0,"ex":"Apnea, dysphagia and GERD are all common; sedation calls for more caution and monitoring, not less.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: the anatomy makes the airway easier to lose; use caution and often the hospital.","It includes 4, which is false: the anatomy makes the airway easier to lose; use caution and often the hospital.","It leaves out 2, which is true: 60%, so they can aspirate stomach contents.","It includes 4, which is false: the anatomy makes the airway easier to lose; use caution and often the hospital. It leaves out 1, which is true: 50-80%."]},{"id":"ds-num-3","lec":"down","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are oral findings in Down syndrome?\n1. Taurodontism\n2. Supernumerary teeth\n3. Peg or conical teeth\n4. Narrow palate","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"Missing teeth are increased, not extra ones; taurodontism, peg teeth and a narrow palate are typical.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: teeth are more often missing (laterals), not extra. It leaves out 4, which is true: a narrow palate with spacing.","It leaves out 4, which is true: a narrow palate with spacing.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: teeth are more often missing (laterals), not extra.","It leaves out 1, which is true: large pulp chambers."]},{"id":"ds-match","lec":"down","type":"match","stem":"Match each heart defect with its share of cardiac cases in Down syndrome.","pairs":[["AV canal defect","45-60%"],["Ventricular septal defect","32-35%"],["Patent ductus arteriosus","7%"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","4-6%"]],"ex":"AV canal 45-60%, VSD 32-35%, PDA 7%, tetralogy 4-6% (ASD 1-8%).","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"down","t":"Congenital heart disease (about half; AV canal) and antibiotic prophylaxis","basis":"hint","ids":["ds-heart50","ds-avcanal","ds-tof","ds-prophy","ds-match"]}},{"id":"ds-match2","lec":"down","type":"match","stem":"Match each type of Down syndrome to its share of cases.","pairs":[["Trisomy 21","94%"],["Translocation","5%"],["Mosaicism","1%"]],"ex":"Trisomy 21 94%, translocation 5%, mosaicism 1%.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"down","t":"The genetics: nondisjunction trisomy 21, translocation, mosaicism","basis":"lecture","ids":["ds-nondis","ds-pct","ds-trans","ds-mosaic","ds-match2"]}},{"id":"ds-mm-1","lec":"down","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which oral findings are more common in Down syndrome?","choices":["Class III malocclusion","Posterior crossbite","Missing lateral incisors","Taurodontism","Severe crowding","A large mandible"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Class III (small maxilla), crossbite 71%, missing laterals, microdontia, taurodontism, spacing, macroglossia.","src":"rec","why":["30-70%, from the underdeveloped maxilla.","71%.","35-43%, against 2% generally.","Bifurcation and pulp chamber floor displaced apically: large pulp chambers.","Spacing is typical; crowding is rare in Down syndrome.","The Class III comes from midface hypoplasia, not a big mandible."]},{"id":"ds-mm-2","lec":"down","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: under the current guideline, which patients need antibiotic prophylaxis?","choices":["Cyanotic congenital heart disease not fully repaired, including shunts and conduits","A defect completely repaired with prosthetic material or a device, in the first six months","Repaired congenital heart disease with a residual defect at or next to a prosthetic patch","A VSD repaired in infancy and healed with no residual defect","Mitral valve prolapse found on an EKG","Any patient with Down syndrome"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Prophylaxis is now only for the highest risk of endocarditis: unrepaired cyanotic disease, the first six months after a prosthetic repair, residual defects at a patch or device.","src":"rec","why":["The first of the three highest-risk groups.","Only for those first six months.","The third highest-risk group.","Usually no antibiotics once healed.","Common in Down syndrome (57% on EKG) but not on the highest-risk list.","It depends on the heart history, not the syndrome: consult the cardiologist."]},{"id":"ds-mm-3","lec":"down","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which describe periodontal disease in Down syndrome?","choices":["It's the most common dental condition in Down syndrome","Early onset in 60-95%, detectable as young as 6","It progresses faster than usual","It's treated with scaling and root planing, like early-onset periodontitis","Poor brushing alone explains it","Probing can wait until the teen years"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Perio is the big risk: early, fast, more than other disabilities. Prevention, early SRP, long-term chlorhexidine.","src":"rec","why":["Higher than in the general population and in other disabilities.","Probe from about age 6.","Some lose teeth to bone loss in their early teens.","SRP as for anyone, started early.","It's higher than in other disabilities, so brushing alone doesn't explain it.","Start at about 6 and look for bone loss on every film."]},{"id":"cp-def","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best describes cerebral palsy?","choices":["A permanent, non-progressive disorder of voluntary movement and posture from early brain damage","A progressive muscle disease that worsens through adolescence","A disorder that children usually outgrow","A movement disorder that can start at any age, including after an injury in adolescence or adulthood"],"answer":0,"ex":"CP is a neuromuscular disorder affecting voluntary muscle movement and posture: permanent, non-progressive, from abnormal development or brain damage before birth, during delivery or in the first years of life.","src":"rec","why":["Permanent, but it doesn't get worse.","CP is non-progressive.","It's permanent.","Later injuries can mimic CP, but CP starts before birth, at delivery or in the first years."]},{"id":"cp-prev","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"About how common is cerebral palsy?","choices":["About 1 in 303 children","About 1 in 36 children","About 1 in 700 births","About 1 in 65,000 births"],"answer":0,"ex":"CP is the most common motor disorder in children: about 1 in 303, 0.2% of live births.","src":"rec","why":["0.2% of live births; the most common motor disorder in children.","That's the 2020 autism figure.","That's Down syndrome.","That's Apert syndrome."]},{"id":"cp-weight","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"How does birth weight affect CP prevalence?","choices":["It rises sharply as birth weight falls: 59 per 1,000 under 3.3 lb vs 1.3 over 5.5 lb","It rises with heavier birth weight, especially over 9 pounds","Birth weight has no effect","Only weight over 10 lb matters"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prevalence increases as birth weight decreases (59/1,000 under 3.3 lb; 1.3/1,000 over 5.5 lb) and as gestation shortens (43/1,000 under 28 weeks; 1.3/1,000 at 37+).","src":"rec","why":["Low birth weight and prematurity raise the risk.","The reverse.","It has a large effect.","Not what he said."]},{"id":"cp-prenatal","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"When do most cases of cerebral palsy arise?","choices":["Before birth (70-80%)","During delivery (most cases)","After birth (most cases)","In adolescence"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prenatal 70-80%, neonatal 10-28%, postnatal 5-18% (closer to 5% in his view).","src":"rec","why":["Prenatal causes dominate.","Neonatal is 10-28%.","Postnatal is 5-18%.","CP isn't caused that late."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Causes: mostly prenatal, TORCH infections, hypoxia at delivery is rare","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-torch","cp-prenatal","cp-hypoxia"]}},{"id":"cp-torch","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What does the \"R\" in TORCH infections stand for?","choices":["Rubella","Rotavirus","Rabies","Respiratory syncytial virus"],"answer":0,"ex":"TORCH: Toxoplasmosis, Rubella, Cytomegalovirus, Herpes. Viral infections account for about 5% of CP. Dr. Mitchell could see the term \"popping up on a board.\"","src":"rec","why":["Toxoplasmosis, Rubella, Cytomegalovirus, Herpes.","Not in TORCH.","Not in TORCH.","Not in TORCH."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Causes: mostly prenatal, TORCH infections, hypoxia at delivery is rare","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-torch","cp-prenatal","cp-hypoxia"]}},{"id":"cp-hypoxia","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What is now understood about hypoxia at delivery as a cause of CP?","choices":["It was once thought a chief cause but is now known to be a small share","It causes most cases of CP, as long believed and still widely taught today","It never causes CP","It only causes ataxic CP"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy was once viewed as a main cause; it's now recognized as a small number of cases.","src":"rec","why":["People still overestimate it.","Most are prenatal.","It can; it's just a smaller share.","Not what he said."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Causes: mostly prenatal, TORCH infections, hypoxia at delivery is rare","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-torch","cp-prenatal","cp-hypoxia"]}},{"id":"cp-post","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is a postnatal cause of cerebral palsy?","choices":["Shaken baby syndrome","Multiple embryos","TORCH infection","Mechanical ventilation of a premature newborn"],"answer":0,"ex":"Postnatal causes: trauma (shaken baby, abusive head trauma, drowning), infections (encephalitis, meningitis), toxins and brain tumors.","src":"rec","why":["Abusive head trauma in the first years.","Prenatal.","Prenatal.","Neonatal."]},{"id":"cp-spastic","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which type of CP is most common?","choices":["Spastic","Dyskinetic","Ataxic","Mixed"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spastic CP is 70-80% of cases: cortex damage, stiff muscles, hypertonicity, hyperreflexia, contractures.","src":"rec","why":["70-80%, cerebral cortex damage.","10-20%.","5-10%.","Less common."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Types of CP and the brain area: spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-spastic","cp-dysk","cp-ataxic","cp-match","cp-loud"]}},{"id":"cp-spasticsigns","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which finding is typical of spastic CP?","choices":["Contractures that curl the hands","Slow writhing movements","Poor balance with intention tremor","Low muscle tone"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spastic CP: hypertonicity, hyperreflexia and contractures; startling stimuli like loud noises can trigger it.","src":"rec","why":["Stiff muscles and tendons lose elasticity.","That's athetosis (dyskinetic).","That's ataxic.","Spastic means increased tone."]},{"id":"cp-loud","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with spastic CP stiffens violently when the suction tip clatters onto the tray. What explains this?","choices":["Startling stimuli such as loud noises can trigger spasticity","A tonic-clonic seizure that will need EMS if it lasts","Dystonic tremor","Atlantoaxial instability"],"answer":0,"ex":"In spastic CP, startling stimuli (loud noises) can trigger spasms. Keep the operatory calm.","src":"rec","why":["Hyperreflexia: overreactive reflexes.","A startle response isn't a seizure.","That shake comes with voluntary movement.","That's Down syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Types of CP and the brain area: spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-spastic","cp-dysk","cp-ataxic","cp-match","cp-loud"]}},{"id":"cp-dysk","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Dyskinetic CP results from a lesion in which part of the brain?","choices":["Basal ganglia","Cerebellum","Cerebral cortex","Hippocampus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dyskinetic CP (10-20%): basal ganglia lesions cause athetosis, dystonic tremor and chorea.","src":"rec","why":["Involuntary, non-rhythmic movements.","That's ataxic CP.","That's spastic CP.","Not a CP type."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Types of CP and the brain area: spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-spastic","cp-dysk","cp-ataxic","cp-match","cp-loud"]}},{"id":"cp-athet","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which movement is athetosis?","choices":["Slow, uncontrolled twisting and writhing","Fast, jerky movements","A shake that appears with attempts to move","Loss of balance while walking"],"answer":0,"ex":"Athetosis is slow writhing; chorea is fast; dystonic tremor comes from opposing muscles contracting at once during movement.","src":"rec","why":["The slow dyskinetic movement.","That's chorea.","That's dystonic tremor.","That's ataxia."]},{"id":"cp-dyston","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does a dystonic tremor shake?","choices":["Opposing muscles contract at the same time during movement","The tremor happens only at rest","The cerebellum can't time the movement, so the hand overshoots","Muscles have no tone at all"],"answer":0,"ex":"Normally one side contracts while the other relaxes; in dystonic tremor both contract at once, so the hand shakes when the patient tries to use it.","src":"rec","why":["Mixed tone fights itself.","Rest tremor is Parkinson's; dystonic tremor is with movement.","That's ataxic CP.","Not dystonia."]},{"id":"cp-ataxic","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with CP walks with a walker because of poor balance and can't coordinate the brush to the tooth. Which type and brain area?","choices":["Ataxic CP, cerebellum","Spastic CP, cortex","Dyskinetic CP, basal ganglia","Spastic diplegia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Ataxic CP (5-10%): a cerebellum lesion; poor balance, poor hand-eye coordination, intention tremors.","src":"rec","why":["Lack of coordination and balance.","Spastic is stiffness.","Dyskinetic is involuntary movement.","That's leg stiffness, not coordination."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Types of CP and the brain area: spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-spastic","cp-dysk","cp-ataxic","cp-match","cp-loud"]}},{"id":"cp-hemi","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which term means involvement of the arm and leg on the same side?","choices":["Hemiplegia","Diplegia","Paraplegia","Quadriplegia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hemiplegia: one side; quadriplegia: all four (arms worse than in diplegia); diplegia: legs with minimal arms; monoplegia: one limb (rare); paraplegia: legs only; triplegia: any three.","src":"rec","why":["Half the body.","Legs, with little arm involvement.","Only the legs.","All four limbs."]},{"id":"cp-iq","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What did Dr. Mitchell call maybe his most important slide about CP?","choices":["Most people with CP have normal or near-normal intellect","Most people with CP have severe intellectual disability","CP always progresses","People with CP can't consent"],"answer":0,"ex":"30-50% have normal cognitive function, about 45% mild ID, and only about 15% severe ID. Never assume a cognitive disability because speech is hard.","src":"rec","why":["30-50% normal, about 45% mild, about 15% severe.","Only about 15%.","It's non-progressive.","Most can."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Most people with CP have normal intelligence: speak to them as peers; dysarthria isn't cognition","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-iq","cp-dysarth","cp-consent","cp-normal"]}},{"id":"cp-epi","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of people with CP have epilepsy?","choices":["About 30%","About 2.5%","About 88%","About 58%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Epilepsy is present in about 30% of people with CP; tonic-clonic seizures are most common.","src":"rec","why":["vs about 2.5% of the general population.","That's the general population.","That's dysarthria.","That's bruxism."]},{"id":"cp-seizure","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with CP starts a tonic-clonic seizure during a restoration. What do you do first?","choices":["Stop the procedure and remove everything from the mouth","Put a mouth prop in at once to protect the tongue and teeth","Hold the patient's arms and legs still","Call EMS immediately"],"answer":0,"ex":"Stop, Remove, Side, Safe, Allow (it should end in under 5 minutes), then complete urgent work and Release.","src":"rec","why":["Then turn them on their side and keep them safe.","Remove objects; don't add them.","Keep them safe; let the seizure run its course.","EMS is for status epilepticus (over 5 minutes)."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Seizures and their drugs: managing a seizure, Dilantin overgrowth, Depakote bleeding","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-seizure","cp-dilantin","cp-depakote","cp-match2","cp-status"]}},{"id":"cp-side","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Why turn a seizing patient onto their side?","choices":["To protect against aspiration if they vomit","To shorten the seizure by taking pressure off the airway","To make room for a mouth prop","To check the pulse"],"answer":0,"ex":"Lay the patient on their side so that if they vomit, they won't aspirate it.","src":"rec","why":["The \"Side\" step.","Positioning doesn't shorten it.","Nothing goes in the mouth.","Not the reason given."]},{"id":"cp-status","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which defines status epilepticus?","choices":["A seizure longer than 5 minutes, or two or more within 5 minutes without recovering between","Any seizure during dental treatment","A seizure longer than 30 seconds, or any seizure in a patient with known epilepsy","A seizure with loss of bladder control"],"answer":0,"ex":"Status epilepticus: a single seizure over five minutes, or two or more in five minutes without returning to normal. Activate EMS, monitor the airway and breathing, BLS if they collapse.","src":"rec","why":["An emergency: activate EMS.","Most end in under 5 minutes.","Normal seizures can last longer than that.","Not the definition."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Seizures and their drugs: managing a seizure, Dilantin overgrowth, Depakote bleeding","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-seizure","cp-dilantin","cp-depakote","cp-match2","cp-status"]}},{"id":"cp-afterseiz","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"After a seizure ends in the dental chair, what does Dr. Mitchell recommend?","choices":["Finish only urgent work (temporize) and let the patient go home","Finish the full planned appointment while the patient is calm and still","Start the next procedure while they're calm","Always send them to the emergency room"],"answer":0,"ex":"Once the seizure ends, temporize anything urgent, stop the appointment as soon as safely possible, and release the patient.","src":"rec","why":["They'll be exhausted.","Stop as soon as you safely can.","They'll be exhausted.","Only for status epilepticus or other emergencies."]},{"id":"cp-dilantin","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which medication is classically associated with gingival hyperplasia?","choices":["Dilantin (phenytoin)","Depakote (valproic acid)","Bisphosphonates","Glycopyrrolate"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dilantin causes gingival hyperplasia. Consult the physician about switching, keep tissues clean, and refer for resection when indicated.","src":"rec","why":["Not used much anymore; most newer drugs don't do it.","Its concern is thrombocytopenia.","Their concern is osteonecrosis.","An anticholinergic for drooling."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Seizures and their drugs: managing a seizure, Dilantin overgrowth, Depakote bleeding","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-seizure","cp-dilantin","cp-depakote","cp-match2","cp-status"]}},{"id":"cp-resect","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"In a patient with CP whose hygiene can't be controlled, when should Dilantin gingival overgrowth be sent for resection?","choices":["When there's a risk or occurrence of periodontal infection, or tissue grows over the occlusal surfaces","As soon as any overgrowth appears, before it can spread to other teeth","Never: it resolves by itself","Only after the patient turns 21"],"answer":0,"ex":"If hygiene can't be controlled, delay resection; send when infection risk rises or the tissue grows onto the occlusal surfaces and ulcerates. Modified flap, electrocautery or lasers; often with sedation.","src":"rec","why":["Otherwise it grows back quickly.","Without hygiene control it just regrows.","It doesn't.","Age isn't the trigger."]},{"id":"cp-depakote","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with CP takes Depakote (valproic acid). What should you watch for?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia and bleeding","Gingival hyperplasia","Osteonecrosis after extractions","Hypersalivation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Depakote (valproic acid) carries a risk of thrombocytopenia and bleeding tendencies.","src":"rec","why":["Plan for bleeding procedures.","That's Dilantin.","That's bisphosphonates.","Not a Depakote concern he named."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Seizures and their drugs: managing a seizure, Dilantin overgrowth, Depakote bleeding","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-seizure","cp-dilantin","cp-depakote","cp-match2","cp-status"]}},{"id":"cp-bisph","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Why might a patient with CP take a bisphosphonate, and what does it mean for extractions?","choices":["For osteoporosis or osteopenia; risk of osteonecrosis, so consult and consider the oral surgeon","For seizures; they raise the risk of bleeding during and after extractions","For drooling; risk of dry socket","For GERD; no dental concern"],"answer":0,"ex":"Bisphosphonates may be prescribed for osteoporosis or osteopenia in CP; extractions carry a risk of osteonecrosis of alveolar bone.","src":"rec","why":["Same as any patient on bisphosphonates.","That's Depakote's issue.","Not the indication.","Not the indication."]},{"id":"cp-dysph","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What is dysphagia?","choices":["Abnormal swallowing with a delayed reflex","Difficulty articulating words clearly","Overproduction of saliva","Grinding the teeth"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dysphagia leads to a sensitive gag reflex and aspiration risk: often don't lay the patient back fully, suction often, use water intermittently.","src":"rec","why":["Leads to a sensitive gag, coughing, choking and aspiration risk.","That's dysarthria.","Drooling in CP is not hypersalivation.","That's bruxism."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Drooling comes from dysphagia, not hypersalivation","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-drool","cp-droolrx","cp-dysph"]}},{"id":"cp-gerd","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"On a patient with CP who can't describe heartburn, which tooth wear suggests GERD?","choices":["A scooped-out appearance","Flat, polished facets","Notches at the cervical third from brushing","Wear only on the incisal edges"],"answer":0,"ex":"Over 50% have GERD, and erosion may be the first sign: scooped-out lesions, unlike the flat surfaces of bruxism. Refer to the physician.","src":"rec","why":["Acid erosion.","That's bruxism.","Abrasion, not GERD.","Not what he described."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"GERD erosion: control the GERD before restoring","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-gerd","cp-gerdrest"]}},{"id":"cp-gerdrest","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with CP has GERD erosion but no symptoms. When should the teeth be restored?","choices":["Not until after the GERD is resolved; they may not need it","Immediately, before more enamel is lost","Never; eroded enamel can't be restored once it's lost","Only under general anesthesia"],"answer":0,"ex":"If symptomatic, restore. If not, don't restore until after the GERD is resolved; it may never need restoration.","src":"rec","why":["Restoring sooner just fails.","Without symptoms, wait.","Symptomatic teeth may need it.","Not the point."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"GERD erosion: control the GERD before restoring","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-gerd","cp-gerdrest"]}},{"id":"cp-drool","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What causes drooling in cerebral palsy?","choices":["Dysphagia: saliva isn't swallowed, not overproduced","Hypersalivation from overactive salivary glands","Anticholinergic medications","Macroglossia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Drooling (10-40%) comes from dysphagia: they can't clear the saliva as it's made.","src":"rec","why":["Present in 10-40%.","He stressed it is not hypersalivation.","Those reduce saliva.","Tongue size is normal in CP."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Drooling comes from dysphagia, not hypersalivation","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-drool","cp-droolrx","cp-dysph"]}},{"id":"cp-droolrx","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Why are treatments that reduce saliva a last resort in CP?","choices":["The dry mouth can cause rampant caries and tooth loss, with no clear evidence of benefit","They make drooling worse","They are illegal for minors","They cause gingival hyperplasia, which is hard to keep clean"],"answer":0,"ex":"Botox, anticholinergics (scopolamine, glycopyrrolate) and surgery (parotid duct ligation, submandibular gland excision) have no clear evidence of value and can trigger rampant caries.","src":"rec","why":["Saliva protects the patient.","They reduce it, at a cost.","Not the reason.","That's Dilantin."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Drooling comes from dysphagia, not hypersalivation","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-drool","cp-droolrx","cp-dysph"]}},{"id":"cp-dysarth","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Dysarthria is present in up to 88% of people with CP. What does it tell you about cognition?","choices":["Nothing: it doesn't indicate cognitive capacity","That the patient has severe ID","That the patient can't consent and needs a caregiver to sign","That the patient is hard of hearing"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dysarthria (difficulty articulating) gives slow, poorly pronounced speech but does not indicate cognitive capacity.","src":"rec","why":["Speak to them as a peer.","Most have normal or near-normal intellect.","Most can.","Not related."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Most people with CP have normal intelligence: speak to them as peers; dysarthria isn't cognition","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-iq","cp-dysarth","cp-consent","cp-normal"]}},{"id":"cp-consent","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"An adult with CP has normal cognition but can barely speak. How can consent be obtained?","choices":["From the patient: signed, verbal and witnessed, a blink code, or a communication device","From a caregiver, since the patient can't speak clearly enough to consent","It can't be obtained, so treat under implied consent","Only through a court order"],"answer":0,"ex":"Most people with CP consent to their own care: some sign, most can give verbal consent witnessed by a second party, or use one blink for yes and looking down for no, or a device. Unless very low functioning, they can refuse.","src":"rec","why":["Most consent for themselves.","Only if the patient is low functioning.","Use a system that works for them.","Not needed."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Most people with CP have normal intelligence: speak to them as peers; dysarthria isn't cognition","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-iq","cp-dysarth","cp-consent","cp-normal"]}},{"id":"cp-face","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which facial type is common in cerebral palsy?","choices":["Long and narrow (dolichofacial), with hyperdivergent planes","Short and square (brachyfacial)","Midface hypoplasia with Class III","Severe micrognathia with a U-shaped cleft palate"],"answer":0,"ex":"Weak musculature allows excess vertical growth: a long, narrow face with palatal, occlusal and mandibular planes diverging.","src":"rec","why":["Weak muscles allow excess vertical growth.","A strong-jawed face, the opposite.","That's Down syndrome.","That's Pierre Robin."]},{"id":"cp-tongue","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What role does tongue thrust play in the open bite of CP?","choices":["It maintains the open bite once it forms","It creates the open bite","It closes the open bite over time","None: there's no tongue thrust in CP"],"answer":0,"ex":"The open bite develops from the growth pattern; the tongue slips into the space and maintains it.","src":"rec","why":["The tongue fills the space and acts as a retainer.","He said tongue thrust doesn't create it.","It keeps it open.","It's common."]},{"id":"cp-class2","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the usual malocclusion in CP?","choices":["Skeletal Class II with a retrusive mandible, flared incisors and increased overjet","Class III from midface hypoplasia, with a posterior crossbite and spacing","Class I with crowding","Class III from a large mandible"],"answer":0,"ex":"Open bite, procumbent incisors, increased overjet, mandibular retrusion and skeletal Class II.","src":"rec","why":["Plus open bite.","That's Down syndrome.","Not what he described.","Not CP."]},{"id":"cp-trauma","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Why are avulsions and incisor fractures more common in CP?","choices":["Unprotected flared incisors with overjet, plus falls from poor balance and seizures","Because their enamel is always hypoplastic and breaks easily","Because of macroglossia","Because of Dilantin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Excess overjet and procumbent incisors leave the teeth unprotected; poor coordination, balance and epilepsy add falls.","src":"rec","why":["All of it adds up.","Hypoplasia is in 25-35%, not the trauma reason.","Tongue size is normal in CP.","Not related to trauma."]},{"id":"cp-calc","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"What is true about calculus in CP?","choices":["No more calculus than usual, except in patients fed by g-tube","Heavy calculus in every patient, which causes their periodontal disease","No calculus forms in CP","Calculus is the main cause of their caries"],"answer":0,"ex":"Periodontal disease is up to 3 times more common, but calculus isn't increased: it's plaque that isn't cleaned. G-tube patients are the exception.","src":"rec","why":["Their periodontal disease is mostly plaque.","Only g-tube patients.","It forms as usual.","Not the point."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"G-tube calculus: clean buccal and lingual, leave the occlusal table","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-gtube","cp-calc"]}},{"id":"cp-gtube","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient fed by g-tube has heavy calculus on every surface, including the occlusal table. What do you do?","choices":["Remove it from the buccal and lingual, but leave it on the occlusal table","Remove it all, occlusal included, with an ultrasonic scaler","Leave it all, since they don't eat","Refer for extraction of all teeth"],"answer":0,"ex":"Patients who don't eat by mouth build heavy calculus; clean from around the gingival margins (often ultrasonic, controlling water) but leave it on the occlusal table.","src":"rec","why":["It protects the occlusal surface.","Leave the occlusal calculus.","Buccal and lingual calculus still drives perio.","No reason given for that."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"G-tube calculus: clean buccal and lingual, leave the occlusal table","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-gtube","cp-calc"]}},{"id":"cp-brux","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Bruxism is present in about 58% of people with CP. What is true of it?","choices":["Pulp exposures are rare because the wear is slow","It leaves scooped-out lesions that need restoring right away","It always needs full-coverage crowns","It's less severe with lower muscle function"],"answer":0,"ex":"Bruxism: flat wear; pulp exposures rare; more severe with decreased muscle and cognitive function.","src":"rec","why":["The pulp recedes ahead of it.","Scooped is erosion; bruxism is flat.","Only if symptomatic or problematic.","It's more severe with decreased muscle and cognitive function."]},{"id":"cp-normal","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is normal in cerebral palsy?","choices":["Number of teeth, eruption timing and tongue size","Occlusion: most have Class I with ideal overjet","Periodontal health","Enamel"],"answer":0,"ex":"Things that appear normal in CP: number of teeth, eruption times and tongue size.","src":"rec","why":["No increase in missing or extra teeth.","Open bite and Class II are common.","Up to 3x more perio.","Hypoplasia in 25-35%."],"likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Most people with CP have normal intelligence: speak to them as peers; dysarthria isn't cognition","basis":"hint","ids":["cp-iq","cp-dysarth","cp-consent","cp-normal"]}},{"id":"cp-resto","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with CP can't tolerate the chair time and impressions for a cast crown on a broken-down molar. What is a good alternative?","choices":["A stainless steel crown","A large composite without isolation","A porcelain veneer","Leave it untreated"],"answer":0,"ex":"Stainless steel crowns may be preferred over cast crowns; use fast-set alginate if taking impressions; resin only with isolation; RMGI if not in function; amalgam where function matters.","src":"rec","why":["Full coverage without the cast crown process.","Resin only with good isolation.","Not a molar solution.","Not if it needs coverage."]},{"id":"cp-noaids","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which home aid does Dr. Mitchell say does NOT help people with CP?","choices":["Waterpik","A toothbrush pushed through a tennis ball","A toothbrush lengthened with tongue blades","The Surround three-headed toothbrush"],"answer":0,"ex":"Things that don't help: Waterpik, disclosing tablets and mouth rinses (most can't rinse and spit; drooling makes disclosing messy).","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["Not proven helpful, along with disclosing tablets and rinses.","A homemade fat handle that helps.","Helps when the hand can't come close to the mouth.","He recommends it."]},{"id":"cp-wheel","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with CP arrives in a power wheelchair. Which is right?","choices":["Lock the wheels; you can treat in the chair, often at about 45 degrees","You should lift the patient into the dental chair yourself, with an assistant","Patients can't be treated in a wheelchair","Tilt the chair fully flat every time"],"answer":0,"ex":"If transferring, let the caregivers do it (lifts help). Or treat from the wheelchair: lock the wheels (a 300-lb chair can roll onto your foot), use the tilt; you may need to stay at about 45 degrees.","src":"rec","why":["Or let caregivers transfer.","Let caregivers do the transfer.","Sparks does it often.","Dysphagia often limits it to about 45 degrees."]},{"id":"cp-tf-1","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Cerebral palsy gets worse as the child grows.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"CP is non-progressive: the same limbs stay affected.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: it's permanent but non-progressive.","Correct: it doesn't progress."]},{"id":"cp-tf-2","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Drooling in CP comes from dysphagia, not from overproduction of saliva.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"They can't clear saliva as it's made.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: it's not hypersalivation.","It's true; that's why drying the mouth is the wrong target."]},{"id":"cp-tf-3","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most people with CP have severe intellectual disability.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Only about 15% have severe ID; 30-50% have normal cognition.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: most have normal or near-normal intellect.","Correct: about 15% severe."]},{"id":"cp-tf-4","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: A seizure that lasts more than five minutes is status epilepticus and needs EMS.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Over five minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery, is the emergency.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: activate EMS.","It's true."]},{"id":"cp-tf-5","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Patients with CP have more missing and supernumerary teeth than average.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The number of teeth is normal in CP.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: tooth number, eruption timing and tongue size are normal.","Correct: no increase in missing or extra teeth."]},{"id":"cp-tf-6","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Hypoxia during delivery is now understood to cause only a small share of CP.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"It used to be considered a chief cause; most CP is prenatal.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: a small number of cases.","It's true; prenatal causes are 70-80%."]},{"id":"cp-tf-7","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Periodontal disease in CP is up to three times more common, mostly because they form much more calculus.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Perio is up to 3x more common, but calculus isn't increased (except g-tube patients); it's uncleaned plaque.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Half right: the perio rate is right, but calculus isn't the reason.","Correct: calculus isn't increased."]},{"id":"cp-tf-8","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Unless they are very low functioning, people with CP have the right to refuse dental treatment.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Most have the capacity to consent, and so to refuse.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: trouble speaking doesn't take that right away.","It's true; only low-functioning patients rely on caregiver consent."]},{"id":"cp-2s-1","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Spastic CP comes from damage to the cerebellum.\n2. Ataxic CP comes from damage to the basal ganglia.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Spastic: cortex. Ataxic: cerebellum. Dyskinetic: basal ganglia.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: spastic CP is the cerebral cortex; a cerebellum lesion is ataxic CP. Statement 2 is false, not true: ataxic is the cerebellum; basal ganglia is dyskinetic.","Statement 1 is false: spastic CP is the cerebral cortex; a cerebellum lesion is ataxic CP. Statement 2 is false: ataxic is the cerebellum; basal ganglia is dyskinetic.","Statement 1 is false, not true: spastic CP is the cerebral cortex; a cerebellum lesion is ataxic CP.","Statement 2 is false, not true: ataxic is the cerebellum; basal ganglia is dyskinetic."]},{"id":"cp-2s-2","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Epilepsy is present in about 30% of people with CP.\n2. Tonic-clonic seizures are the most common type in CP.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: vs about 2.5% of everyone. Statement 2 is true: tonic-clonic is most common.","Statement 1 is true, not false: vs about 2.5% of everyone. Statement 2 is true, not false: tonic-clonic is most common.","Statement 2 is true, not false: tonic-clonic is most common.","Statement 1 is true, not false: vs about 2.5% of everyone."]},{"id":"cp-2s-3","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Depakote's main dental concern is gingival hyperplasia.\n2. Dilantin is now the most widely used anticonvulsant in CP.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Depakote's concern is thrombocytopenia; Dilantin isn't used much anymore.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Depakote's risk is thrombocytopenia and bleeding; Dilantin causes hyperplasia. Statement 2 is false, not true: Dilantin is not frequently used anymore.","Statement 1 is false: Depakote's risk is thrombocytopenia and bleeding; Dilantin causes hyperplasia. Statement 2 is false: Dilantin is not frequently used anymore.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Depakote's risk is thrombocytopenia and bleeding; Dilantin causes hyperplasia.","Statement 2 is false, not true: Dilantin is not frequently used anymore."]},{"id":"cp-2s-4","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Erosion from GERD looks scooped out, while bruxism wear looks flat.\n2. If erosion is symptomatic, it may need to be restored.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true; restore asymptomatic erosion only after the GERD is controlled.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: scooped vs flat is how to tell them apart. Statement 2 is true: symptomatic teeth may need restoring.","Statement 1 is true, not false: scooped vs flat is how to tell them apart. Statement 2 is true, not false: symptomatic teeth may need restoring.","Statement 2 is true, not false: symptomatic teeth may need restoring.","Statement 1 is true, not false: scooped vs flat is how to tell them apart."]},{"id":"cp-2s-5","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Waterpiks have been shown to help people with CP clean at home.\n2. Lengthening a toothbrush handle with tongue blades helps a patient who can't bring the hand close to the mouth.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Waterpiks haven't been shown to help; lengthened handles do.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Waterpiks are on the list of things that don't help.","Statement 2 is true, not false: extra length lets the hand stay farther from the mouth.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Waterpiks are on the list of things that don't help. Statement 2 is true, not false: extra length lets the hand stay farther from the mouth.","Statement 1 is false: Waterpiks are on the list of things that don't help. Statement 2 is true: extra length lets the hand stay farther from the mouth."]},{"id":"cp-num-1","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are prenatal causes of cerebral palsy?\n1. TORCH infections\n2. Multiple embryos (twins, triplets)\n3. Shaken baby syndrome\n4. Intracranial hemorrhage","choices":["1 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Shaken baby syndrome is postnatal; the others are prenatal.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 2, which is true: multiple pregnancies raise the risk. It leaves out 4, which is true: a prenatal cause.","It leaves out 4, which is true: a prenatal cause.","It includes 3, which is false: it happens after birth: postnatal. It leaves out 4, which is true: a prenatal cause.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: it happens after birth: postnatal."]},{"id":"cp-num-2","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are steps in managing a seizure in the dental chair?\n1. Remove everything from the mouth\n2. Turn the patient on their side\n3. Let the seizure run its course\n4. Place a bite block to protect the tongue","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"Stop, Remove, Side, Safe, Allow, Release: nothing goes into the mouth.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 3, which is true: it should end in under five minutes.","Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: you remove objects; you don't add one.","It includes 4, which is false: you remove objects; you don't add one.","It includes 4, which is false: you remove objects; you don't add one. It leaves out 3, which is true: it should end in under five minutes."]},{"id":"cp-num-3","lec":"cp","type":"mcq","stem":"Which factors raise caries risk in CP?\n1. Macroglossia\n2. Soft diet\n3. Enamel hypoplasia\n4. Anti-drooling medications","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"Tongue size is normal in CP; the other three are listed factors (with hygiene, mouth breathing and food held in the mouth).","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: tongue size is normal in CP. It leaves out 4, which is true: they dry the mouth.","It includes 1, which is false: tongue size is normal in CP. It leaves out 2, which is true: a listed factor.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: tongue size is normal in CP.","It leaves out 3, which is true: hypoplastic enamel decays faster."]},{"id":"cp-match","lec":"cp","type":"match","stem":"Match each type of CP to the brain area involved.","pairs":[["Spastic","Cerebral cortex"],["Dyskinetic","Basal ganglia"],["Ataxic","Cerebellum"]],"ex":"Spastic: cortex (70-80%). Dyskinetic: basal ganglia (10-20%). Ataxic: cerebellum (5-10%).","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Types of CP and the brain area: spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-spastic","cp-dysk","cp-ataxic","cp-match","cp-loud"]}},{"id":"cp-match2","lec":"cp","type":"match","stem":"Match each medication to its dental concern.","pairs":[["Dilantin","Gingival hyperplasia"],["Depakote","Thrombocytopenia and bleeding"],["Bisphosphonates","Osteonecrosis after extraction"],["Glycopyrrolate","Dry mouth and caries"]],"ex":"Dilantin: hyperplasia. Depakote: bleeding. Bisphosphonates: osteonecrosis. Anticholinergics for drooling: dry mouth and rampant caries.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"cp","t":"Seizures and their drugs: managing a seizure, Dilantin overgrowth, Depakote bleeding","basis":"lecture","ids":["cp-seizure","cp-dilantin","cp-depakote","cp-match2","cp-status"]}},{"id":"cp-mm-1","lec":"cp","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: a patient with CP has a seizure in the chair. Which are right?","choices":["Stop the procedure and remove everything from the mouth","Turn the patient on the side","Let the seizure run its course","Call EMS if a single seizure lasts more than five minutes","Place something between the teeth to protect the tongue","Finish the planned treatment once the patient is calm"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Stop, Remove, Side, Safe, Allow, Release. Over five minutes, or two without recovering between: status epilepticus, call EMS.","src":"rec","why":["Stop, then Remove.","So vomit isn't aspirated.","Allow: it should end in under five minutes.","That's status epilepticus: activate EMS, monitor the airway, BLS if needed.","Remove everything from the mouth instead.","Complete only urgent treatment, then release them home."]},{"id":"cp-mm-2","lec":"cp","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which causes of cerebral palsy are prenatal?","choices":["TORCH infections","Prematurity","Multiple embryos (twins, triplets)","Intracranial hemorrhage","Shaken baby syndrome","Mechanical ventilation of a premature baby"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Prenatal 70-80%, neonatal 10-28%, postnatal 5-18% (closer to 5%).","src":"rec","why":["Toxoplasmosis, rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes: about 5% of CP.","Listed under prenatal causes (70-80% of CP).","A prenatal cause.","A prenatal cause.","Postnatal: trauma in the first years.","Neonatal, with hypoxia and blood pressure regulation."]},{"id":"cp-mm-3","lec":"cp","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are usually normal in cerebral palsy?","choices":["The number of teeth","Eruption timing","Tongue size","Occlusion","Incisor position","Tooth wear"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Normal in CP: number of teeth, eruption times, tongue size.","src":"rec","why":["No more missing or extra teeth.","Normal in CP, unlike Down syndrome.","Normal: macroglossia is Down syndrome.","Open bite, kept open by tongue thrust, with Class II and more overjet.","Flared, procumbent incisors with more overjet.","Bruxism in 58%: flat wear."]},{"id":"sy-brachy","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is brachydactyly?","choices":["Short fingers","Fused fingers","Extra fingers","A permanently bent finger"],"answer":0,"ex":"-dactyly always means fingers or toes. Brachydactyly: short fingers.","src":"rec","why":["Brachy = short.","That's syndactyly.","That's polydactyly.","That's clinodactyly."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"The -dactyly terms: fingers and toes","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-brachy","sy-syndac","sy-ectro","sy-clino"]}},{"id":"sy-syndac","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"A newborn's fingers are joined together, though X-rays show the normal number of bones. What is this?","choices":["Syndactyly","Ectrodactyly","Clinodactyly","Brachydactyly"],"answer":0,"ex":"Syndactyly is fusion: the right number of bones, joined. Ectrodactyly is congenital absence of fingers or toes.","src":"rec","why":["Abnormal fusion of the bones of the hands or feet.","That's missing fingers, with bones actually absent.","That's a bent finger.","That's short fingers."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"The -dactyly terms: fingers and toes","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-brachy","sy-syndac","sy-ectro","sy-clino"]}},{"id":"sy-ectro","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is ectrodactyly?","choices":["Congenital absence of one or more fingers or toes","More than five digits on a hand or a foot","Fusion of fingers","Short fingers"],"answer":0,"ex":"Ectrodactyly: congenital absence of one or more fingers or toes.","src":"rec","why":["Bones are actually missing.","That's polydactyly.","That's syndactyly.","That's brachydactyly."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"The -dactyly terms: fingers and toes","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-brachy","sy-syndac","sy-ectro","sy-clino"]}},{"id":"sy-clino","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is clinodactyly?","choices":["Permanent deflection of one or more fingers","Short fingers","Extra fingers","Webbed fingers joined by skin along their length"],"answer":0,"ex":"Clinodactyly: a permanent bend of one or more fingers, seen in many syndromes.","src":"rec","why":["A bent finger.","That's brachydactyly.","That's polydactyly.","That's syndactyly."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"The -dactyly terms: fingers and toes","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-brachy","sy-syndac","sy-ectro","sy-clino"]}},{"id":"sy-colob","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is a coloboma?","choices":["A hole in a structure of the eye, usually the iris in these syndromes","Wide-set eyes from facial development stopping early","A dilated pupil","Crossed eyes"],"answer":0,"ex":"Coloboma: a hole in the iris, retina, choroid or optic disc; in syndromes it usually refers to the iris.","src":"rec","why":["The one a dentist can see.","That's hypertelorism.","That's mydriasis.","That's strabismus."]},{"id":"sy-miosis","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is miosis?","choices":["Constriction of the pupil","Dilation of the pupil","Misalignment of the eye","Involuntary eye movement"],"answer":0,"ex":"Miosis: contraction (constriction) of the pupil. Mydriasis: dilation.","src":"rec","why":["A small pupil.","That's mydriasis.","That's strabismus.","That's nystagmus."]},{"id":"sy-hypertel","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Hypertelorism most often describes:","choices":["Wide-set eyes","Crossed eyes","A hole in the iris","Low-set ears"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypertelorism: abnormal distance between two paired organs, usually wide-set eyes, when facial development stops before the eyes reach their normal position.","src":"rec","why":["An abnormal distance between paired organs, usually the eyes.","That's strabismus.","That's coloboma.","A Down syndrome feature, not hypertelorism."]},{"id":"sy-gloss","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is glossoptosis, and why does it matter?","choices":["A downward, retracted tongue that can cause cleft palate and block the airway","An enlarged tongue","A fissured tongue","A tongue thrust that keeps an open bite open after it forms"],"answer":0,"ex":"Glossoptosis: the tongue is displaced downward and retracted; it can block palatal fusion (cleft palate) and the airway.","src":"rec","why":["A feature of Pierre Robin and Down syndrome.","That's macroglossia.","A Down syndrome finding, not glossoptosis.","That's CP."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Pierre Robin sequence: the airway is the big issue","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-pr","sy-prcleft","sy-gloss","sy-prtrach"]}},{"id":"sy-kypho","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient's spine curves with convexity backward, bending them forward. What is it, and what do you do in the chair?","choices":["Kyphosis; support the head with pillows because it won't reach the headrest","Scoliosis; recline the chair fully","Meningocele; avoid the chair","Kyphosis; lay the patient flat so the spine straightens against the chair"],"answer":0,"ex":"Scoliosis is sideways (C- or S-shaped); kyphosis is a backward convexity. Especially with kyphosis, support the head with pillows.","src":"rec","why":["The headrest sits too low for them.","Scoliosis curves sideways.","Not the right term or plan.","They can't comfortably get the head back."]},{"id":"sy-scolio","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"In S-shaped scoliosis, where does the head end up compared with C-shaped scoliosis?","choices":["Off to the side, rather than near the midline","Exactly at the midline in both, since the curves cancel out","Bent far forward","Tilted backward"],"answer":0,"ex":"C-shaped: the spine curves and straightens, so the head ends near its normal position. S-shaped: it doesn't return to midline, so the head is off to the side.","src":"rec","why":["S doesn't make it back to midline.","Only the C-shape ends near the midline.","That's kyphosis.","Not described."]},{"id":"sy-mening","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"How does a meningomyelocele differ from a meningocele?","choices":["A meningomyelocele includes part of the spinal cord; a meningocele only the meninges","A meningocele includes part of the spinal cord; a meningomyelocele only the meninges","They are the same thing","A meningocele is in the skull only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Meningomyelocele: meninges and spinal cord herniate (spina bifida); meningocele: only the meninges with fluid.","src":"rec","why":["Both are herniations through the spine.","Reversed.","They differ in what herniates.","Both involve the spinal column here."]},{"id":"sy-dolicho","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which describes dolichocephaly?","choices":["A long, narrow face with hyperdivergent palatal, occlusal and mandibular planes","A short, square face with a flat jaw line and nearly parallel planes","A flat midface with Class III","A small head"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dolichocephaly: long, narrow face; the three planes diverge. Brachycephaly: short, square face with nearly parallel planes.","src":"rec","why":["The child keeps growing longer.","That's brachycephaly.","That's midface hypoplasia.","Microcephaly, not dolichocephaly."]},{"id":"sy-fxgene","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What causes fragile X syndrome?","choices":["Repeats of part of the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome","Deletion of the paternal genes on chromosome 15","An HPRT1 mutation","An extra chromosome 21"],"answer":0,"ex":"A small part of the FMR1 gene's code is repeated on a fragile area of the X chromosome; the more repeats, the more likely the problems.","src":"rec","why":["More repeats, more likely symptoms.","That's Prader-Willi.","That's Lesch-Nyhan.","That's Down syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Prader-Willi and fragile X: causes and hallmarks","basis":"lecture","ids":["sy-pwhall","sy-pwcause","sy-fxgene","sy-fxboys","sy-match"]}},{"id":"sy-fxboys","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is fragile X more common and more severe in boys?","choices":["They have only one X, so an affected X shows fully (a dosing effect)","The FMR1 gene sits on the Y chromosome, which only boys have","Girls can't inherit it","Boys have two X chromosomes"],"answer":0,"ex":"Males have one X: if it's affected, there's full penetrance. Females have two; if the affected one is inactivated they're near carriers, and if the healthy one is turned off they can show the disease.","src":"rec","why":["Girls may have the healthy X active.","It's on the X.","They can, and some show it.","Boys are XY."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Prader-Willi and fragile X: causes and hallmarks","basis":"lecture","ids":["sy-pwhall","sy-pwcause","sy-fxgene","sy-fxboys","sy-match"]}},{"id":"sy-fxface","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which facial feature is typical of fragile X?","choices":["A long face with a prominent jaw","A smooth philtrum and thin upper lip","Thick eyebrows meeting in the middle","Severe micrognathia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Fragile X: delayed walking, hand flapping, hyperactivity, mental and speech delay, poor eye contact, a long face with a prominent jaw, flat feet. Some consider it part of the autism spectrum.","src":"rec","why":["Billy the Kid's face prompted the theory.","That's fetal alcohol syndrome.","That's Cornelia de Lange.","That's Pierre Robin."]},{"id":"sy-pwcause","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"In about 70% of Prader-Willi cases, what is the genetic problem?","choices":["The critical paternal genes on chromosome 15 are deleted, and the maternal copy is switched off","The maternal copy of chromosome 15 is deleted, and the paternal copy is switched off","An extra chromosome 15","Repeats in the FMR1 gene"],"answer":0,"ex":"Normally only the paternal genes on 15 are on and the maternal copy is non-functional. In 70% of Prader-Willi, the paternal genes are deleted. Most are new mutations.","src":"rec","why":["The maternal copy is there but not turned on.","Normally only the paternal copy is active.","Not a trisomy.","That's fragile X."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Prader-Willi and fragile X: causes and hallmarks","basis":"lecture","ids":["sy-pwhall","sy-pwcause","sy-fxgene","sy-fxboys","sy-match"]}},{"id":"sy-pwhall","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the hallmark behavior of Prader-Willi syndrome?","choices":["An insatiable appetite, leading to obesity","Self-biting of the lips and fingers","Hand flapping","Passive, gentle behavior"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prader-Willi: insatiable appetite and obesity, mild to moderate ID, temper outbursts, stubbornness, compulsive behavior, and possible insensitivity to pain.","src":"rec","why":["Families may lock the refrigerator.","That's Lesch-Nyhan.","Fragile X and autism.","Down syndrome tends to be passive; Prader-Willi has outbursts."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Prader-Willi and fragile X: causes and hallmarks","basis":"lecture","ids":["sy-pwhall","sy-pwcause","sy-fxgene","sy-fxboys","sy-match"]}},{"id":"sy-pwpain","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Why can insensitivity to pain in Prader-Willi be a diagnostic problem?","choices":["A tooth that would hurt anyone else may never be reported","Local anesthetic doesn't work in them, so you can't numb a tooth","They overreport pain to get food","It causes rampant caries"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insensitivity to pain can make procedures easier but diagnosis harder: a painful tooth may go unreported.","src":"rec","why":["Easier procedures, harder diagnosis.","Not what he said.","Not described.","Caries comes from saliva and eating."]},{"id":"sy-pwdental","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which dental concern is linked to Prader-Willi syndrome?","choices":["Thick, scant saliva leading to heavy calculus, caries and gingivitis","Widely spaced microdont teeth","Self-injury of the lips that may require full-mouth extractions","Gingival fibromas from tumors"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prader-Willi: obesity complicates sedation; outbursts; decreased, viscous saliva (heavy calculus, caries, gingivitis); high-arched palate.","src":"rec","why":["Overeating adds to the caries.","That's Cornelia de Lange.","That's Lesch-Nyhan.","That's tuberous sclerosis."]},{"id":"sy-pwsed","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Why may a patient with Prader-Willi be considered for general anesthesia rather than deep sedation?","choices":["Obesity makes sedation and the airway risky","They are allergic to sedatives","They are insensitive to sedatives","Their seizures prevent sedation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Obesity creates a challenge with sedation; general anesthesia may be safer, with careful airway attention.","src":"rec","why":["Plan around the airway.","Not what he said.","Not what he said.","Not the reason."]},{"id":"sy-ts","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What determines a patient's findings in tuberous sclerosis?","choices":["Where the benign tumors grow","How many FMR1 repeats they have","The mother's age","How early the skull bones fused"],"answer":0,"ex":"Tuberous sclerosis: benign tumors throughout the body. Brain: seizures, ADHD, cognitive disability (seizure drugs can cause gingival overgrowth). Kidney: impaired function (local anesthetic). Skin: light patches, acne-like growths. Mouth: weak enamel, gingival fibromas.","src":"rec","why":["Brain, kidney, skin or jaws.","That's fragile X.","That's Down syndrome.","That's Apert."]},{"id":"sy-tskidney","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with tuberous sclerosis has tumors in the kidneys. What dental issue might this raise?","choices":["Processing of local anesthetics","Gingival hyperplasia","Weakened enamel","Obesity and sedation risk"],"answer":0,"ex":"If the tumors are in the kidneys, function may be impaired, affecting how the body processes local anesthetics.","src":"rec","why":["Impaired kidney function changes drug handling.","That comes from seizure drugs (brain tumors).","That comes from tumors in the jaws during tooth formation.","That's Prader-Willi."]},{"id":"sy-apert","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the core problem in Apert syndrome?","choices":["Craniosynostosis: skull and facial bones fuse too early","An extra chromosome 21","Alcohol exposure during the pregnancy, an environmental cause","Overproduction of uric acid"],"answer":0,"ex":"Apert: early fusion of skull bones (craniosynostosis) giving dolichocephaly, hypertelorism and midface deficiency, plus syndactyly; about 1 in 65,000 births.","src":"rec","why":["Almost always a new mutation.","That's Down syndrome.","That's FAS.","That's Lesch-Nyhan."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Midface-deficient patients (Apert) struggle with the airway","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-apertair","sy-apert","sy-apertc3","sy-apertoral"]}},{"id":"sy-apertoral","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which oral features are typical of Apert syndrome?","choices":["Severe crowding, high-arched palate, anterior open bite and Class III","Widely spaced microdont teeth","Class II with a retrusive mandible and flared incisors","Normal occlusion with heavy calculus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Apert: severe crowding, high-arched palate, anterior open bite (diverging planes), midface hypoplasia with Class III, delayed eruption. Orthodontic management is often the hardest part.","src":"rec","why":["With delayed eruption.","That's Cornelia de Lange.","That's CP.","Not Apert."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Midface-deficient patients (Apert) struggle with the airway","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-apertair","sy-apert","sy-apertc3","sy-apertoral"]}},{"id":"sy-apertc3","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Why do people with Apert syndrome tend to have Class III malocclusion?","choices":["The maxilla is deficient (midface hypoplasia)","The mandible grows too large (true prognathism)","Tongue thrust","Missing lower incisors"],"answer":0,"ex":"As in Down syndrome, Class III in Apert comes from maxillary deficiency.","src":"rec","why":["Not a large mandible.","He stressed it's the maxilla.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason given."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Midface-deficient patients (Apert) struggle with the airway","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-apertair","sy-apert","sy-apertc3","sy-apertoral"]}},{"id":"sy-apertair","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which general problem is common in Apert syndrome, as in other midface-deficient patients?","choices":["Airway problems, including obstructive sleep apnea","An insatiable appetite that leads to obesity","Self-biting","Atlantoaxial instability"],"answer":0,"ex":"Midface-deficient patients struggle with the airway: sleep apnea, ear and sinus infections, hearing loss.","src":"rec","why":["Midface deficiency narrows the airway.","That's Prader-Willi.","That's Lesch-Nyhan.","That's Down syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Midface-deficient patients (Apert) struggle with the airway","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-apertair","sy-apert","sy-apertc3","sy-apertoral"]}},{"id":"sy-ln","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What causes Lesch-Nyhan syndrome?","choices":["An HPRT1 gene mutation that overproduces uric acid","A deletion of the paternal genes on chromosome 15","Early fusion of skull bones","Repeats in FMR1"],"answer":0,"ex":"Lesch-Nyhan: HPRT1 mutation, uric acid overproduction, involuntary movements, usually can't walk, and severe self-injury; almost exclusively males.","src":"rec","why":["Neurologic and behavioral effects.","That's Prader-Willi.","That's Apert.","That's fragile X."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Lesch-Nyhan: extracting healthy teeth to stop self-injury","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-lnext","sy-ln","sy-lnsex"]}},{"id":"sy-lnsex","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which syndrome occurs almost exclusively in males?","choices":["Lesch-Nyhan","Prader-Willi","Apert","Pierre Robin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Lesch-Nyhan is almost exclusively male; fragile X leans heavily male but girls can show it.","src":"rec","why":["Almost only males.","Not described as male-only.","Not sex-linked.","Not sex-linked."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Lesch-Nyhan: extracting healthy teeth to stop self-injury","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-lnext","sy-ln","sy-lnsex"]}},{"id":"sy-lnext","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What did Dr. Mitchell call the most important point about Lesch-Nyhan?","choices":["Full-mouth extraction of healthy teeth may be the best treatment to prevent disfigurement","Oral appliances solve the self-biting for life once the patient adapts to them","The self-injury fades by adulthood","Extractions are always overtreatment"],"answer":0,"ex":"Self-biting of lips and fingers can be devastating and lifelong. Removing the teeth is an ethical decision made with the family, but it may be the best treatment.","src":"rec","why":["Not doing harm, though aggressive.","They're hard to maintain for life.","It never goes away.","Case reports show failure to extract led to disfigurement."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Lesch-Nyhan: extracting healthy teeth to stop self-injury","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-lnext","sy-ln","sy-lnsex"]}},{"id":"sy-pr","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the main concern when treating a patient with Pierre Robin sequence?","choices":["Airway management","Severe intellectual disability","Self-injury","Bleeding from thrombocytopenia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pierre Robin: severe micrognathia/retrognathia, glossoptosis and a U-shaped cleft palate cause respiratory difficulty; airway management is the big issue.","src":"rec","why":["Severe cases treated in the OR, intubated.","Cognition is usually normal.","That's Lesch-Nyhan.","Not a Pierre Robin feature."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Pierre Robin sequence: the airway is the big issue","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-pr","sy-prcleft","sy-gloss","sy-prtrach"]}},{"id":"sy-prcleft","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What shape is the cleft palate in Pierre Robin sequence?","choices":["U-shaped","V-shaped","Unilateral lip only","There is no cleft"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pierre Robin: hypoplastic mandible, glossoptosis, and a U-shaped cleft palate.","src":"rec","why":["From the tongue impacted in the palate.","He described it as U-shaped.","Not the description.","A cleft is a defining feature."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Pierre Robin sequence: the airway is the big issue","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-pr","sy-prcleft","sy-gloss","sy-prtrach"]}},{"id":"sy-prtrach","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with Pierre Robin sequence has a tracheotomy. How does that affect routine dental work?","choices":["It makes it simpler, as long as the tracheotomy is kept clear","It rules out any dental treatment","It means every visit must be done in the OR under general anesthesia","It requires a papoose board"],"answer":0,"ex":"With a tracheotomy kept clear, the patient breathes through it and work in the mouth is much less difficult.","src":"rec","why":["They breathe through the tube.","Routine care can be done.","Only severe cases.","Not related."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Pierre Robin sequence: the airway is the big issue","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-pr","sy-prcleft","sy-gloss","sy-prtrach"]}},{"id":"sy-fas","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which facial features suggest fetal alcohol syndrome?","choices":["Smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, small jaw","Long face with a prominent jaw","Thick eyebrows with a short upturned nose","Wide-set eyes with webbed hands"],"answer":0,"ex":"FAS: small head, low nasal bridge, epicanthal folds, small eye openings, short nose, flat midface, smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, mild micrognathia; cleft palate in some.","src":"rec","why":["Plus a small head and short nose.","That's fragile X.","That's Cornelia de Lange.","That's Apert."]},{"id":"sy-fasbeh","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is true of the behavior and intellect of people with fetal alcohol syndrome?","choices":["Usually mild to moderate: lack of focus, poor judgment, mood swings","Always profound ID, so most live in institutions","Self-injury is typical","An insatiable appetite"],"answer":0,"ex":"FAS behavior: hyperactivity, lack of focus, poor judgment, learning disabilities, mood swings; usually mild to moderate, so many are integrated and some undiagnosed.","src":"rec","why":["Many live in society; some are never diagnosed.","Usually not profound.","That's Lesch-Nyhan.","That's Prader-Willi."]},{"id":"sy-fasenv","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which syndrome in the lecture is environmental rather than genetic?","choices":["Fetal alcohol syndrome","Apert syndrome","Fragile X","Lesch-Nyhan"],"answer":0,"ex":"Fetal alcohol syndrome is environmental: alcohol consumption during pregnancy affects the developing child.","src":"rec","why":["Caused by alcohol in pregnancy.","A genetic new mutation.","Genetic.","Genetic."]},{"id":"sy-cdl","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which syndrome has thick eyebrows, moderate to severe ID, and widely spaced, small teeth?","choices":["Cornelia de Lange","Prader-Willi","Fragile X","Pierre Robin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cornelia de Lange: slow growth, short stature, moderate to severe ID, limb anomalies, thick eyebrows, short upturned nose, long smooth philtrum, thin upper lip; micrognathia, cleft palate 20%, delayed eruption, widely spaced teeth, microdontia; severe behavior problems.","src":"rec","why":["With cleft palate in 20% and delayed eruption.","Mild to moderate ID; obesity.","Long face, prominent jaw.","Usually normal cognition."]},{"id":"sy-cdlbeh","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Why do patients with Cornelia de Lange more often end up treated in institutional or sedation settings?","choices":["They are more profoundly affected and often combative","They have the most heart defects of any syndrome in the lecture","Their teeth are crowded","They need airway surgery"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cornelia de Lange is more profoundly disabling than most syndromes here, and patients tend to be more combative and difficult.","src":"rec","why":["Severe behavior problems for dental care.","Not the reason given.","They're widely spaced.","That's Pierre Robin."]},{"id":"sy-lookup","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"What skill did Dr. Mitchell call incredibly important for working with syndromes?","choices":["Looking things up quickly in a good reference","Memorizing every syndrome's gene","Diagnosing syndromes for the family","Ordering genetic tests"],"answer":0,"ex":"Keep the reference reading handy; being able to look things up quickly is an incredibly important skill.","src":"rec","why":["Nobody remembers every syndrome.","He said you won't remember them all.","He wouldn't diagnose FAS to the patient.","Not what he said."],"likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Look it up: quick access to good resources","basis":"hint","ids":["sy-lookup"]}},{"id":"sy-tf-1","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In syndactyly, the fingers have the normal number of bones but are joined together.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Syndactyly is fusion; ectrodactyly is the one with bones missing.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True: fused, not missing.","It's true; missing digits would be ectrodactyly."]},{"id":"sy-tf-2","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Mydriasis means a constricted pupil.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Mydriasis is dilation; miosis is constriction.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: that's miosis.","Correct: mydriasis is a dilated pupil."]},{"id":"sy-tf-3","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most cases of Prader-Willi syndrome are inherited from a parent who has it.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Most are new mutations, so there's usually no family pattern.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: most are new mutations.","Correct: usually not inherited."]},{"id":"sy-tf-4","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Girls can show fragile X syndrome if the healthy X chromosome is the one inactivated.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"With the affected X active, a girl can show the disease.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true; fragile X is more common in boys but not limited to them."]},{"id":"sy-tf-5","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: People with Pierre Robin sequence usually have normal cognitive function.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Communication may be hard anatomically, but cognition is usually normal.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true; the problem is the airway."]},{"id":"sy-tf-6","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Brachycephaly describes a long, narrow face.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Brachycephaly is a short, square face; dolichocephaly is long and narrow.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: brachy = short.","Correct."]},{"id":"sy-tf-7","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In Cornelia de Lange syndrome, teeth are often widely spaced despite microdontia.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Widely spaced teeth with microdontia, delayed eruption and clefts in 20%.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"sy-tf-8","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In tuberous sclerosis, the tumors are malignant and spread through the body.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"They are benign tumors; the problems depend on where they grow.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: benign tumors.","Correct: benign."]},{"id":"sy-2s-1","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Apert syndrome is almost always a new mutation.\n2. Apert syndrome typically causes widely spaced teeth.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"New mutation, yes; but crowding, not spacing, is typical.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: severe crowding is typical.","Statement 1 is true, not false: there's usually no family history.","Statement 1 is true: there's usually no family history. Statement 2 is false: severe crowding is typical.","Statement 1 is true, not false: there's usually no family history. Statement 2 is false, not true: severe crowding is typical."]},{"id":"sy-2s-2","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Prader-Willi syndrome tends to cause passive, gentle behavior like Down syndrome.\n2. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome is almost exclusively seen in males.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Prader-Willi has outbursts and stubbornness; Lesch-Nyhan is nearly all male.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Prader-Willi brings temper outbursts and compulsive behavior.","Statement 2 is true, not false: almost exclusively male.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Prader-Willi brings temper outbursts and compulsive behavior. Statement 2 is true, not false: almost exclusively male.","Statement 1 is false: Prader-Willi brings temper outbursts and compulsive behavior. Statement 2 is true: almost exclusively male."]},{"id":"sy-2s-3","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Glossoptosis can cause a cleft palate by blocking palatal fusion.\n2. Glossoptosis is a feature of the Pierre Robin sequence.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both true.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: the retracted tongue gets in the way of fusion. Statement 2 is true: with micrognathia and a U-shaped cleft.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the retracted tongue gets in the way of fusion. Statement 2 is true, not false: with micrognathia and a U-shaped cleft.","Statement 2 is true, not false: with micrognathia and a U-shaped cleft.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the retracted tongue gets in the way of fusion."]},{"id":"sy-2s-4","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Fetal alcohol syndrome usually causes profound intellectual disability.\n2. Fragile X is caused by a deletion on chromosome 15.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"FAS is usually mild to moderate; the chromosome 15 deletion is Prader-Willi.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: usually mild to moderate. Statement 2 is false, not true: fragile X is FMR1 repeats on the X chromosome.","Statement 1 is false: usually mild to moderate. Statement 2 is false: fragile X is FMR1 repeats on the X chromosome.","Statement 1 is false, not true: usually mild to moderate.","Statement 2 is false, not true: fragile X is FMR1 repeats on the X chromosome."]},{"id":"sy-2s-5","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Kyphosis is a sideways curve of the spine.\n2. A meningocele involves herniation of the meninges only.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Meningocele: meninges only. Kyphosis: backward convexity; sideways is scoliosis.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: sideways is scoliosis.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the spinal cord stays put.","Statement 1 is false, not true: sideways is scoliosis. Statement 2 is true, not false: the spinal cord stays put.","Statement 1 is false: sideways is scoliosis. Statement 2 is true: the spinal cord stays put."]},{"id":"sy-num-1","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are dental concerns in Prader-Willi syndrome?\n1. Heavy calculus from thick, scant saliva\n2. Obesity complicating sedation\n3. High-arched palate\n4. Gingival fibromas from tumors","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":0,"ex":"Gingival fibromas come from tuberous sclerosis; the rest are Prader-Willi.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: that's tuberous sclerosis.","It includes 4, which is false: that's tuberous sclerosis.","It leaves out 2, which is true: sedation is a challenge.","It includes 4, which is false: that's tuberous sclerosis. It leaves out 1, which is true: decreased, viscous saliva."]},{"id":"sy-num-2","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are features of Apert syndrome?\n1. Craniosynostosis\n2. Syndactyly\n3. Insatiable appetite\n4. Hypertelorism","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4","1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"Appetite belongs to Prader-Willi; the others are Apert.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: wide-set eyes.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: that's Prader-Willi.","It leaves out 2, which is true: webbed, mitten-like hands and feet.","It includes 3, which is false: that's Prader-Willi. It leaves out 1, which is true: early skull fusion."]},{"id":"sy-num-3","lec":"syndromes","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are true of fetal alcohol syndrome?\n1. Some people with it are never diagnosed\n2. It is usually profound intellectual disability\n3. It is caused by alcohol in pregnancy\n4. It produces a smooth philtrum and thin upper lip","choices":["1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Usually mild to moderate, not profound; the rest are true.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: usually mild to moderate.","It leaves out 3, which is true: it's environmental.","It includes 2, which is false: usually mild to moderate. It leaves out 1, which is true: mild cases may go undiagnosed.","It leaves out 1, which is true: mild cases may go undiagnosed."]},{"id":"sy-match","lec":"syndromes","type":"match","stem":"Match each syndrome to its cause.","pairs":[["Fragile X","FMR1 repeats on the X chromosome"],["Prader-Willi","Deleted paternal genes on chromosome 15"],["Lesch-Nyhan","HPRT1 mutation, excess uric acid"],["Apert","Early fusion of skull bones"],["Fetal alcohol syndrome","Alcohol in pregnancy"]],"ex":"Fragile X: FMR1. Prader-Willi: 15q. Lesch-Nyhan: HPRT1. Apert: craniosynostosis. FAS: environmental.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"syndromes","t":"Prader-Willi and fragile X: causes and hallmarks","basis":"lecture","ids":["sy-pwhall","sy-pwcause","sy-fxgene","sy-fxboys","sy-match"]}},{"id":"sy-match2","lec":"syndromes","type":"match","stem":"Match each term to its meaning.","pairs":[["Polydactyly","Extra fingers or toes"],["Ectrodactyly","Missing fingers or toes"],["Clinodactyly","A permanently bent finger"],["Brachydactyly","Short fingers"]],"ex":"Poly = extra, ectro = absent, clino = bent, brachy = short.","src":"rec"},{"id":"sy-mm-1","lec":"syndromes","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are dental concerns in Prader-Willi syndrome?","choices":["Decreased, viscous saliva with heavy calculus","More caries","A high-arched palate","Sedation risk from obesity","Self-biting of the lips that may need full-mouth extractions","Gingival fibromas from benign tumors"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Prader-Willi: obesity (sedation), outbursts, insensitive to pain, thick saliva (calculus, caries, gingivitis), high palate.","src":"rec","why":["Thick saliva: calculus, caries and gingivitis.","The saliva, and overeating adds to it.","On the list of dental concerns.","General anesthesia may be safer than deep sedation; watch the airway.","That's Lesch-Nyhan syndrome.","That's tuberous sclerosis."]},{"id":"sy-mm-2","lec":"syndromes","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are features of Apert syndrome?","choices":["Craniosynostosis","Syndactyly","Midface hypoplasia with Class III malocclusion","Usually inherited from a parent","A U-shaped cleft palate from glossoptosis","Brachycephaly"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Apert: craniosynostosis, dolichocephaly, hypertelorism, midface deficiency, syndactyly, sleep apnea; crowding, high palate, open bite, Class III, delayed eruption.","src":"rec","why":["The skull and facial bones fuse too early.","Webbed or mitten-like hands and feet.","A deficient maxilla, not a big mandible.","Almost always a new mutation.","That's the Pierre Robin sequence.","Apert gives dolichocephaly; brachycephaly is less common in syndromes."]},{"id":"sy-mm-3","lec":"syndromes","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which terms are matched with the right meaning?","choices":["Ectrodactyly: one or more fingers or toes congenitally absent","Hypertelorism: an abnormal distance between paired organs, usually wide-set eyes","Glossoptosis: an enlarged tongue","Kyphosis: the spine curves sideways","Miosis: a dilated pupil","Syndactyly: more than five digits"],"correct":[0,1],"ex":"Know the -dactylies, the eye terms, glossoptosis, scoliosis vs kyphosis, and dolichocephaly vs brachycephaly.","src":"rec","why":["The bones are actually missing.","Facial development stopped before the eyes moved to the midline.","An enlarged tongue is macroglossia; glossoptosis is a tongue displaced downward and retracted.","Sideways is scoliosis; kyphosis curves with convexity backward.","Miosis is constricted; mydriasis is dilated.","Syndactyly is fused digits; more than five is polydactyly."]},{"id":"au-cdc","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How does the CDC define autism spectrum disorders?","choices":["Developmental disabilities that can cause significant social, communication and behavioral challenges","Psychiatric illnesses that come and go over a lifetime, like depression","Intellectual disabilities in every case","Motor disorders of voluntary movement"],"answer":0,"ex":"CDC: a group of developmental disabilities that can cause significant social, communication and behavioral challenges; people with ASDs handle information in their brain differently.","src":"module","why":["People with ASD handle information differently.","That sounds like mental illness.","Many have no intellectual disability.","That's CP."]},{"id":"au-prev","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What did the CDC's 2020 numbers report for autism?","choices":["1 in 36 children (2.8%)","1 in 68","1 in 88","1 in 303"],"answer":0,"ex":"CDC 2012: 1 in 68. CDC 2020: 1 in 36 (2.8%), almost doubling in 8 years, only partly explained by better diagnosis.","src":"module","why":["Almost double 2012's 1 in 68.","That was 2012.","The figure in the TED talk.","That's cerebral palsy."]},{"id":"au-boys","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How much more likely is autism in boys than girls?","choices":["About four times","About the same","About twice","Girls are more likely"],"answer":0,"ex":"Autism is four times more likely in boys than girls, for reasons not understood.","src":"module","why":["About 1 in 23 boys vs 1 in 88 girls in the module.","Boys are much more often affected.","The figure is four times.","Reversed."]},{"id":"au-vacc","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What does the TED talk say about vaccines and autism?","choices":["Vaccines do not cause autism; the original study was fraudulent and retracted","Thimerosal is a proven cause","The question is still open, and thimerosal is still under study as a cause","Vaccines cause about 25% of cases"],"answer":0,"ex":"Vaccines do not cause autism. The Lancet retracted the fraudulent study; the IOM and CDC found no credible evidence; removing thimerosal didn't change prevalence.","src":"module","why":["Its author lost his medical license.","Removing it didn't change prevalence.","The IOM and CDC found no credible evidence.","Not true."]},{"id":"au-ident","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the autism concordance rate in identical twins?","choices":["77%","100%","31%","Under 10%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Identical twins 77%, fraternal twins 31%, and ordinary siblings lower still. Genetics plays a larger role than in cancer, heart disease or diabetes.","src":"module","why":["Not 100%: genes don't account for everything.","It isn't 100%.","That's fraternal twins.","Too low."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Twins and siblings: identical 77%, fraternal 31%","basis":"hint","ids":["au-ident","au-frat"]}},{"id":"au-frat","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why do fraternal twins have a higher concordance than ordinary siblings, though both share 50% of their genes?","choices":["Fraternal twins also share the same intrauterine environment","Fraternal twins share 100% of their genes, like identical twins","Siblings are usually different sexes","It's random chance"],"answer":0,"ex":"Fraternal twins and siblings both share 50% of their DNA, but twins also share the womb, suggesting common exposures.","src":"module","why":["Suggesting shared exposures matter.","That's identical twins.","Not the explanation.","The talk pointed to the shared womb."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Twins and siblings: identical 77%, fraternal 31%","basis":"hint","ids":["au-ident","au-frat"]}},{"id":"au-single","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"In the Simons Foundation study of 2,600 families without a family history, about how many cases traced to a single powerful genetic factor?","choices":["About 25%","About 75%","About 5%","All of them"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 25% had one powerful genetic change, often a new (de novo) mutation not inherited from either parent; 200-400 genes can cause autism.","src":"module","why":["75% are still unexplained.","That's the unexplained share.","Too low.","No."]},{"id":"au-denovo","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How can autism be genetic without running in the family?","choices":["A new mutation in the egg or sperm, not passed down through generations","Through vaccines","It can't; genetic always means inherited from a parent who carries it","Through the father's age only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Some genetic changes start brand new in the child, present at conception but not inherited.","src":"module","why":["De novo mutations.","Vaccines don't cause autism.","The talk said otherwise.","Paternal age is an association, not the mechanism."]},{"id":"au-assoc","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is an association with autism named in the TED talk?","choices":["Valproic acid taken during pregnancy","Thimerosal in vaccines","Advanced maternal age","Fluoride in drinking water"],"answer":0,"ex":"Associations: advanced paternal age; exposures in pregnancy such as valproic acid and some infections.","src":"module","why":["An exposure while the fetal brain develops.","Removing it had no effect.","He named advanced paternal age.","Not mentioned."]},{"id":"au-eye","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What did Ami Klin's eye-tracking studies show in infants who later developed autism?","choices":["They looked at the mouth, nose or elsewhere instead of the eyes","They made more eye contact than other infants, staring at the singer's eyes","They couldn't track moving objects","Their eyes moved involuntarily"],"answer":0,"ex":"Infants on the spectrum don't focus on the socially interactive parts of a scene (the eyes), so their brains fail to learn the usual social cues.","src":"module","why":["They miss social cues.","The reverse.","Not the finding.","That's nystagmus."]},{"id":"au-onset","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"When does ASD begin?","choices":["Before age 3","At puberty","Only at birth, never later","After age 5"],"answer":0,"ex":"ASD begins before age 3. Some show hints in the first months; some develop typically until 18-24 months and then stop gaining or lose skills.","src":"module","why":["It lasts for life, though symptoms improve.","Far too late.","Some develop typically until 18-24 months, then regress.","Too late."]},{"id":"au-signs","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is an early sign of ASD listed in the module?","choices":["Not pointing at objects of interest by 14 months","Walking unusually early, by about 9 months of age","Strong interest in faces","Liking every change in routine"],"answer":0,"ex":"Signs: no response to name by 12 months, no pointing by 14 months, no pretend play by 18 months, poor eye contact, echolalia, upset by minor changes, flapping or rocking, unusual sensory reactions, no sense of danger.","src":"module","why":["Along with not responding to their name by 12 months.","Not a sign.","They tend to avoid eye contact.","They get upset by minor changes."]},{"id":"au-echo","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What is echolalia?","choices":["Repeating words or phrases over and over","Flapping the hands when excited or upset","Painful sensitivity to sound","Avoiding eye contact"],"answer":0,"ex":"Echolalia: repeating words or phrases over and over.","src":"module","why":["A common ASD feature.","That's a stim.","Sensory, not echolalia.","A separate feature."]},{"id":"au-stim","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with autism is rocking and humming during a cleaning, and it isn't interfering. What does the module recommend?","choices":["Let it continue; you don't need to stop or change it","Stop it at once with a papoose board so it can't escalate","Tell the patient to stop","End the appointment"],"answer":0,"ex":"Ask beforehand whether the patient stims, what it looks like and how to respond. Unless it disrupts dental work, don't feel obligated to stop it; if it does, redirect.","src":"module","why":["Only redirect if it disrupts the work.","Stabilization isn't for stimming that doesn't interfere.","Not needed if it's not disruptive.","No reason to."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Stimming: allow it unless it disrupts, then redirect","basis":"module","ids":["au-stim","au-redirect"]}},{"id":"au-redirect","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"If stimming is disrupting the dental work, what should you do?","choices":["Redirect: show another item or give the hands something to do","Restrain the hands with a papoose board until the stimming stops","Ignore it and keep working","Ask the parent to leave"],"answer":0,"ex":"If stimming disrupts the work, redirect by showing another item or giving the patient something else to do with their hands.","src":"module","why":["Gentle redirection.","Not the first step for stimming.","It's disrupting care.","Not related."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Stimming: allow it unless it disrupts, then redirect","basis":"module","ids":["au-stim","au-redirect"]}},{"id":"au-melt","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which describes a meltdown rather than a tantrum?","choices":["The child doesn't care who is watching and doesn't consider their own safety","The child looks around every so often to see whether anyone is reacting","It stops as soon as the child gets what they want","The child avoids getting hurt"],"answer":0,"ex":"Meltdown: no concern for others' reactions or own safety, runs on its own power and tapers slowly, no one feels in control, and nothing satisfies until it's over.","src":"module","why":["A struggle to cope, not a bid for attention.","That's a tantrum.","That's a tantrum.","That's a tantrum."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"A meltdown is not a temper tantrum","basis":"hint","ids":["au-melt","au-meltprep"]}},{"id":"au-meltprep","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Before treating a patient with autism, what is the clinician responsible for finding out about meltdowns?","choices":["Whether they occur, what triggers them and what warning signs come first","How long the last one lasted, so you can book a longer appointment","Which medicine stops them","Nothing; meltdowns can't be anticipated"],"answer":0,"ex":"Find out whether the patient has meltdowns, what triggers them and what signs the patient gives before one, and show compassion for the family.","src":"module","why":["So you can avoid or anticipate them.","Not the point.","Not described.","You can learn triggers and signs."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"A meltdown is not a temper tantrum","basis":"hint","ids":["au-melt","au-meltprep"]}},{"id":"au-sens","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What is sensory defensiveness?","choices":["Defensive reactions to sensations the brain misprocesses as uncomfortable or painful","Another name for autism","A tantrum the child uses on purpose to avoid having treatment","A medication side effect"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sensory defensiveness may relate to sensory integration disorders: a tag feels like a cut, a noise hurts. The patient defends against it.","src":"module","why":["Often with autism, but not the same thing.","They're not the same, though often together.","It's a protective response, not manipulation.","Not described that way."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Sensory defensiveness: firm touch, the x-ray vest","basis":"module","ids":["au-sens","au-vest","au-touch","au-fight"]}},{"id":"au-vest","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why might you have a child wear the x-ray vest for the entire appointment?","choices":["Its weight gives calming deep pressure","For radiation safety throughout","To keep the child's hands still","To keep the shirt clean"],"answer":0,"ex":"The x-ray vest provides extra weight and deep pressure. Also: sunglasses, warning before each step, headphones, firm touch, chewy food beforehand, a fidget toy, a heavy or tight hat.","src":"module","why":["A sensory strategy.","Not the reason given.","It isn't a restraint.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Sensory defensiveness: firm touch, the x-ray vest","basis":"module","ids":["au-sens","au-vest","au-touch","au-fight"]}},{"id":"au-touch","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"For a patient with sensory defensiveness, how should you touch them?","choices":["With firm touch","With the lightest touch possible","Only through a barrier","Without warning, to get it over quickly"],"answer":0,"ex":"Use firm touch whenever touching the child, and verbally warn before each thing you do.","src":"module","why":["Light touch is more alarming.","Firm touch is recommended.","Not the recommendation.","Warn before each thing you do."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Sensory defensiveness: firm touch, the x-ray vest","basis":"module","ids":["au-sens","au-vest","au-touch","au-fight"]}},{"id":"au-fight","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"If a sensory-defensive child's fear isn't respected, what typically happens?","choices":["Flight, then more and more physical fight responses","They calm down after a minute once they realize it won't hurt","They fall asleep","They stop feeling the stimulus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Children first try to escape the distressing stimulus; if they can't, they become more and more physically reactive. Several stimuli together are harder than one.","src":"module","why":["They escalate to get away.","Fear escalates.","Not described.","Not described."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Sensory defensiveness: firm touch, the x-ray vest","basis":"module","ids":["au-sens","au-vest","au-touch","au-fight"]}},{"id":"au-dental","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How does oral anatomy in autism compare with Down syndrome or CP?","choices":["No typical physical differences such as missing teeth or malocclusion","They have more missing laterals","They have open bites, flared incisors and Class II like patients with CP","They have macroglossia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Patients with autism generally have the same oral health issues as everyone, without the physical differences seen in Down syndrome or CP, but several conditions are more common.","src":"module","why":["Their issues are behavioral and dietary.","That's Down syndrome.","That's CP.","Down syndrome."]},{"id":"au-caries","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is caries more common in autism?","choices":["Fixed diets and oral sensory defensiveness that prevents regular cleaning","Their enamel is always hypoplastic from birth","They make almost no saliva because of autism itself","Antiseizure medicines"],"answer":0,"ex":"Caries is higher: patients may fixate on specific drinks or foods, and sensory defensiveness can prevent regular cleaning.","src":"module","why":["One patient drinks only one brand of juice.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason.","Not the reason given here."]},{"id":"au-attr","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is attrition more common in autism?","choices":["Grinding to relieve anxiety or as self-stimulation","GERD bringing stomach acid into the mouth","A hard diet","Class III occlusion"],"answer":0,"ex":"Attrition is higher because patients grind to relieve anxiety or repetitively as stimming. Erosion is higher from GERD.","src":"module","why":["Both reasons were given.","That's erosion.","Not the reason.","Not the reason."]},{"id":"au-trauma","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is a reason for the higher rate of dental trauma in autism?","choices":["A reduced sense of danger","Atlantoaxial instability","Flared incisors from tongue thrust","Seizures in every patient"],"answer":0,"ex":"Trauma is higher: reduced sense of danger, habits that damage teeth, and a higher incidence of abuse.","src":"module","why":["Plus damaging habits and a higher incidence of abuse.","Down syndrome.","CP.","Not stated."]},{"id":"au-perio","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is periodontal treatment hard for many adults with autism?","choices":["Behavior, and Medicaid's limited or absent adult dental benefit","They form no calculus","Their tissues don't respond to scaling","Periodontitis is much more common in autism than in Down syndrome"],"answer":0,"ex":"Periodontitis is slightly higher; treatment is challenging because of behavior and limited insurance (Medicaid has limited or no adult dental benefits).","src":"module","why":["Periodontitis is slightly higher.","Not true.","Not stated.","It's only slightly higher."]},{"id":"au-kit","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What belongs in a practice kit for home?","choices":["A disposable XCP holder without the metal bar","A loaded local anesthetic syringe to practice with","A high-speed handpiece","A papoose board"],"answer":0,"ex":"Practice kit items: prophy angle, saliva ejector, varnish brush, bib and holder, gloves and mask for the caregiver, a disposable XCP holder without the metal bar, cotton-tip applicator, disposable mirror, dry angles, and pictures of each in the mouth.","src":"module","why":["Lets the patient practice bitewings.","Not something to practice at home.","Not in the kit.","Not in the kit."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Preparing the visit: first appointment, the operatory, a practice kit","basis":"module","ids":["au-first","au-op","au-ready","au-kit","au-drybrush"]}},{"id":"au-drybrush","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"When a caregiver practices brushing the patient's teeth lying down at home, why use a dry toothbrush?","choices":["So the patient won't choke while lying down","So no fluoride is wasted before the real dental visit","Because wet bristles feel worse","To harden the bristles"],"answer":0,"ex":"Practice lying down to have teeth brushed several times a week, with a dry toothbrush so the patient won't choke.","src":"module","why":["Safety while practicing.","Not the reason.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Preparing the visit: first appointment, the operatory, a practice kit","basis":"module","ids":["au-first","au-op","au-ready","au-kit","au-drybrush"]}},{"id":"au-first","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which appointment time is generally best for a patient with autism?","choices":["The first appointment of the morning","The last appointment of the day","Right after lunch","Whenever the office is busiest, for distraction"],"answer":0,"ex":"Mornings are generally better; the first appointment of the day is ideal (even 10 minutes early): no waiting, quiet, sterilizers off, empty waiting room, smells settled. Trust parents who say afternoons are better.","src":"module","why":["Minimal waiting, a quiet office, fresh staff.","After school and therapy is often the tipping point.","Not recommended.","Busy is worse."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Preparing the visit: first appointment, the operatory, a practice kit","basis":"module","ids":["au-first","au-op","au-ready","au-kit","au-drybrush"]}},{"id":"au-op","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How should the operatory be prepared for a patient with autism?","choices":["Overhead lights low, music off, sterilizers and ultrasonic off, sunglasses offered","Bright fluorescent lights so you can see clearly, and music to cover equipment noise","Music up to cover equipment noise","A flashing screen saver for distraction"],"answer":0,"ex":"Turn off overhead music, sterilizers and the ultrasonic; keep lights low (chair or loupe light); always offer sunglasses; headphones; quiet phones; a consultation room instead of the waiting room; no flashing screen savers.","src":"module","why":["Reduce sensory input.","People with ASD are particularly sensitive to fluorescent light.","Turn overhead music off; headphones are better.","Flashing patterns can overwhelm."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Preparing the visit: first appointment, the operatory, a practice kit","basis":"module","ids":["au-first","au-op","au-ready","au-kit","au-drybrush"]}},{"id":"au-ready","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why should everything you might need be out on the counter before a patient with autism arrives?","choices":["Walking away and opening drawers distracts and adds noise","To save on sterilization","So the patient can play with the instruments","Because cabinets must stay locked"],"answer":0,"ex":"Have everything within arm's reach: leaving the operatory is distracting, and opening and closing drawers is noisy. And no perfume or scented lotion.","src":"module","why":["Sensory overload.","Not the reason.","Not the reason.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Preparing the visit: first appointment, the operatory, a practice kit","basis":"module","ids":["au-first","au-op","au-ready","au-kit","au-drybrush"]}},{"id":"au-literal","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why say \"Open your mouth\" instead of \"Open like an alligator\" to a patient with autism?","choices":["They understand language literally, and an alligator may derail them","Alligators frighten most children, so the comparison makes them anxious","\"Open\" is shorter to say","It's more polite"],"answer":0,"ex":"People with ASD understand language very literally; abstract examples may confuse, and a patient who loves alligators may fixate on them.","src":"module","why":["Keep it concrete.","Not the reason.","Not the point.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Communication (the Crest course page): literal words, say it once, calm tone","basis":"hint","ids":["au-literal","au-once","au-tone","au-hands","au-no","au-match"]}},{"id":"au-hands","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which phrase does the module recommend for keeping a patient's hands still?","choices":["\"Hands on belly\"","\"Stop moving!\"","\"Hands behind your head\"","\"Sit on your hands\""],"answer":0,"ex":"\"Hands on belly\" is one of the first rules taught; it sounds less scary and works wonders.","src":"module","why":["Friendlier than \"put your hands down\" or \"stop.\"","Less friendly; the module offers a better rule.","Not recommended.","Not the phrase."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Communication (the Crest course page): literal words, say it once, calm tone","basis":"hint","ids":["au-literal","au-once","au-tone","au-hands","au-no","au-match"]}},{"id":"au-once","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why give an instruction once and then wait, rather than repeating it in several ways?","choices":["Each new phrasing must be processed again, and the wait time is longer","Repeating is rude","They can follow only one instruction per appointment","So the parent can repeat it instead"],"answer":0,"ex":"\"Open your mouth, open big, open, open\": each new phrase has to be processed. Say it once (maybe \"like this\" and show), then wait.","src":"module","why":["Simple, direct, then patience.","Not the reason.","Not the reason.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Communication (the Crest course page): literal words, say it once, calm tone","basis":"hint","ids":["au-literal","au-once","au-tone","au-hands","au-no","au-match"]}},{"id":"au-tone","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What tone of voice does the module recommend for instructions to a patient with autism?","choices":["Direct, even and neutral, with a deeper voice than usual for children","High-pitched and sing-song, the way you'd talk to any young child","Loud and firm","A whisper"],"answer":0,"ex":"A sing-song, higher voice may not alert the patient that something must be done. Use a direct, even, neutral tone and a deeper voice.","src":"module","why":["So it registers as an instruction.","May not register as a request.","Not loud or rude.","Not recommended."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Communication (the Crest course page): literal words, say it once, calm tone","basis":"hint","ids":["au-literal","au-once","au-tone","au-hands","au-no","au-match"]}},{"id":"au-praise","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How should you praise a patient with autism who opens for the first time?","choices":["Calmly","With clapping and cheering","Not at all","With a sticker and a loud song"],"answer":0,"ex":"Excitement can be scary: offer compliments freely, but calmly.","src":"module","why":["Clapping and yelling can be scary.","That may keep them from ever opening again.","Praise freely, just calmly.","Excitement can be scary."]},{"id":"au-no","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which instruction follows the module's advice?","choices":["\"It is time to polish your teeth. Please open your mouth.\"","\"Can I polish your teeth now? It won't take very long.\"","\"Do you want to open your mouth?\"","\"Is it okay if we start?\""],"answer":0,"ex":"Don't ask questions that allow \"no\" when no isn't an option. Consent was obtained beforehand; treatment decisions are made in advance.","src":"module","why":["No question that allows \"no.\"","The rookie mistake: the child says no.","Allows \"no.\"","Allows \"no.\""],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Communication (the Crest course page): literal words, say it once, calm tone","basis":"hint","ids":["au-literal","au-once","au-tone","au-hands","au-no","au-match"]}},{"id":"au-midline","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why start polishing at the midline (the central incisors)?","choices":["The patient gets used to the vibration before you reach gag-triggering areas","The central incisors are where most plaque builds up in autism","It's faster","Molars can't be polished in autism"],"answer":0,"ex":"Start at the midline with the mouth slightly closed, then work toward gag-triggering areas. Always go from least to most invasive.","src":"module","why":["And tastes the paste only near the end.","Not the reason.","Not the reason.","Not true."]},{"id":"au-count","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"In the \"count to 10\" technique, what should you do if the patient is cooperating and you need more time?","choices":["Count more slowly","Stop at 6 and start over","Skip the count","Count faster"],"answer":0,"ex":"Count fast when the patient struggles, slowly when they cooperate, and always reach 10 so \"ten\" means a break. Use a smaller number if needed, consistently.","src":"module","why":["The clinician controls the pace.","Always reach 10 so \"ten\" means a break.","Counting gives structure.","Fast counting is for when they're struggling."]},{"id":"au-chair","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with autism hates the feeling of the chair moving back. What does the module suggest?","choices":["Have them stand, recline the chair, then have them lie down; they sit up before you raise it","Recline the chair quickly while they're distracted so it's over fast","Use a papoose board","Treat them standing"],"answer":0,"ex":"Start lying down: if the moving chair bothers them, position it first, then have them lie down, and have them sit up on their own before raising it.","src":"module","why":["Start lying down.","They dislike the motion.","Not needed for this.","Not suggested."]},{"id":"au-ocs","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What can families realistically expect from oral conscious sedation for a patient with severe autism?","choices":["Only mild sedation, often of limited value","Complete unconsciousness","Deep sedation equal to IV sedation","Permanent behavior change"],"answer":0,"ex":"Oral sedatives such as lorazepam or diazepam (Alabama oral conscious sedation license) give only very mild sedation.","src":"module","why":["Not the \"knock out\" families hope for.","That's general anesthesia.","IV is a higher level.","No."]},{"id":"au-iv","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What does the Alabama Board require for IV sedation in a dental office?","choices":["A higher license with office inspection and training, even when an anesthesiologist comes in","No license at all if an anesthesiologist does the sedation in the office","Only a CPR card","The same license as oral sedation"],"answer":0,"ex":"IV sedation needs a higher license; the Board inspects the office and requires training even if the dentist brings an anesthesiologist. Many autistic patients won't tolerate the IV start.","src":"module","why":["The requirement applies regardless.","The Board still requires it.","Far more is required.","IV is a higher licensure."]},{"id":"au-ga","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the main barrier to general anesthesia for adults with autism in Alabama?","choices":["Very limited access to operating rooms for adults","GA doesn't work in autism","Medicaid pays for GA only after 21","It's illegal for adults with disabilities"],"answer":0,"ex":"General anesthesia lets many be treated, but OR access is very limited; very few Alabama hospitals open ORs to adult patients.","src":"module","why":["Under 21, pediatric dentists usually have access.","It allows many to be treated.","Dental Medicaid ends at 21.","No."]},{"id":"au-surg","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"What can most oral surgeons in Alabama offer a patient with severe autism?","choices":["Extractions in the OR or under IV sedation, but not restorations","Comprehensive restorative care under general anesthesia","Orthodontics","Nothing for special needs patients"],"answer":0,"ex":"Oral surgeons can take patients into an OR and IV sedate, but are limited to extractions.","src":"module","why":["They are limited to extractions.","They can't restore teeth.","Not described.","They can take patients to the OR for extractions."]},{"id":"au-sparks","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"How does Sparks Dental Clinic manage adults with complex behavior, since its OR access is at Children's for patients under 21?","choices":["Oral sedation and protective stabilization","General anesthesia at Children's for all ages","IV sedation with an on-site anesthesiologist","It refers all adults elsewhere"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sparks (UAB School of Medicine/Civitan-Sparks) uses Children's OR for patients under 21 and provides oral sedation and protective stabilization for older patients.","src":"module","why":["Sparks is a dental home for complex patients.","Only under 21.","That's the Foundry.","It treats many adults."]},{"id":"au-foundry","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which Alabama clinic has an anesthesiologist coming in and has given comprehensive care to adults with complex needs?","choices":["The Foundry Dental Clinic (GPR, Bessemer)","UAB Hospital Dentistry, the GPR focused on medically complex patients","Most oral surgery offices","Most general dentists"],"answer":0,"ex":"The Foundry Dental Clinic, a GPR program in Bessemer, has an anesthesiologist come to the clinic.","src":"module","why":["Comprehensive adult care.","Focuses on medically complex patients, with limited OR time.","Extractions only.","Only a few accept these patients."]},{"id":"au-refer","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"A dentist feels they cannot safely treat a patient with profound autism. What should they do?","choices":["Decline to treat, but refer the family to a place that can","Treat anyway to avoid an ethics complaint","Decline and let the family keep searching on their own","Use a papoose board without consent"],"answer":0,"ex":"A dentist should never attempt treatment they can't do safely, but the ADA Code of Ethics says not to simply decline: refer.","src":"module","why":["Do no harm, and don't simply decline.","Never attempt unsafe treatment.","The ADA code says refer.","Consent is always required."],"likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Never treat a patient you can't treat safely: refer","basis":"hint","ids":["au-refer"]}},{"id":"au-tf-1","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Autism and sensory defensiveness are the same thing.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"They're different, though patients frequently have both.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["No: often together, but not the same.","Correct."]},{"id":"au-tf-2","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: A meltdown in an autistic child stops as soon as the child gets what they wanted.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"That describes a tantrum; a meltdown runs on its own power and tapers off slowly.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["No: that's a tantrum.","Correct."]},{"id":"au-tf-3","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: People with ASD are particularly sensitive to fluorescent lights.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Keep overhead lights off and use the chair or loupe light.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"au-tf-4","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Autism is more common than pediatric cancer, diabetes and AIDS combined.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The module states this.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"au-tf-5","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Identical twins have a 100% concordance rate for autism.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It's 77%: genes account for a lot of the risk, but not all.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["No: 77%.","Correct: 77%, not 100%."]},{"id":"au-tf-6","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Some children with autism seem to develop typically until 18-24 months, then stop gaining or lose skills.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"That pattern is described in the module.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"au-tf-7","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: If a patient raises a hand during the count to 10, you should stop immediately so they learn to trust you.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"If treatment stops every time the patient raises a hand or makes a noise, it will never be completed; try to reach 10.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["No: always try to get to 10.","Correct."]},{"id":"au-tf-8","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Patients with autism generally lack the physical oral differences, such as missing teeth, seen in Down syndrome or CP.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Their higher rates of caries, attrition, erosion and trauma come from behavior and diet.","src":"module","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"au-2s-1","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The CDC's 2012 data showed about 1 in 68 children on the autism spectrum.\n2. The 2020 rise to 1 in 36 is fully explained by better diagnosis.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"1 in 68 in 2012; the rise is only partly explained by better diagnosis.","src":"module","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: careful research shows it's only partly explained.","Statement 1 is true, not false: that's the 2012 figure.","Statement 1 is true: that's the 2012 figure. Statement 2 is false: careful research shows it's only partly explained.","Statement 1 is true, not false: that's the 2012 figure. Statement 2 is false, not true: careful research shows it's only partly explained."]},{"id":"au-2s-2","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Advanced paternal age is associated with autism.\n2. Valproic acid taken during pregnancy can increase autism risk.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true.","src":"module","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: the talk named the father's age. Statement 2 is true: valproic acid is the example he gave.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the talk named the father's age. Statement 2 is true, not false: valproic acid is the example he gave.","Statement 2 is true, not false: valproic acid is the example he gave.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the talk named the father's age."]},{"id":"au-2s-3","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Stimming should always be stopped before dental work begins.\n2. Stimming can happen when a person is happy.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Only stop or redirect it if it disrupts work; it can occur when bored, overwhelmed, nervous or happy.","src":"module","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: only if it's disrupting the work.","Statement 2 is true, not false: happiness is one trigger.","Statement 1 is false, not true: only if it's disrupting the work. Statement 2 is true, not false: happiness is one trigger.","Statement 1 is false: only if it's disrupting the work. Statement 2 is true: happiness is one trigger."]},{"id":"au-2s-4","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Clapping and cheering are the best way to praise a patient with autism.\n2. Use the same phrase for each instruction every visit, with every staff member.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Praise calmly; keep language consistent and document what works.","src":"module","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: excitement can be scary; praise calmly.","Statement 2 is true, not false: consistency reduces anxiety.","Statement 1 is false, not true: excitement can be scary; praise calmly. Statement 2 is true, not false: consistency reduces anxiety.","Statement 1 is false: excitement can be scary; praise calmly. Statement 2 is true: consistency reduces anxiety."]},{"id":"au-2s-5","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Sparks uses Children's Hospital's OR for patients under 21.\n2. Very few Alabama hospitals open their ORs to adult special needs patients.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true.","src":"module","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: OR access at Children's under 21. Statement 2 is true: adult OR access is the big gap.","Statement 1 is true, not false: OR access at Children's under 21. Statement 2 is true, not false: adult OR access is the big gap.","Statement 2 is true, not false: adult OR access is the big gap.","Statement 1 is true, not false: OR access at Children's under 21."]},{"id":"au-num-1","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which help reduce sensitivity during a visit for a sensory-defensive child?\n1. Light, feathery touch\n2. Wearing the x-ray vest the whole appointment\n3. Sunglasses\n4. Warning before each step","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Firm touch, not light touch; the others are on the list.","src":"module","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: firm touch is recommended.","It includes 1, which is false: firm touch is recommended. It leaves out 2, which is true: deep pressure. It leaves out 4, which is true: verbal warning before each thing.","It includes 1, which is false: firm touch is recommended. It leaves out 2, which is true: deep pressure.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: firm touch is recommended."]},{"id":"au-num-2","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is the first appointment of the day ideal for a patient with autism?\n1. Minimal waiting\n2. The office is quiet and staff are fresh\n3. The waiting room is at its busiest\n4. Cleaning-solution smells have settled overnight","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"The waiting room is empty first thing, not busy.","src":"module","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 3, which is false: it's at its quietest.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: it's at its quietest.","It leaves out 2, which is true: sterilizers off, staff patient.","It includes 3, which is false: it's at its quietest. It leaves out 1, which is true: waiting is especially hard."]},{"id":"au-num-3","lec":"autism","type":"mcq","stem":"Which dental conditions have a higher incidence in autism?\n1. Caries\n2. Missing lateral incisors\n3. Attrition\n4. Trauma","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Missing laterals are a Down syndrome finding; caries, attrition and trauma are higher in autism.","src":"module","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: no physical tooth differences in autism. It leaves out 4, which is true: reduced sense of danger, habits, abuse.","It includes 2, which is false: no physical tooth differences in autism.","It leaves out 4, which is true: reduced sense of danger, habits, abuse.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: no physical tooth differences in autism."]},{"id":"au-match","lec":"autism","type":"match","stem":"Match each communication rule to its reason.","pairs":[["\"Open your mouth,\" not \"like an alligator\"","They understand language literally"],["Give the instruction once, then wait","Each repetition must be processed again"],["Praise calmly","Excitement can be scary"],["Don't ask \"Can I polish now?\"","No isn't an option once consent is given"]],"ex":"The Crest module's practical communication rules.","src":"module","likely":{"lec":"autism","t":"Communication (the Crest course page): literal words, say it once, calm tone","basis":"hint","ids":["au-literal","au-once","au-tone","au-hands","au-no","au-match"]}},{"id":"au-match2","lec":"autism","type":"match","stem":"Match each place to what it offers patients with complex behavior.","pairs":[["Oral surgeons","Extractions only, in the OR or with IV sedation"],["The Foundry Dental Clinic","An anesthesiologist comes to the clinic"],["Sparks Dental Clinic","Children's OR under 21; stabilization for adults"],["Pediatric dentists","OR or in-office sedation for children"]],"ex":"Advanced treatment options in Alabama.","src":"module"},{"id":"au-mm-1","lec":"autism","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which describe a meltdown rather than a tantrum?","choices":["The person doesn't look or care whether anyone reacts","The person doesn't consider their own safety","It tapers off slowly, and nothing satisfies until it's over","It stops as soon as the goal is reached","The child checks whether anyone is reacting","It's an attempt to manipulate the situation"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Meltdown: struggling to cope, no audience, no regard for safety, tapers slowly, no one feels in control.","src":"module","why":["A meltdown isn't aimed at an audience.","A tantrum avoids getting hurt; a meltdown doesn't.","It runs on its own power.","That's a tantrum.","That's a tantrum: it seeks attention.","That's a tantrum; in a meltdown the person is struggling to cope."]},{"id":"au-mm-2","lec":"autism","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which dental conditions are more common in people with autism?","choices":["Caries","Attrition","Erosion","Trauma","Missing or extra teeth","Malocclusion"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Higher: caries, attrition, erosion, trauma; slightly higher: periodontitis; no difference in tooth number or malocclusion.","src":"module","why":["Fixed diets and cleaning that isn't tolerated.","Grinding for anxiety relief or as stimming.","From more GERD.","A reduced sense of danger, habits, and more abuse.","No difference, unlike Down syndrome.","No difference, unlike Down syndrome or CP."]},{"id":"au-mm-3","lec":"autism","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which follow the module's communication advice for a patient with autism?","choices":["Saying \"hands on belly\" instead of \"stop\"","Giving the instruction once and waiting","A direct, even, neutral tone","\"Open like an alligator,\" to make it fun","Clapping and cheering after each step","Asking \"Can I polish your teeth now?\""],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Short, concrete, consistent words; one instruction and wait; even tone; calm praise; visual supports.","src":"module","why":["Friendlier, and it works wonders.","Each repetition has to be processed again.","A sing-song voice may not register as an instruction.","Language is taken literally: say \"open your mouth.\"","Excitement can be scary: praise freely, but calmly.","Don't offer a no when no isn't an option: \"It is time to polish your teeth.\""]},{"id":"pv-varnish","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does Sparks use fluoride varnish instead of foam or gel in trays?","choices":["Patients can't tolerate a tray for 4 minutes, and varnish gags less","Varnish is cheaper, though it's weaker and must be reapplied weekly","Gels and foams are banned for special needs patients","Varnish needs no saliva to set"],"answer":0,"ex":"Foam or gel must sit in a tray for at least 4 minutes; few patients tolerate it, and brushing gel on lacks evidence. Varnish: fewer gag problems, stronger, quick to apply, and it stays on as a reservoir.","src":"rec","why":["It's also stronger and stays on.","It's stronger, not weaker.","Not banned; not tolerated.","Saliva actually helps it congeal."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Varnish, sealants and SDF: the ADA guideline","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-varnish","pv-occl","pv-sdf","pv-67","pv-root","pv-prox"]}},{"id":"pv-eat","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"After fluoride varnish, when can the patient eat and drink?","choices":["Almost immediately","After 30 minutes","After 24 hours","Only the next morning"],"answer":0,"ex":"A real advantage of varnish: no waiting period; patients can eat and drink almost immediately. For a very sensitive gag reflex, the excess can even be rinsed out.","src":"rec","why":["No waiting period, unlike gels and foams.","That's the usual advice for gels and foams.","No.","No."]},{"id":"pv-occl","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"For a non-cavitated occlusal lesion, what did the ADA's 2018 guideline find most effective?","choices":["A sealant over the lesion plus fluoride varnish every 3-6 months","Fluoride varnish alone every year","Restoring every occlusal lesion right away, before it can grow","Silver diamine fluoride once"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sealant plus fluoride varnish every 3-6 months prevented progression 67 of 100 times (strong evidence). Varnish alone was in the 40s.","src":"rec","why":["67 of 100 arrested.","Varnish alone arrested about 40-something of 100, at 3-6 months.","The guideline is about not cutting.","Not the guideline's best option for this lesion."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Varnish, sealants and SDF: the ADA guideline","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-varnish","pv-occl","pv-sdf","pv-67","pv-root","pv-prox"]}},{"id":"pv-67","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"In the ADA guideline, sealant plus varnish arrested about how many non-cavitated occlusal lesions out of 100?","choices":["67","46","100","20"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sealant plus varnish: 67 of 100 arrested. For a special needs patient, avoiding a bur two-thirds of the time is a big win.","src":"rec","why":["Almost two-thirds.","That's varnish on approximal lesions.","No treatment is perfect.","Too low."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Varnish, sealants and SDF: the ADA guideline","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-varnish","pv-occl","pv-sdf","pv-67","pv-root","pv-prox"]}},{"id":"pv-prox","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What does 5% fluoride varnish every 3-6 months do for non-cavitated approximal lesions?","choices":["Doubles the chance of reversing or stopping them: 46 of 100","Arrests all of them","Has no effect between the teeth","It works only when a sealant is placed over the contact as well"],"answer":0,"ex":"Interproximal lesions on bitewings: varnish every 3-6 months gives a twofold chance of arrest or reversal, 46 of 100.","src":"rec","why":["Simple and cost-effective.","Not all.","It's effective.","Sealants are for occlusal lesions."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Varnish, sealants and SDF: the ADA guideline","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-varnish","pv-occl","pv-sdf","pv-67","pv-root","pv-prox"]}},{"id":"pv-root","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What did the ADA guideline recommend for root caries?","choices":["Prevident 5000 used at home","Sealants","Silver diamine fluoride only","Nothing: root caries can't be arrested"],"answer":0,"ex":"For root caries, the ADA recommended Prevident 5000 for daily home care.","src":"rec","why":["High-fluoride toothpaste.","For occlusal lesions.","Not the ADA recommendation he showed.","It can be slowed."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Varnish, sealants and SDF: the ADA guideline","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-varnish","pv-occl","pv-sdf","pv-67","pv-root","pv-prox"]}},{"id":"pv-sdf","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which material arrests small lesions but turns them black, and is used a lot at Sparks when restoring would be hard?","choices":["Silver diamine fluoride","Resin-modified glass ionomer","Stainless steel crown","Fluoride varnish"],"answer":0,"ex":"When cavitation occurs: SDF for small lesions or difficult behavior, RMGI (Fuji II LC, Fuji IX, Ketac Nano) for small-moderate cavities, stainless steel crowns for large ones.","src":"rec","why":["Arrests; stains black.","Restores and releases fluoride, without staining black.","For larger lesions.","Varnish doesn't turn lesions black."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Varnish, sealants and SDF: the ADA guideline","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-varnish","pv-occl","pv-sdf","pv-67","pv-root","pv-prox"]}},{"id":"pv-rmgi","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why are resin-modified glass ionomers good restorations for this population?","choices":["They release fluoride, protecting the surrounding tooth","They are the strongest material for large lesions","They need perfect isolation","They turn lesions black"],"answer":0,"ex":"RMGIs fill the hole and release fluoride to strengthen the surrounding tooth.","src":"rec","why":["They also go in easily.","Steel crowns are for large lesions.","They tolerate moisture better than resin.","That's SDF."]},{"id":"pv-ssc","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about stainless steel crowns matches the lecture?","choices":["Sparks uses them on permanent teeth too, for lesions too big for RMGI","They are used only on primary teeth, never on permanent teeth","They are for small occlusal lesions","They release fluoride"],"answer":0,"ex":"If a lesion is too large for RMGI, stainless steel crowns are an excellent option, including on permanent teeth.","src":"rec","why":["They cover and protect the whole tooth.","He said permanent teeth too.","For large lesions.","That's RMGI."]},{"id":"pv-hidden","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient's gingiva is swollen, but you see no calculus. What should you expect?","choices":["A ring of calculus just below the gingival margin","No calculus, since none is visible","Only soft plaque, which the patient can brush away at home","A drug reaction"],"answer":0,"ex":"Swollen tissue almost always means calculus under the margin, often a ring just below the tissue. At Sparks, a patient charted \"no calculus\" had extensive subgingival calculus and mobile lower incisors.","src":"rec","why":["Explore under the tissue.","That's how it gets missed.","Plaque is there too, but swollen tissue almost always hides calculus.","Not the likely cause."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Swollen gingiva: check under the margins for calculus","basis":"hint","ids":["pv-hidden"]}},{"id":"pv-sickle","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which instrument does Dr. Mitchell use for supragingival calculus?","choices":["A sickle scaler","A curette","A periodontal probe","A rubber cup"],"answer":0,"ex":"Supragingival: sickle scaler (point interproximally, lift). Subgingival: curette (rounded toe, slid below the margin).","src":"rec","why":["Its point reaches interproximally.","For subgingival calculus.","Not for removal.","For plaque and stain."]},{"id":"pv-curette","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"How does he remove subgingival calculus with a curette?","choices":["Slide the toe below the margin, feel for calculus, engage it and lift with smooth strokes","Use the pointed tip of the curette above the gumline to lift the deposits","Scrub side to side","Press the cup against the sulcus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Slide the curette below the tissue, feel for rough or raised areas, engage with the toe and lift against the tooth.","src":"rec","why":["Explore first, then engage.","That's sickle work.","Use long, smooth strokes.","That's a prophy cup."]},{"id":"pv-lip","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Calculus builds on the facial of a patient's lower incisors. What causes it, and what do you teach?","choices":["A tight lower lip blocks the brush; teach caregivers to pull the lip down","Tongue thrust; teach a tongue exercise","G-tube feeding; leave it alone","Saliva from the submandibular gland; nothing can be done to prevent it"],"answer":0,"ex":"Many patients tighten the lower lip while brushing; teach caregivers to pull it down, or place a finger where the lip meets the buccal mucosa and roll it out.","src":"rec","why":["Reach the gingival margin.","Not the cause given.","That's occlusal calculus in g-tube patients.","Usually lingual; facial calculus here is from the lip."]},{"id":"pv-cup","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which describes good rubber cup prophy technique?","choices":["Reach the gingival margin, follow the contour, roll into the interproximal and let the spin work","Stay on the occlusal third, well away from the gums, to avoid bleeding","Brush back and forth like a toothbrush","Hold the cup in one spot for a long time"],"answer":0,"ex":"Focus at the gingival margin: the cup must touch the tissue (not for long), follow the contour, let the spin do the work, and roll into interproximal areas.","src":"rec","why":["Moderate to high speed.","The cup must touch the tissue.","Let the spin do the work.","Not for long periods."]},{"id":"pv-pulse","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"How do you use the rubber cup on a patient with a sensitive gag reflex?","choices":["In pulses of 1-2 seconds, then off","Continuously, to finish quickly","Only on the anterior teeth","With extra paste"],"answer":0,"ex":"Work in pulses: 1-2 seconds on the tooth, then off. Most valuable on the lingual of the lower molars, near the tongue.","src":"rec","why":["Remove the stimulus before the gag fires.","Continuous contact triggers the gag.","Pulse instead, including posteriors.","More paste adds stimulus."]},{"id":"pv-gtube","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Where should calculus be left on a patient fed by gastrostomy tube?","choices":["On the occlusal surfaces","On the buccal surfaces","On the lingual surfaces","Nowhere; remove it all"],"answer":0,"ex":"G-tube patients often have very heavy calculus and hypersensitive gag reflexes; remove it from buccal and lingual surfaces but leave it on the occlusal.","src":"rec","why":["A natural sealant.","Remove buccal and lingual.","Remove buccal and lingual.","Leave the occlusal calculus."]},{"id":"pv-recall","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which recall plan fits what Dr. Mitchell described?","choices":["Some patients every 3-4 months, even monthly, with families aware insurance may not pay","Everyone every 6 months, since insurance requires that interval","Once a year to save money","Only when the patient complains"],"answer":0,"ex":"Six months is common, but many patients need 3- or 4-month recall, or even monthly. Payers often cover only two a year; keep cost down by charging only for the cleaning.","src":"rec","why":["Six months isn't always best.","Not always best.","Too infrequent.","Many can't communicate pain."]},{"id":"pv-surround","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the Surround toothbrush best for?","choices":["Patients who tolerate only a short brushing: it cleans three surfaces at once","Everyone, because it brushes more thoroughly than a regular brush","Patients who can't open at all","Applying fluoride varnish"],"answer":0,"ex":"The Surround brush has three heads that clean both sides and the occlusal at once. If a regular brush can be used, use it.","src":"rec","why":["Faster, though less efficient than a regular brush.","It's not as efficient.","It still needs access.","Not its use."]},{"id":"pv-handle","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient can brush cognitively but can't grip or bend the arm well. Which is a fitting aid?","choices":["A handle enlarged with a tennis ball or foam, or lengthened with a tongue blade","The Surround toothbrush, used by a caregiver who brushes for them","A Waterpik","Disclosing tablets"],"answer":0,"ex":"Modified handles help patients who can brush but are physically challenged; the Surround brush helps when someone else must brush quickly.","src":"rec","why":["Self-modifications for physical limits.","For quick caregiver brushing.","Not shown to help.","Messy, not helpful here."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Home brushing positions and aids","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-stand","pv-recliner","pv-floor","pv-match2","pv-handle"]}},{"id":"pv-floss","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does Dr. Mitchell like a flosser with the floss at 90 degrees to the handle?","choices":["It reaches the back teeth easily, including for a caregiver","It's cheaper","It releases fluoride between the teeth as you floss","It doesn't need to touch the gums"],"answer":0,"ex":"With the floss perpendicular to the handle, it's much easier to reach the back teeth, especially when flossing someone else.","src":"rec","why":["He uses one himself.","Not the reason.","No.","Not the reason."]},{"id":"pv-sonic","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What did he say about electric toothbrushes?","choices":["Sonic-type brushes can be better, and the vibration comforts some sensory patients","They should never be used in special needs patients","Round spinning heads are his first choice because they cover more teeth","Vibration always upsets autistic patients"],"answer":0,"ex":"Electric brushes, especially Sonicare-type with small heads, can be better choices; the vibration comforts some sensory patients. Round spin-brush heads can be too big.","src":"rec","why":["Not one size fits all; experiment.","He recommends them for many.","He's more cautious about spin brushes.","Some find it comforting."]},{"id":"pv-stand","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which home brushing position has the lowest gag risk?","choices":["The caregiver standing in front of the patient","Lying in bed","Lying on the floor with two caregivers","Reclined in a recliner"],"answer":0,"ex":"Standing in front: for more cooperative patients, little control of the hands, lowest gag risk.","src":"rec","why":["The patient can even lean forward.","Most gag risk.","Most gag risk.","More gag risk."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Home brushing positions and aids","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-stand","pv-recliner","pv-floor","pv-match2","pv-handle"]}},{"id":"pv-recliner","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why recommend brushing in a recliner at home?","choices":["It replicates the dental chair, training the patient for visits","It has the lowest gag risk of any position at home","It lets one caregiver hold the hands","It's best for very tall patients"],"answer":0,"ex":"The recliner position mirrors the dental chair, so parents can help the patient get used to it, though the gag risk rises.","src":"rec","why":["Desensitization.","It has more.","The two-person floor position does that.","Sitting with the caregiver behind suits tall patients."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Home brushing positions and aids","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-stand","pv-recliner","pv-floor","pv-match2","pv-handle"]}},{"id":"pv-floor","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"In the two-person floor position, what does caregiver 1 do?","choices":["Holds the head in their lap and holds the hands","Brushes the teeth while caregiver 2 holds the legs","Holds the legs","Shines a light"],"answer":0,"ex":"The patient lies on the floor with the head in caregiver 1's lap; caregiver 1 holds the hands (crossed is easier); caregiver 2 brushes. Most control, most gag risk.","src":"rec","why":["Caregiver 2 brushes.","That's caregiver 2.","Not described.","Not described."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Home brushing positions and aids","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-stand","pv-recliner","pv-floor","pv-match2","pv-handle"]}},{"id":"pv-age8","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Before what age should take-home fluoride rinses or gels not be prescribed?","choices":["8 years","21 years","3 years","12 years"],"answer":0,"ex":"Don't prescribe supplemental home fluoride under about 8: swallowed fluoride can cause fluorosis in developing teeth.","src":"rec","why":["They'll swallow some, risking fluorosis while teeth develop.","That's the Medicaid dental age.","Too young a cutoff for what he said.","Not the age he gave."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"No take-home fluoride rinses or gels under age 8","basis":"hint","ids":["pv-age8"]}},{"id":"pv-contact","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient tolerates only 5-10 seconds of anything in the mouth. Why won't a product needing 30-60 seconds of contact help?","choices":["It needs that contact time to work","It will stain the teeth","It must be swallowed to work","It works only with a tray"],"answer":0,"ex":"Consider the contact time required: if the product needs 30-60 seconds and the patient tolerates 5-10, there's no efficacy.","src":"rec","why":["Consider contact time for efficacy.","Not the reason.","Not the reason.","Trays are rarely tolerated."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Products and contact time: Prevident, GelKam, MI Paste Plus","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-contact","pv-prev","pv-mi","pv-gelkam","pv-match"]}},{"id":"pv-prev","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"How does Dr. Mitchell typically tell families to use Prevident 5000?","choices":["Brush it on at night, spit or wipe out the excess, and go to bed without rinsing","Swish with it for a minute each morning, then rinse with water","Put it in a custom tray for 4 minutes","Swallow a small amount daily"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prevident 5000 replaces their toothpaste; apply at night, remove the excess, and don't rinse.","src":"rec","why":["It sits on the teeth overnight.","It's a toothpaste, and most can't rinse.","Trays are rarely tolerated.","Never."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Products and contact time: Prevident, GelKam, MI Paste Plus","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-contact","pv-prev","pv-mi","pv-gelkam","pv-match"]}},{"id":"pv-gelkam","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"How does GelKam differ from Prevident 5000?","choices":["GelKam is stannous fluoride only, not a toothpaste","GelKam is a sodium fluoride toothpaste","GelKam contains calcium and phosphate","GelKam is a mouth rinse"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prevident 5000: a high-fluoride sodium fluoride toothpaste. GelKam: stannous fluoride only, not a toothpaste.","src":"rec","why":["Use it alongside a paste they like.","That's Prevident 5000.","That's MI Paste.","It's a gel."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Products and contact time: Prevident, GelKam, MI Paste Plus","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-contact","pv-prev","pv-mi","pv-gelkam","pv-match"]}},{"id":"pv-mi","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why recommend MI Paste Plus rather than plain MI Paste?","choices":["MI Paste Plus adds fluoride to the calcium and phosphate","Plain MI Paste stains the teeth brown with long use","MI Paste Plus has no flavor","Plain MI Paste needs a tray"],"answer":0,"ex":"MI Paste is a calcium- and phosphate-rich product meant to help remineralization (evidence lacking); MI Paste Plus adds sodium fluoride, which is why he'd use it.","src":"rec","why":["Plain MI Paste has weak evidence.","Not the reason.","It comes in several.","Not the reason."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Products and contact time: Prevident, GelKam, MI Paste Plus","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-contact","pv-prev","pv-mi","pv-gelkam","pv-match"]}},{"id":"pv-toothette","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient can't rinse and spit. How can a fluoride rinse still be used?","choices":["Soak a Toothette and apply it repeatedly for 30-60 seconds","Spray it into the mouth once and let the patient swallow","Add it to drinking water","It can't be used"],"answer":0,"ex":"A Toothette (a sponge on a stick) or toothbrush can soak up the rinse and apply it to all surfaces, repeatedly for 30-60 seconds.","src":"rec","why":["Coach families on the time.","Too little contact.","Not recommended.","It can, with a Toothette or brush."]},{"id":"pv-listerine","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"A tall autistic patient uses Listerine daily but refuses toothpaste. What did Sparks recommend?","choices":["Brush without paste and switch to a Listerine that contains fluoride","Force the toothpaste","Stop the Listerine","Prescribe GelKam in a custom tray to wear at night instead"],"answer":0,"ex":"The patient could rinse, which is unusual, so the family brushed without toothpaste and switched to a fluoride Listerine (or a rinse like ACT).","src":"rec","why":["Use what the patient will accept.","Not recommended.","They kept the rinse and added fluoride.","Trays are rarely tolerated."]},{"id":"pv-ssb","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why are sugar-sweetened beverages especially harmful for this population?","choices":["They bathe every tooth and are often sipped over long periods","They contain acid but no sugar, so they only cause erosion","They stain the teeth","They dry the mouth"],"answer":0,"ex":"SSBs bathe all teeth in sugar, reaching every nook, and are often consumed frequently and over long periods.","src":"rec","why":["Frequency and coverage.","They contain sugar.","Stain isn't the main harm.","Not the reason given."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Diet: sugar-sweetened drinks, and \"what do you drink the most?\"","basis":"hint","ids":["pv-ssb","pv-question","pv-drinks","pv-10cal"]}},{"id":"pv-question","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What diagnostic question does Dr. Mitchell ask about diet?","choices":["\"In a typical day, what does he or she drink the most?\"","\"How many pieces of candy does he or she eat in a week?\"","\"Do you brush after meals?\"","\"What did he eat yesterday?\""],"answer":0,"ex":"The key question: in a typical day, what does the patient drink the most?","src":"rec","why":["It helps the most.","Not the question he named.","Not about diet.","Not the question."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Diet: sugar-sweetened drinks, and \"what do you drink the most?\"","basis":"hint","ids":["pv-ssb","pv-question","pv-drinks","pv-10cal"]}},{"id":"pv-drinks","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which drink is NOT on Dr. Mitchell's list of healthy drinks?","choices":["100% fruit juice","Water","Plain milk","Unsweet tea"],"answer":0,"ex":"Healthy drinks: water, plain milk (not chocolate or strawberry) and unsweet tea. Juice isn't listed.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["High in natural sugar: like eating five to ten apples.","On the list.","On the list.","On the list."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Diet: sugar-sweetened drinks, and \"what do you drink the most?\"","basis":"hint","ids":["pv-ssb","pv-question","pv-drinks","pv-10cal"]}},{"id":"pv-10cal","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What label rule does Dr. Mitchell teach families for drinks?","choices":["10 calories or fewer per serving is not a caries risk","Under 100 calories per serving is a safe amount of sugar","Any drink labeled natural is safe","Check the sodium, not the calories"],"answer":0,"ex":"Look at the calorie count on the label: 10 or fewer per serving is typically safe for caries.","src":"rec","why":["Coke Zero 0, Dr Pepper Ten 10.","Coke has 110; 100 is far too high.","Juice is natural and high in sugar.","Calories are the guide."],"likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Diet: sugar-sweetened drinks, and \"what do you drink the most?\"","basis":"hint","ids":["pv-ssb","pv-question","pv-drinks","pv-10cal"]}},{"id":"pv-sweet","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"What does he recommend about artificially sweetened drinks?","choices":["Use them instead of SSBs day to day, limited to 2-3 a day; talk with the PCP","Avoid them entirely, because they're worse for teeth than sugar","Unlimited, since they're safe","Only on special occasions"],"answer":0,"ex":"Talk with the primary care provider about sweeteners; day to day use artificially sweetened drinks instead of SSBs, 2-3 a day; on special occasions, enjoy.","src":"rec","why":["Special occasions: have fun.","He likes them for oral health.","He limits them to 2-3 a day.","The reverse: daily instead of SSBs."]},{"id":"pv-asp","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Using the FDA's acceptable daily intake for aspartame, about how much Coke Zero would a 150-lb adult need to reach the limit?","choices":["About 19 cans (6.7 liters) a day","About 2 cans a day","About 1 liter a day","About 50 cans a day"],"answer":0,"ex":"The ADI is 50 mg/kg/day, about 19 cans or 6.7 liters a day for a 150-lb adult.","src":"rec","why":["Far beyond what anyone drinks.","Far too low.","Too low.","Higher than the figure."]},{"id":"pv-resist","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why do families sometimes resist diet changes for a loved one with special needs?","choices":["The drink is a treat, and taking it away can cause a meltdown","They don't believe sugar causes cavities in people with disabilities","Medicaid pays for sugary drinks","Doctors forbid diet drinks"],"answer":0,"ex":"Families may see the SSB as one thing the patient can enjoy, and removing it can trigger a meltdown. It takes coaching.","src":"rec","why":["One family chose Dr Pepper over the teeth.","Not the reason he described.","No.","Not the reason."]},{"id":"pv-pain","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does untreated gum inflammation matter for behavior in this population?","choices":["Patients who can't say they hurt may become more combative","It causes autism","It improves cooperation, because sore gums make patients sleepy","It only matters for esthetics"],"answer":0,"ex":"Many patients can't communicate discomfort; chronic inflammation hurts, and pain can show up as worse behavior.","src":"rec","why":["An autistic patient hit his face because his mouth hurt.","No.","Pain worsens it.","It causes pain."]},{"id":"pv-tf-1","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Brushing a fluoride gel or foam onto the teeth is well supported by evidence.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Gel or foam needs about 4 minutes of contact; brushing it on has no real evidence.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: that's why Sparks uses varnish.","Correct."]},{"id":"pv-tf-2","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Fluoride varnish can stop existing early lesions from getting bigger, not just prevent new ones.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The ADA guideline on non-restorative treatment showed it.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"pv-tf-3","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: On a g-tube-fed patient, occlusal calculus should be left in place.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"It acts as a natural sealant; remove buccal and lingual calculus.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"pv-tf-4","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Plain milk is on the list of tooth-friendly drinks, including chocolate milk.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Plain milk is; chocolate or strawberry milk is not.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No: flavored milk is excluded.","Correct."]},{"id":"pv-tf-5","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most third-party payers will pay for more than two cleanings a year.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Most won't pay for more than two, and some require six months between.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No.","Correct."]},{"id":"pv-tf-6","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Almost all sugar-sweetened beverages are sweetened with high-fructose corn syrup.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"He made the point to families committed to \"all natural\" diets.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"pv-tf-7","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Lying in bed is the home brushing position with the lowest gag risk.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Lying down carries the most gag risk; standing in front carries the least.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No.","Correct."]},{"id":"pv-tf-8","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The Surround toothbrush cleans more efficiently than a regular toothbrush.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It's faster, not more efficient; use a regular brush when possible.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["No.","Correct."]},{"id":"pv-2s-1","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Fluoride varnish every 3-6 months doubles the chance of arresting non-cavitated approximal lesions.\n2. For non-cavitated occlusal lesions, the ADA's best option was varnish alone.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Approximal: varnish, 46 of 100. Occlusal: sealant plus varnish (67 of 100).","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: sealant plus varnish was best.","Statement 1 is true, not false: a twofold chance, 46 of 100.","Statement 1 is true: a twofold chance, 46 of 100. Statement 2 is false: sealant plus varnish was best.","Statement 1 is true, not false: a twofold chance, 46 of 100. Statement 2 is false, not true: sealant plus varnish was best."]},{"id":"pv-2s-2","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. A sickle scaler is used for subgingival calculus.\n2. A curette's rounded toe is used below the gingival margin.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Sickle above the gumline, curette below.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the sickle is for supragingival calculus.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the curette goes subgingival.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the sickle is for supragingival calculus. Statement 2 is true, not false: the curette goes subgingival.","Statement 1 is false: the sickle is for supragingival calculus. Statement 2 is true: the curette goes subgingival."]},{"id":"pv-2s-3","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. MI Paste Plus contains fluoride.\n2. GelKam is a toothpaste.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"MI Paste Plus adds sodium fluoride; GelKam is fluoride only, not a toothpaste.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: it's stannous fluoride only.","Statement 1 is true, not false: it adds sodium fluoride.","Statement 1 is true: it adds sodium fluoride. Statement 2 is false: it's stannous fluoride only.","Statement 1 is true, not false: it adds sodium fluoride. Statement 2 is false, not true: it's stannous fluoride only."]},{"id":"pv-2s-4","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Diet control may be the best caries option when good hygiene isn't realistic.\n2. 100% fruit juice is a healthy alternative to soda for teeth.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Diet control can be the best tool; juice is high in natural sugar.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: juice isn't on the list.","Statement 1 is true, not false: families can control what goes in the mouth.","Statement 1 is true: families can control what goes in the mouth. Statement 2 is false: juice isn't on the list.","Statement 1 is true, not false: families can control what goes in the mouth. Statement 2 is false, not true: juice isn't on the list."]},{"id":"pv-2s-5","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Working the rubber cup in short pulses helps patients with sensitive gag reflexes.\n2. Pulsing is most valuable on the lingual of the lower molars.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: 1-2 seconds on, then off. Statement 2 is true: near the tongue.","Statement 1 is true, not false: 1-2 seconds on, then off. Statement 2 is true, not false: near the tongue.","Statement 2 is true, not false: near the tongue.","Statement 1 is true, not false: 1-2 seconds on, then off."]},{"id":"pv-num-1","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are advantages of fluoride varnish at Sparks?\n1. It must sit in a tray for 4 minutes\n2. Fewer gag reflex problems than trays\n3. No waiting to eat or drink\n4. A stronger fluoride concentration","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"The 4-minute tray is the drawback of foams and gels, not varnish.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: that's foam or gel. It leaves out 4, which is true: stronger fluoride.","It includes 1, which is false: that's foam or gel. It leaves out 2, which is true: no tray.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: that's foam or gel.","It leaves out 3, which is true: eat and drink almost immediately."]},{"id":"pv-num-2","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are good restorative choices to prevent future decay in this population?\n1. Silver diamine fluoride for small lesions\n2. Resin-modified glass ionomer\n3. Stainless steel crowns for large lesions\n4. Large posterior composites without isolation","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","1 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":0,"ex":"Composites need isolation; the other three are his choices.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: resin needs isolation.","It includes 4, which is false: resin needs isolation. It leaves out 3, which is true: covers the whole tooth.","It leaves out 2, which is true: releases fluoride.","It includes 4, which is false: resin needs isolation. It leaves out 1, which is true: arrests small lesions."]},{"id":"pv-num-3","lec":"prevention","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about home fluoride are true?\n1. Don't prescribe rinses or gels before about age 8\n2. Custom trays are usually well tolerated\n3. Products are often brushed on\n4. Contact time must be considered","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":2,"ex":"Custom trays are rarely tolerated; the others are true.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: trays are rarely tolerated. It leaves out 4, which is true: too short a contact time means no effect.","It includes 2, which is false: trays are rarely tolerated. It leaves out 3, which is true: few can rinse and spit.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: trays are rarely tolerated.","It includes 2, which is false: trays are rarely tolerated. It leaves out 1, which is true: fluorosis risk."]},{"id":"pv-match","lec":"prevention","type":"match","stem":"Match each product to what it is.","pairs":[["Prevident 5000","High-fluoride sodium fluoride toothpaste"],["GelKam","Stannous fluoride gel, not a toothpaste"],["MI Paste Plus","Calcium, phosphate and fluoride"],["Toothette","A sponge on a stick to apply rinses"]],"ex":"Home preventive medicaments and an applicator.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Products and contact time: Prevident, GelKam, MI Paste Plus","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-contact","pv-prev","pv-mi","pv-gelkam","pv-match"]}},{"id":"pv-match2","lec":"prevention","type":"match","stem":"Match each brushing position to its main trait.","pairs":[["Standing in front","Lowest gag risk"],["Recliner","Trains for the dental chair"],["Sitting, caregiver behind","Good for tall patients"],["Floor, two caregivers","Most control of uncooperative patients"]],"ex":"Positions taught to caregivers.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"prevention","t":"Home brushing positions and aids","basis":"lecture","ids":["pv-stand","pv-recliner","pv-floor","pv-match2","pv-handle"]}},{"id":"pv-mm-1","lec":"prevention","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: why does Sparks use fluoride varnish instead of gels or foams in trays?","choices":["Fewer gag reflex problems","It's applied in seconds with a brush","The patient can eat and drink right away","It stays on as a reservoir of fluoride","It works with a four-minute tray application","Patients avoid eating for 20-30 minutes after it"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Varnish: less gagging, fast, stays on, no waiting. Sparks uses it exclusively.","src":"rec","why":["No tray to hold in the mouth.","Saliva helps it set.","No waiting, unlike gels and foams.","A stronger fluoride, right where you want it.","The four-minute tray is what gels and foams need, and most patients can't tolerate it.","That's gels and foams."]},{"id":"pv-mm-2","lec":"prevention","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: by the 10-calorie rule, which drinks are not a caries risk?","choices":["Coke Zero","Dr Pepper Ten","Crystal Light","Coke","100% apple juice"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Read the label: 10 calories or fewer per serving is not a caries risk. Limit them to 2-3 a day.","src":"rec","why":["0 calories.","10 calories: at the limit.","Artificially sweetened: the treat without the caries.","110 calories of sugar-sweetened beverage.","High in natural sugar: like eating five to ten apples."]},{"id":"pv-mm-3","lec":"prevention","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which match Dr. Mitchell's advice on home fluoride products?","choices":["No fluoride rinses or gels before about age 8","Prevident 5000: brush on at night, spit or wipe out the excess, no rinse","Plain MI Paste for its fluoride","Custom fluoride trays as the usual way to apply it","A product that needs 60 seconds for a patient who tolerates 10"],"correct":[0,1],"ex":"Age 8 or older, brushed on, enough contact time; Prevident 5000 at night without rinsing.","src":"rec","why":["They'd likely swallow it while the teeth are still developing: fluorosis.","Used instead of the regular toothpaste.","Plain MI Paste has no fluoride: use MI Paste Plus.","Trays are rarely tolerated: products are usually brushed on.","Contact time: it does nothing if it can't stay on long enough."]},{"id":"mi-def","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"How does the reading distinguish mental illness from intellectual disability (\"mental retardation\" in the reading)?","choices":["Mental illness is psychiatric or emotional; ID is a cognitive deficit","Mental illness is permanent, while intellectual disability comes and goes","They are the same condition","ID is more common"],"answer":0,"ex":"Mental illness: unable to perform major activities of daily living because of a psychiatric or emotional disorder; ID: a similar dysfunction from a cognitive deficit. ID is permanent; mental illness may be sporadic.","src":"reading","why":["They can coexist.","Reversed.","They differ.","About 17% have a mental disorder vs 3% with ID."]},{"id":"mi-prev","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of the population has a diagnosable mental disorder, according to the reading?","choices":["About 1 in 6 (17%)","About 3%","About 1%","About 50%"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 17% have some diagnosable mental disorder; about 3% have intellectual disability.","src":"reading","why":["From mild neurosis to schizophrenia.","That's intellectual disability.","That's bipolar disorder.","Too high."]},{"id":"mi-dep","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which set of dental findings is typical of major depression?","choices":["Poor hygiene, rampant decay, advanced perio and xerostomia","Cervical abrasion and abraded mucosa from over-vigorous brushing","Gingival hyperplasia","Tardive dyskinesia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Major depression: poor oral hygiene, rampant decay, advanced generalized perio, missing teeth, ill-fitting prostheses, orofacial pain, xerostomia, poor nutrition.","src":"reading","why":["Plus missing teeth, ill-fitting prostheses and facial pain.","That's the manic phase of bipolar disorder.","A Dilantin effect.","An antipsychotic effect."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antidepressants: tricyclics, xerostomia and vasoconstrictors, MAO inhibitors","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-tca","mi-maoi","mi-xero","mi-dep"]}},{"id":"mi-xero","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"In the reading, xerostomia was seen in about what share of patients taking tricyclic antidepressants?","choices":["45%","14%","73%","5%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Xerostomia: 14% on Prozac, 45% on tricyclics. Patients often fight it with sugary drinks, candy and gum; prescribe saliva substitutes.","src":"reading","why":["vs 14% on Prozac.","That's Prozac.","That's lithium in one study.","Too low."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antidepressants: tricyclics, xerostomia and vasoconstrictors, MAO inhibitors","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-tca","mi-maoi","mi-xero","mi-dep"]}},{"id":"mi-tca","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient takes a tricyclic antidepressant. Which vasoconstrictor is contraindicated?","choices":["Levonordefrin (Neo-Cobefrin)","Epinephrine, used with prudence","No vasoconstrictor at all is ever allowed","Felypressin"],"answer":0,"ex":"With tricyclics, epinephrine may be used with prudence, but levonordefrin (Neo-Cobefrin) and norepinephrine (Levophed) are contraindicated.","src":"reading","why":["Also norepinephrine (Levophed).","Allowed with prudence.","Epinephrine may be used carefully.","Not discussed."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antidepressants: tricyclics, xerostomia and vasoconstrictors, MAO inhibitors","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-tca","mi-maoi","mi-xero","mi-dep"]}},{"id":"mi-maoi","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which drug is absolutely contraindicated in a patient taking an MAO inhibitor?","choices":["Meperidine (Demerol)","Ibuprofen","Amoxicillin","Chlorhexidine"],"answer":0,"ex":"The reading: meperidine is absolutely contraindicated with MAO inhibitors, and it lists epinephrine as contraindicated with them as well.","src":"reading","why":["Absolutely contraindicated.","Not the contraindication named.","Not discussed.","Not discussed."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antidepressants: tricyclics, xerostomia and vasoconstrictors, MAO inhibitors","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-tca","mi-maoi","mi-xero","mi-dep"]}},{"id":"mi-ect","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Before a patient's electroconvulsive therapy, what might the dentist be asked to do?","choices":["Make a mouth guard, and address loose teeth, calculus and loose prostheses","Extract all teeth","Place a papoose board","Prescribe an MAO inhibitor beforehand to calm the patient"],"answer":0,"ex":"For ECT: a mouth guard; loose teeth, gross calculus and loose prostheses addressed beforehand to prevent aspiration; existing conditions documented for liability.","src":"reading","why":["Aspiration risk and documentation.","Not indicated.","Not related.","Not the dentist's role."]},{"id":"mi-mania","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"What dental finding is linked to the manic phase of bipolar disorder?","choices":["Abraded mucosa and cervical abrasion from over-vigorous brushing or flossing","Rampant decay from a near-total absence of oral hygiene","Gingival fibromas","Tongue protrusion"],"answer":0,"ex":"Mania can cause abraded oral mucosa and cervical tooth abrasion from over-vigorous brushing or flossing; the depressive phase looks like major depression.","src":"reading","why":["Hyperactivity extends to oral hygiene.","That's the depressive phase.","Tuberous sclerosis.","Tardive dyskinesia."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Bipolar disorder and lithium","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-lith","mi-lithside","mi-mania"]}},{"id":"mi-lith","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Why use NSAIDs carefully in a patient taking lithium?","choices":["NSAIDs can decrease lithium's renal clearance, raising it to toxic levels","NSAIDs combine with lithium to cause gingival hyperplasia","Lithium blocks NSAID absorption","NSAIDs trigger mania"],"answer":0,"ex":"NSAIDs may decrease the renal clearance of lithium and allow toxic levels; short-term use may not be a problem. Benzodiazepines need caution too.","src":"reading","why":["Short-term use may be fine.","Not described.","Reversed concern.","Not described."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Bipolar disorder and lithium","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-lith","mi-lithside","mi-mania"]}},{"id":"mi-lithside","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which oral side effects are linked to lithium?","choices":["Generalized stomatitis and xerostomia","Gingival hyperplasia, as with Dilantin","Black tooth staining","Increased taste sensitivity"],"answer":0,"ex":"People on lithium may complain of generalized stomatitis and xerostomia (73% in one study), though hypersalivation has also been reported.","src":"reading","why":["Xerostomia in 73% in one study.","Not lithium.","Not lithium.","Not described."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Bipolar disorder and lithium","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-lith","mi-lithside","mi-mania"]}},{"id":"mi-para","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"How should you approach a patient with paranoid schizophrenia?","choices":["Slowly and non-threateningly, with no sudden movements, showing each step","Quickly, to finish before they have time to become suspicious","Without explaining, so they won't worry","Only under general anesthesia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Approach verbally and physically very slowly and non-threateningly; warn them of what to expect and show each next step.","src":"reading","why":["They may be very suspicious.","Rushing heightens suspicion.","Warn and show them each step.","Not required."]},{"id":"mi-td","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Tardive dyskinesia is associated with long-term use of which drugs?","choices":["Antipsychotics, especially phenothiazines","SSRIs such as Prozac, taken for many years","Lithium","Benzodiazepines"],"answer":0,"ex":"Long-term antipsychotic therapy can cause tardive dyskinesia: trismus, swallowing problems, tongue protrusion, lip chewing, tongue wiping, smacking. Cogentin and Artane can help.","src":"reading","why":["Constant lip, tongue and jaw movements.","Not described.","Not described.","Not described."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antipsychotics: tardive dyskinesia, EPS, akathisia, sedatives","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-td","mi-eps","mi-akath","mi-schsed"]}},{"id":"mi-eps","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which describes short-term extrapyramidal symptoms from antipsychotics?","choices":["Agitation, neck spasms (torticollis) and oculogyric crises","Xerostomia and caries","Constant lip smacking and tongue wiping after years of use","Weight loss and insomnia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Short-term EPS: generalized agitation or jitteriness, torticollis and oculogyric crises; tardive dyskinesia is the long-term version.","src":"reading","why":["Usually controlled with IM Benadryl.","Side effects, but not EPS.","That's tardive dyskinesia (long-term).","Not EPS."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antipsychotics: tardive dyskinesia, EPS, akathisia, sedatives","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-td","mi-eps","mi-akath","mi-schsed"]}},{"id":"mi-akath","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient on antipsychotics can't sit still and keeps wanting to get up during treatment. What is this?","choices":["Akathisia","Catatonia","Mania","Status epilepticus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Akathisia: restlessness, an inability to sit still, a tendency to move the body and legs, and a desire to get up during the appointment.","src":"reading","why":["Restlessness from the medication.","Reduced movement, not restlessness.","A mood state, not a drug effect here.","A prolonged seizure."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antipsychotics: tardive dyskinesia, EPS, akathisia, sedatives","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-td","mi-eps","mi-akath","mi-schsed"]}},{"id":"mi-schsed","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the concern with dental sedatives in a patient on neuroleptics?","choices":["A synergistic reaction causing excessive respiratory depression","Sedatives have no effect","A hypertensive crisis if any epinephrine is used with the sedative","Gingival bleeding"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sedatives can combine with neuroleptics to cause excessive respiratory depression. Local anesthetic with epinephrine causes no adverse effects in normotensive patients.","src":"reading","why":["Use with caution.","Not the concern.","Epinephrine is fine if normotensive.","Not the concern."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Antipsychotics: tardive dyskinesia, EPS, akathisia, sedatives","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-td","mi-eps","mi-akath","mi-schsed"]}},{"id":"mi-dem","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Why should dental treatment be completed as early as possible in progressive dementia?","choices":["Cooperation declines as the dementia progresses","Teeth decay faster in dementia's early stages","Insurance ends at diagnosis","Anesthetics stop working later"],"answer":0,"ex":"In progressive dementia (e.g., Alzheimer's), the ability to cooperate declines, so finish treatment early and take full-mouth radiographs for future reference.","src":"reading","why":["Take full-mouth radiographs early too.","Not the reason.","Not the reason.","Not the reason."]},{"id":"mi-elderly","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the reading's rule of thumb for drugs in the elderly?","choices":["A single dose gives about twice the peak level and twice the half-life","Doses should be doubled because older patients absorb less of each drug","Half-lives are shorter","No change is needed"],"answer":0,"ex":"In the elderly, a single dose of most drugs produces a peak blood level twice as high and a half-life twice as long as in a younger patient.","src":"reading","why":["Liver and kidney function decline.","The reverse.","They're longer.","Prescribe with caution."]},{"id":"mi-demcomm","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which communication technique does the reading recommend for patients with dementia?","choices":["Use nouns rather than pronouns, with short words and sentences","Use abstract terms and pronouns to keep the conversation natural","Avoid eye contact and touch","Give all instructions once at the start"],"answer":0,"ex":"Reassurance, nonverbal communication (smiling, eye contact, gentle touch), repetition, no abstract terms, nouns rather than pronouns, short words and sentences.","src":"reading","why":["As with ID.","Avoid abstract terms.","Nonverbal communication is encouraged.","Repeat instructions."]},{"id":"mi-alcbleed","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Before oral surgery on a patient with long-term alcoholism, what is often indicated?","choices":["A bleeding profile: CBC, PT, PTT and bleeding time","An EKG and blood pressure check, with no lab work","Nothing different","A thyroid panel"],"answer":0,"ex":"Liver damage brings a bleeding tendency and impaired healing; a bleeding profile is often indicated before surgery.","src":"reading","why":["Liver damage raises bleeding risk.","Not what the reading said.","Bleeding risk is higher.","Not indicated by the reading."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Substance use: alcohol and bleeding, cocaine and epinephrine, Antabuse","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-alcbleed","mi-coke","mi-antabuse","mi-recov"]}},{"id":"mi-alcsed","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"How might a patient with alcoholism respond to sedative drugs?","choices":["They may need higher doses, from cross-tolerance","They need much lower doses because the liver is damaged","Sedatives are always contraindicated","Normally"],"answer":0,"ex":"As they develop tolerance to ethanol, they develop tolerance to sedatives and often need higher doses. Drugs metabolized by the liver need caution.","src":"reading","why":["Tolerance to ethanol brings tolerance to sedatives.","The reverse.","Not stated.","An altered response is expected."]},{"id":"mi-coke","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient used cocaine yesterday. What does the reading say about local anesthetic with epinephrine?","choices":["It is strongly contraindicated within 48 hours of cocaine use","It is preferred, because it reduces bleeding and the anesthetic dose","It's fine at any time","Only felypressin is allowed"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cocaine causes cardiac irritability and hypotension; epinephrine is strongly contraindicated with anyone who has used cocaine in the last 48 hours.","src":"reading","why":["Cardiac irritability.","Contraindicated.","Not within 48 hours.","Not discussed."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Substance use: alcohol and bleeding, cocaine and epinephrine, Antabuse","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-alcbleed","mi-coke","mi-antabuse","mi-recov"]}},{"id":"mi-recov","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"For a recovering addict needing post-op pain control, which approach matches the reading?","choices":["NSAIDs first and a long-acting local such as bupivacaine; avoid mood-altering drugs","Nitrous oxide for comfort","A week of opioids to be safe, since recovering patients feel pain more","Benzodiazepines for anxiety"],"answer":0,"ex":"Avoid mood-altering drugs (including nitrous oxide); prefer NSAIDs; if narcotics are needed after major surgery, prescribe only after NSAIDs fail, about 48 hours' worth, dispensed by someone else if possible.","src":"reading","why":["Narcotics only if NSAIDs fail, ~48 hours' worth.","Avoid it: mood-altering.","Never for anticipated pain; limit to about 48 hours.","Mood-altering."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Substance use: alcohol and bleeding, cocaine and epinephrine, Antabuse","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-alcbleed","mi-coke","mi-antabuse","mi-recov"]}},{"id":"mi-antabuse","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"A recovering alcoholic takes Antabuse (disulfiram). What should they avoid?","choices":["Alcohol-containing mouthwashes and products","Fluoride toothpaste and high-fluoride gels","Ibuprofen","Chewing gum"],"answer":0,"ex":"Patients on Antabuse should avoid all oral or topical alcohol-based products such as mouthwashes; alcohol-free mouthwashes exist.","src":"reading","why":["Use alcohol-free rinses.","No interaction described.","Not the issue.","Not the issue."],"likely":{"lec":"mental","t":"Substance use: alcohol and bleeding, cocaine and epinephrine, Antabuse","basis":"reading","ids":["mi-alcbleed","mi-coke","mi-antabuse","mi-recov"]}},{"id":"mi-alcoral","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is a dental implication of alcoholism listed in the reading?","choices":["Higher incidence of oral cancer, with heavy smoking","Lower caries rates, because alcohol kills oral bacteria","Gingival hyperplasia","Macroglossia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Alcoholism: poor hygiene, neglect, attrition (bruxism), xerostomia, oral cancer, candidiasis, impaired healing, trauma and bleeding.","src":"reading","why":["Screen carefully at the first exam.","Not listed.","Not listed.","Not listed."]},{"id":"mi-cand","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"About what share of patients with dementia on antipsychotics have candidiasis?","choices":["5-20%","About 73%","About 1%","About 45%"],"answer":0,"ex":"Candidiasis is experienced by 5-20% of patients on antipsychotics; many can't rinse for 30 seconds and swallow everything, so tailor the treatment.","src":"reading","why":["Chlorhexidine and denture scrubs help.","That's xerostomia on lithium.","Too low.","That's xerostomia on tricyclics."]},{"id":"mi-tf-1","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Mental illness may be sporadic, while intellectual disability is permanent.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Many people have depression once or twice in life and function well otherwise.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"mi-tf-2","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Epinephrine can be used with prudence in patients taking tricyclic antidepressants.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Levonordefrin and norepinephrine are the contraindicated ones.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"mi-tf-3","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Tardive dyskinesia is a short-term side effect that goes away within days.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It's a long-term effect of antipsychotics; short-term effects are extrapyramidal symptoms.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["No: long-term.","Correct."]},{"id":"mi-tf-4","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Patients in recovery from addiction should avoid nitrous oxide.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"It's mood-altering, like sedatives and most analgesics other than NSAIDs.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"mi-tf-5","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Dr. Mitchell considers himself an expert in mental health dentistry.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"He said it isn't his area of expertise, so he posted the reading instead of lecturing.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["No.","Correct."]},{"id":"mi-tf-6","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In the elderly, a single dose of most drugs reaches about twice the peak blood level.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"And has about twice the half-life.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"mi-tf-7","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Patients with schizophrenia often neglect or refuse dental care.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Confusion, depression, withdrawal and anxiety, plus antipsychotic xerostomia, raise decay and perio.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["True.","It's true."]},{"id":"mi-tf-8","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Local anesthetic with epinephrine is safe within 48 hours of cocaine use.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It's strongly contraindicated.","src":"reading","fmt":"tf","why":["No.","Correct."]},{"id":"mi-2s-1","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Xerostomia occurs in about 14% of patients taking Prozac.\n2. Xerostomia occurs in about 45% of patients taking tricyclic antidepressants.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true.","src":"reading","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: Prozac: 14%. Statement 2 is true: tricyclics: 45%.","Statement 1 is true, not false: Prozac: 14%. Statement 2 is true, not false: tricyclics: 45%.","Statement 2 is true, not false: tricyclics: 45%.","Statement 1 is true, not false: Prozac: 14%."]},{"id":"mi-2s-2","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. In untreated bipolar disorder, manic episodes outnumber depressive ones.\n2. Bipolar disorder affects about 1% of the population.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"About 1%; untreated persons have more depressive than manic episodes.","src":"reading","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: depressive episodes are more numerous.","Statement 2 is true, not false: with a strong familial pattern.","Statement 1 is false, not true: depressive episodes are more numerous. Statement 2 is true, not false: with a strong familial pattern.","Statement 1 is false: depressive episodes are more numerous. Statement 2 is true: with a strong familial pattern."]},{"id":"mi-2s-3","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Dementia affects over 20% of people 80 and older.\n2. Dementia is uncommon after age 65.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"2-3% at 65-79, over 20% at 80+; people 65 and older are most susceptible.","src":"reading","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: people over 65 are the most susceptible.","Statement 1 is true, not false: over 20% at 80 and older.","Statement 1 is true: over 20% at 80 and older. Statement 2 is false: people over 65 are the most susceptible.","Statement 1 is true, not false: over 20% at 80 and older. Statement 2 is false, not true: people over 65 are the most susceptible."]},{"id":"mi-2s-4","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Akathisia makes patients want to get up and move during the appointment.\n2. Short-term extrapyramidal symptoms are usually controlled with IM Benadryl.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are true.","src":"reading","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: restlessness from antipsychotics. Statement 2 is true: IM Benadryl is the usual control.","Statement 1 is true, not false: restlessness from antipsychotics. Statement 2 is true, not false: IM Benadryl is the usual control.","Statement 2 is true, not false: IM Benadryl is the usual control.","Statement 1 is true, not false: restlessness from antipsychotics."]},{"id":"mi-2s-5","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. An ester local anesthetic has been recommended for patients with alcoholic cirrhosis.\n2. Patients with long histories of alcoholism usually need less local anesthetic.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Esters have been recommended in cirrhosis; long-time alcoholics may need significantly more anesthetic.","src":"reading","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: they may need significantly more.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the reading names an ester for cirrhosis.","Statement 1 is true: the reading names an ester for cirrhosis. Statement 2 is false: they may need significantly more.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the reading names an ester for cirrhosis. Statement 2 is false, not true: they may need significantly more."]},{"id":"mi-num-1","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are dental manifestations of major depression in the reading?\n1. Cervical abrasion from over-brushing\n2. Rampant dental decay\n3. Ill-fitting dental prostheses\n4. Xerostomia","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Over-brushing abrasion belongs to the manic phase of bipolar disorder.","src":"reading","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: that's mania. It leaves out 4, which is true: from the illness and medications.","It includes 1, which is false: that's mania. It leaves out 3, which is true: on the list.","It leaves out 4, which is true: from the illness and medications.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: that's mania."]},{"id":"mi-num-2","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are dental implications of alcoholism?\n1. Bleeding tendency\n2. Impaired wound healing\n3. Decreased need for local anesthetic\n4. Higher oral cancer risk","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"Long-time alcoholics may need more anesthetic, not less.","src":"reading","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: with heavy smoking.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: they may need significantly more.","It includes 3, which is false: they may need significantly more. It leaves out 2, which is true: liver damage.","It includes 3, which is false: they may need significantly more. It leaves out 1, which is true: liver damage."]},{"id":"mi-num-3","lec":"mental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about lithium are in the reading?\n1. It works in about 70% of patients within 3 weeks\n2. NSAIDs can raise its levels\n3. Xerostomia was reported in 73% in one study\n4. Benzodiazepines can be used without caution","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":0,"ex":"Benzodiazepines need caution because of CNS depression.","src":"reading","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: use them with caution.","It includes 4, which is false: use them with caution.","It leaves out 1, which is true: effective in 70% within 3 weeks.","It includes 4, which is false: use them with caution. It leaves out 1, which is true: effective in 70% within 3 weeks."]},{"id":"mi-match","lec":"mental","type":"match","stem":"Match each patient's medication or history to the caution.","pairs":[["MAO inhibitor","No meperidine (Demerol)"],["Lithium","Watch NSAIDs: reduced clearance"],["Cocaine in the last 48 hours","No epinephrine"],["Antabuse","No alcohol-based mouthwash"]],"ex":"Drug cautions from the reading.","src":"reading"},{"id":"mi-match2","lec":"mental","type":"match","stem":"Match each disorder with a typical dental finding.","pairs":[["Bipolar mania","Cervical abrasion from over-brushing"],["Dementia","Maxillofacial injuries from falls"],["Schizophrenia on antipsychotics","Tardive dyskinesia"],["Alcoholism","Bleeding tendency"]],"ex":"Dental implications by disorder.","src":"reading"},{"id":"mi-mm-1","lec":"mental","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which does the reading call contraindicated?","choices":["Levonordefrin (Neo-Cobefrin) with a tricyclic antidepressant","Meperidine (Demerol) with an MAO inhibitor","Epinephrine within 48 hours of cocaine use","Alcohol-based mouthwash for a patient on disulfiram (Antabuse)","Epinephrine for a normotensive patient on antipsychotics","Epinephrine, used with prudence, with a tricyclic"],"correct":[0,1,2,3],"ex":"Tricyclics: no levonordefrin or norepinephrine. MAOIs: no meperidine (and epinephrine, per the reading). Cocaine: no epinephrine for 48 hours. Antabuse: no alcohol.","src":"reading","why":["Levonordefrin and norepinephrine are contraindicated with tricyclics.","Absolutely contraindicated.","Cocaine causes cardiac irritability.","They must avoid alcohol-containing products.","Local anesthetic with epinephrine is fine if they are normotensive.","Epinephrine may be used with prudence; it's levonordefrin and norepinephrine that are out."]},{"id":"mi-mm-2","lec":"mental","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which are effects of antipsychotic (neuroleptic) drugs in the reading?","choices":["Tardive dyskinesia: tongue protrusion, constant lip and jaw movements","Extrapyramidal symptoms such as neck spasms (torticollis)","Akathisia: restlessness, wanting to get up during the appointment","Gingival hyperplasia","Cervical abrasion from over-vigorous brushing"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Antipsychotics: extrapyramidal symptoms, tardive dyskinesia, akathisia, xerostomia; sedatives can deepen respiratory depression.","src":"reading","why":["The long-term effect.","Short-term; treated with IM Benadryl.","Can't sit still.","That's Dilantin (phenytoin), from the CP lecture.","That's the manic phase of bipolar disorder."]},{"id":"mi-mm-3","lec":"mental","type":"multi","stem":"Select all that apply: which fit the reading's advice for a patient with progressive dementia?","choices":["Complete dental treatment as early as possible","Take full-mouth radiographs early","Use nouns instead of pronouns, with short words and sentences","Prescribe the usual adult dose","Rely on 30-second chlorhexidine rinses"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Treat early, radiograph early, consult the team, prescribe cautiously, communicate simply.","src":"reading","why":["Cooperation declines as the disease progresses.","For future reference.","The same communication techniques used for ID.","In the elderly a single dose gives about twice the peak level and twice the half-life: prescribe cautiously.","Many can't rinse for 30 seconds and swallow everything."]}]});
